          "text": "Let me analyze the current Makefile and answer her questions.\n\nHer 3 questions:\n1. On Linux there won't be \"special\" object files for the DLL, because there's never any flag in the headers (COMMON_API is empty on Linux). On Windows there'll be `geometry_cpp_dll.obj` and `geometry_cpp.obj` because functions are marked differently. So she wrapped the dynamic objects block in `ifeq ($(OS), Windows_NT)`.\n\nLet me verify. On Linux, the shared library is built from the SAME `.o` files as the static ones? Let me check.\n\nIn the Makefile:\n- STATIC objects: `$(BINDIR)/common/geometry_cpp$(OBJ_EXT)` built with `$(DEFINE)COMMON_STATIC` → `-DCOMMON_STATIC`\n- DYNAMIC objects (Windows only): `$(BINDIR)/common/geometry_cpp_dll$(OBJ_EXT)` built with `$(DEFINE)COMMON_EXPORTS` → `/DCOMMON_EXPORTS`\n\nOn Linux else-branch (lines 320-331):\n```\nCPP_OBJ := $(BINDIR)/common/geometry_cpp.o $(BINDIR)/common/string_utils_cpp.o\nC_OBJ := $(BINDIR)/common/geometry_c.o $(BINDIR)/common/string_utils_c.o\n\n$(BINDIR)/common/common_cpp.so: $(CPP_OBJ) | $(BINDIR)/common\n\t$(LINK) $(LINK_FLAGS) $(DLL) $(LINK_OUT)$(BINDIR)/common/common_cpp.so $^\n```\n\nSo on Linux, the shared library is built from `geometry_cpp.o` and `string_utils_cpp.o` — the SAME objects that are built with `-DCOMMON_STATIC` (used by t1-t2 static linking). \n\nWait, but there's a problem! On Linux, `geometry_cpp.o` is built via the STATIC rule with `-DCOMMON_STATIC`. But the pattern rule also builds `$(BINDIR)/common/geometry_cpp.o`. And the static rule explicitly builds `$(BINDIR)/common/geometry_cpp$(OBJ_EXT)` with `-DCOMMON_STATIC`. So the same object `geometry_cpp.o` is used for BOTH the static t1-t2 link AND for the shared library. That's fine on Linux because COMMON_API is empty there — no dllexport needed, and .so exports symbols by default.\n\nSo her statement #1: correct. On Linux, no separate dll objects are needed because there's no dllexport/dllimport distinction — symbols are exported from .so by default. Correct.\n\nBut wait — is there a concern? On Linux, shared libraries need position-independent code (`-fPIC`). She hasn't added `-fPIC` to the Linux build! Let me check. In the Linux flags section:\n\n```\nCONFIG_FLAGS := -O0 -g -fsanitize=address\n```\n\nNo `-fPIC`. And for release:\n```\nCONFIG_FLAGS := -O2 -g -DNDEBUG\n```\n\nNo `-fPIC`. So the objects `geometry_cpp.o` compiled without `-fPIC` will be linked into `.so` via `g++ -shared`. \n\nThis is the -fPIC issue I raised earlier. On x86_64 with GCC, linking a .so from non-PIC objects will typically fail with errors like \"relocation R_X86_64_32S against `.text' can not be used when making a shared object; recompile with -fPIC\". Actually, for code that only has simple functions (no global data references, no function pointers taking addresses), GCC might produce position-independent text by default on x86-64? No — on x86-64, the default is that code is not PIC. Actually, GCC on x86-64: text relocations are only generated if the code needs them. Simple code often links fine without -fPIC on x86-64. But it's not guaranteed, and it's a real concern. Actually the common experience: on x86-64, `g++ -shared` from non-PIC objects often DOES work because x86-64 RIP-relative addressing is the default for most things. The classic failure is on 32-bit x86. But there are cases (e.g., taking address of functions, global arrays accessed via absolute addressing) that fail.\n\nSo I flagged earlier that -fPIC is needed. She hasn't added it. Since the same .o files are used for static AND shared on Linux, adding -fPIC globally would make all objects PIC (slightly slower but fine for static). Alternative: separate PIC objects for the .so. But her design reuses the same objects — simpler. For correctness on Linux she should add -fPIC at least to the objects that go into .so. Since they're shared with static use, simplest is to add -fPIC to all Linux objects. Actually wait — on x86-64, GCC... let me think carefully. I want to be accurate. This is [ФАКТ, ВЫСОКИЙ] that -fPIC is the standard requirement for shared libraries on Linux; without it the link may fail. On x86-64 it often works but it's not guaranteed and it's UB-ish practice. I should flag it.\n\nAlso there's another subtle problem: on Linux the .so is built from `geometry_cpp.o` which is ALSO a prerequisite of t1 (static). That means t1's link and the .so share the same object file. Fine. But there's a deeper concern: the `CPP_OBJ` on Linux uses `geometry_cpp.o` — these are built by the STATIC rules (with `-DCOMMON_STATIC`). Good — no separate compile. And the pattern rule `$(BINDIR)/%$(OBJ_EXT): %.c` would also match `$(BINDIR)/common/geometry_cpp.o`? Wait, there are two rules that could match `build/debug/common/geometry_cpp.o`:\n- Pattern rule: `$(BINDIR)/%$(OBJ_EXT): %.cpp` — matches `build/debug/common/geometry_cpp.o` with stem `common/geometry_cpp`, prerequisite `common/geometry_cpp.cpp`. \n- Explicit rule: `$(BINDIR)/common/geometry_cpp$(OBJ_EXT): common/geometry.cpp common/geometry.hpp | $(BINDIR)/common`.\n\nThe explicit rule wins over pattern rule. Good.\n\nBut wait — the pattern rule compiles with `$(COMMON_FLAG)` which is target-specific and empty by default; the explicit static rule uses `$(DEFINE)COMMON_STATIC`. So the same object `geometry_cpp.o` is built with COMMON_STATIC define. On Linux COMMON_STATIC isn't needed (headers return empty anyway), so fine.\n\nNow, one issue: on Linux, t1 links statically using `geometry_cpp.o` (with COMMON_STATIC define - harmless on Linux), and the .so ALSO links against `geometry_cpp.o`. So the .so is made from objects compiled with `-DCOMMON_STATIC`. Since on Linux COMMON_API is empty regardless, this is fine.\n\nBut hold on — there's an actual problem with ASan and shared libraries! With `-fsanitize=address`, when the .so is loaded, ASan must be initialized before the .so's constructors. If the .so is built with ASan and loaded into an ASan-instrumented exe, it works if ASan runtime is loaded first. The exe links with `-fsanitize=address`, so the runtime is loaded first. Should be OK.\n\nNow her question 1: \"под линуксом не будет 'особых' объектников? т.к. никогда нет флага никакого в заголовочниках\" — Correct. On Linux, no dllexport/dllimport; the same objects work for both static and shared. The ifeq wrapping of dynamic object rules is correct.\n\nBut wait, there's a subtlety on Windows too: the dynamic objects use `$(OBJ_EXT)` in the filename `geometry_cpp_dll$(OBJ_EXT)` but in the CPP_DLL_OBJ (line 309) she hardcoded `.obj`:\n```\nCPP_DLL_OBJ := $(BINDIR)/common/geometry_cpp_dll.obj $(BINDIR)/common/string_utils_cpp_dll.obj\nC_DLL_OBJ := $(BINDIR)/common/geometry_c_dll.obj $(BINDIR)/common/string_utils_c_dll.obj\n```\nThis is inside the Windows ifeq (line 308), so `.obj` is correct there. It would be cleaner to use $(OBJ_EXT) but it's inside Windows branch only, and OBJ_EXT is .obj on Windows. Minor style issue — but could be flagged for consistency. Actually it's correct, just not using the variable. Since it's inside Windows-only ifeq, it's fine. Could suggest using $(OBJ_EXT) for consistency.\n\nHer question 2: \"блок на строках 135-141 (дать имя dll / so) кажется мне бесполезным. это так или он нужен там, где я не заметила?\"\n\nLines 135-141:\n```\nifeq ($(OS), Windows_NT)\n  CPP_SHARED := $(BINDIR)/common/common_cpp.dll\n  C_SHARED := $(BINDIR)/common/common_c.dll\nelse\n  CPP_SHARED := $(BINDIR)/common/libcommon_cpp.so\n  C_SHARED := $(BINDIR)/common/libcommon_c.so\nendif\n```\n\nAre CPP_SHARED / C_SHARED used anywhere? Let me search. I need to grep for CPP_SHARED and C_SHARED usage.\n\nLet me grep the Makefile.",
