          "text": "Now let me analyze the questions she asked and the state.\n\nHer questions:\n1. On Linux there will be no \"special\" objects for DLL — because there's never a flag in headers (COMMON_API is empty on Linux). On Windows there will be geometry_cpp_dll.obj and geometry_cpp.obj because functions are marked differently. So she wrapped the DYNAMIC object rules in `ifeq ($(OS), Windows_NT)`.\n2. The block at lines 135-141 (giving a name to dll/so) — CPP_SHARED / C_SHARED — seems useless to her. Is it needed somewhere she didn't notice?\n3. On Linux linking happens with the .so itself, so does SET_DLL_PATH need to be in the link command so the .so is found?\n\nShe also says she's done points 1-8 from the final plan, build and tests pass (see artifacts).\n\nLet me verify:\n\nPoint 1: She wrapped dynamic objects in ifeq Windows_NT. Let me check the current Makefile state. Lines 278-290: yes, the DYNAMIC section is wrapped in `ifeq ($(OS), Windows_NT)`. \n\nBut wait — on Linux, the shared library is built from the SAME `.o` files as the static ones (`CPP_OBJ := $(BINDIR)/common/geometry_cpp.o $(BINDIR)/common/string_utils_cpp.o`). Those `.o` files are built with `$(DEFINE)COMMON_STATIC` in the STATIC section (lines 264-274)! \n\nWait, let me check. The STATIC rules (lines 264-274):\n```\n$(BINDIR)/common/geometry_cpp$(OBJ_EXT): common/geometry.cpp common/geometry.hpp | $(BINDIR)/common\n\t$(CXX) $(COMPILE_ONLY) $(DEFINE)COMMON_STATIC $(OBJ_OUT)$@ $(CXXFLAGS) $< \n```\n\nSo `geometry_cpp.o` is built with `-DCOMMON_STATIC`. On Linux COMMON_API is empty regardless, so it doesn't matter. But the .so is built from objects compiled with `-DCOMMON_STATIC`. That's fine because on Linux COMMON_API is empty.\n\nBUT — there's a critical issue on Linux: **`-fPIC`**. Shared libraries on Linux require position-independent code. Objects compiled without `-fPIC` cannot be linked into a `.so` — the linker will fail with \"relocation R_X86_64_32S against `.text' can not be used when making a shared object; recompile with -fPIC\" or similar.\n\nThis is a real problem! On Linux, the objects that go into the .so (geometry_cpp.o, string_utils_cpp.o) are the same objects used for static linking t1-t2. They are compiled with the same CXXFLAGS which do NOT include -fPIC. So linking the .so will fail on Linux.\n\nSo her question 1 reasoning is correct (no \"special\" objects needed on Linux because COMMON_API is empty), BUT there's a subtlety: on Linux, to build a .so, objects need -fPIC. Whether she needs separate objects depends on whether the same objects are PIC. Options:\n- Compile all objects with -fPIC (harmless for static too, just slightly larger code). Simplest.\n- Have separate PIC objects for the .so.\n\nI flagged -fPIC earlier in the plan (point 6 in my plan: \"Плюс `PIC_FLAGS` (`-fPIC` на Linux) только для dll-объектников — без него линковка .so упадёт на relocations.\"). But in her final Makefile there's no PIC_FLAGS and no -fPIC anywhere. And she removed the separate dll objects on Linux, reusing static objects. So the .so build will fail on Linux (unless... on x86-64, gcc without -fPIC produces code that often CAN be linked into a shared lib if it doesn't reference global symbols absolutely — actually no, x86-64 by default uses RIP-relative addressing for many things, so simple functions often work without -fPIC on x86-64. Hmm, this is actually a nuanced point).\n\nLet me think carefully about -fPIC on x86-64:\n- On x86-64 (System V AMD64 ABI), the default code model (`-mcmodel=small`) generates RIP-relative addressing for most accesses. Global variable access uses RIP-relative addressing which IS position-independent. So many simple C/C++ functions compile to position-independent code even without -fPIC on x86-64.\n- The problem arises with: taking addresses of functions stored in static/global data (function pointers in initialized globals), global variables that are defined in the same TU and accessed via GOT... actually, for `-mcmodel=small`, accessing a global variable uses RIP-relative addressing, which works in a shared library. BUT if a global symbol is *defined* in the same file and the address is taken into a static initializer, the linker needs a GOT-relative relocation.\n\nActually, the well-known failure on x86-64 without -fPIC: `relocation R_X86_64_32S against symbol ... can not be used when making a shared object`. This happens when an absolute 32-bit relocation is emitted. For `-mcmodel=small`, if you reference a global symbol that the compiler can't know is local, it uses a 32-bit absolute... no wait. \n\nLet me be careful. In practice: on x86-64, linking a .so from objects compiled WITHOUT -fPIC often WORKS for simple code (functions only, no global data, no function pointers in data). This is because x86-64 code is mostly position-independent by default. The classic failures require globals. geometry.cpp / string_utils.cpp are simple — parse_point, trim_str, is_empty, get_string. No global variables, no function pointers in initialized data. So it would likely link fine on x86-64.\n\nBUT it's still considered bad practice, and on 32-bit x86 it would definitely fail. Also, `-mcmodel=small` default actually does generate GOT-relative? No.\n\nHmm, I need to be accurate here. Let me recall: GCC on x86-64 without -fPIC, default code model small:\n- Reference to a global function: RIP-relative call → fine in shared library.\n- Reference to global data: RIP-relative → fine.\n- Taking address of a global and storing it in initialized data: needs a relocation that is absolute → fails in shared object.\n\nSo for these simple libraries it would likely work. However, the mentor's rule and best practice is -fPIC for shared libraries. Since she doesn't have separate PIC objects, the cleanest fix is to add -fPIC to the objects that go into the .so. But those are the same objects as static ones. So either:\n(a) add -fPIC to all Linux objects (simple, acceptable), or\n(b) create separate objects with -fPIC for the .so.\n\nActually — wait. This connects to her question 1! She says \"on Linux there will be no special objects\". That's TRUE for the *export/import marking* reason (COMMON_API is empty). But it may be FALSE for the *PIC* reason. So the answer to her question 1 is nuanced: yes, you don't need separate objects for the __declspec reason, but you may need -fPIC for the objects that go into the .so, which might require either -fPIC on all objects or separate ones.\n\nGiven the simplicity of these functions, it might work without -fPIC on x86-64. But it's fragile and non-standard. I should flag this as [ПРЕДПОЛОЖЕНИЕ/ФАКТ]. Actually the rule is a FACT: shared objects on Linux should be compiled with -fPIC; it may happen to work without on x86-64 for simple code but is not guaranteed and is not portable. I'll mark as [ФАКТ, ВЫСОКИЙ] that the standard requirement is -fPIC, with [МНЕНИЕ, СРЕДНИЙ] on whether it happens to work here.\n\nNow her question 2: Lines 135-141 — CPP_SHARED / C_SHARED variables. Are they used anywhere? Let me check the current Makefile. In the current version:\n- Line 135-141: `CPP_SHARED := ...dll / libcommon_cpp.so`, `C_SHARED := ...`\n- Are CPP_SHARED / C_SHARED referenced anywhere else? From the grep earlier: lines 136,137,139,140 only. And SHARED_CPP_ELEMENT / SHARED_C_ELEMENT are separate variables defined at lines 336-341 and used at 350-360.\n\nSo CPP_SHARED and C_SHARED are indeed DEAD variables — defined but never used! Her instinct is correct. Let me verify by grep specifically in the Makefile.\n\nActually, I should double check. The grep showed:\n- 136: CPP_SHARED :=\n- 137: C_SHARED :=\n- 139: CPP_SHARED :=\n- 140: C_SHARED :=\nNo other uses. And SHARED_CPP_ELEMENT is the one actually used (336-341 define, 350/353/356/359 use). So CPP_SHARED/C_SHARED are dead code — defined but never referenced. Per § 4.1 p.9 / self-review, they should be removed. Her question answer: yes, they're dead. Either remove them, or