ich functions are in the DLL. No need to find the .dll itself at link time (the .lib has the info). So PATH is not needed at link time.\n- Run time: the loader must find the .dll → PATH.\n\nOn Linux:\n- Link time: the linker (g++/ld) needs to find the .so file directly (there's no separate import library). The linker records the SONAME and a NEEDED entry. If you pass the full path to the .so, the linker uses it directly — no need for LD_LIBRARY_PATH at link time IF you give the full path. LD_LIBRARY_PATH is used at link time only when you use `-lfoo` without a full path and need search directories. Since her Makefile passes the full path `$(SHARED_CPP_ELEMENT)` = `build/debug/common/libcommon_cpp.so`, the linker finds it directly. So NO, SET_DLL_PATH is NOT needed in the link command.\n- Run time: the dynamic loader must find the .so. The linker records DT_NEEDED = soname (e.g., `libcommon_cpp.so`). The loader searches: RPATH/RUNPATH embedded in the binary, LD_LIBRARY_PATH, cache, default paths. Since the .so is not in default paths, LD_LIBRARY_PATH (SET_DLL_PATH) IS needed at run time.\n\nSo the answer: SET_DLL_PATH belongs in the RUN commands (tests), NOT in the link command. The link command needs the full path (which it already has via SHARED_CPP_ELEMENT). At link time, since the full path is given, no LD_LIBRARY_PATH needed.\n\nBUT there's a nuance: actually, when linking, g++ with a full path to the .so — it works. So she's right to ask; the answer is: no, SET_DLL_PATH is not needed in the link command; it's needed only at run time (tests). And her current Makefile puts SET_DLL_PATH in the link commands for t3/t4 (lines 351, 354, 357, 360: `$(SET_DLL_PATH) && $(LINK)...`). Wait, let me check: line 351: `$(SET_DLL_PATH) && $(LINK) $(LINK_FLAGS) $(LINK_OUT)$@ $^`. So she put SET_DLL_PATH in the LINK commands for t3/t4. On Windows that's `set \"PATH=...;%PATH%\"` — harmless but useless at link time (link doesn't need the DLL, it needs the .lib). On Linux `export LD_LIBRARY_PATH=...` — harmless but also useless at link time since the full path is passed.\n\nActually wait — is it truly useless on Linux? When linking with `g++ -o main main.o build/debug/common/libcommon_cpp.so`, the linker processes the .so directly (full path). No LD_LIBRARY_PATH needed. Correct.\n\nSo: SET_DLL_PATH in the link recipe is harmless but unnecessary — it's needed only in the test (run) recipes. Actually, removing it from link would be cleaner. But it's not harmful. Hmm, but it does confuse. I'll explain the distinction.\n\nActually, wait — on Windows, is SET_DLL_PATH needed at link? No. On Windows link.exe uses the .lib import library, passed as full path. Fine.\n\nSo the answer to Q3: SET_DLL_PATH is a runtime concern. It belongs in test commands (where it already is). It's not needed in the link commands. It's currently present in link commands too — harmless but misleading. Recommendation: remove from link commands, keep in test commands.\n\nNow, let me also verify: are the tests passing as she says? She says \"сборка и тесты прошли см. артефакты\". On Windows, the tests pass. On Linux we can't verify (no VM here). So the build/tests passing is for the Windows side. The Linux side has bugs (lib prefix mismatch, -fPIC question).\n\nLet me also double-check the `%.o` pattern rule on Linux. Pattern rule: `$(BINDIR)/%$(OBJ_EXT): %.cpp`. On Linux OBJ_EXT := .o. So `build/debug/t3_bbox_cpp/main.o: t3_bbox_cpp/main.cpp`. Good.\n\nNow the STATIC section rules (264-274) use `$(DEFINE)COMMON_STATIC`. On Linux, COMMON_STATIC define is irrelevant (COMMON_API empty), so it's harmless.\n\nThe .so on Linux uses `CPP_OBJ := geometry_cpp.o string_utils_cpp.o`. These are built by the STATIC rules (with -DCOMMON_STATIC). Fine on Linux.\n\nBut wait — there's another subtlety with the pattern rule matching! On Linux, the STATIC explicit rules (264-274) build `geometry_cpp.o` etc. AND the .so depends on those same .o files. But ALSO the pattern rule `$(BINDIR)/%$(OBJ_EXT): %.cpp` could match `geometry_cpp.o` — but the explicit rule takes precedence over the pattern rule (explicit rules beat pattern rules). So geometry_cpp.o is built by the explicit static rule. Good, no conflict.\n\nNow let me also verify the run_case.cpp exit code issue (point 8 of the plan). She said she did points 1-8. But run_case.cpp has NO #ifdef _WIN32 handling of the exit code! Line 21: `int real_code = std::system(argv[1]);` and compares `real_code != expected_code`. On POSIX, std::system returns the wait status (real code << 8), so this will fail on Linux. She did NOT fix run_case.cpp. Let me re-read... No, run_case.cpp is unchanged — no #ifdef. So point 8 was NOT done.\n\nWait, she said \"по идее я сделала пункты 1-8 из итогового плана правок\". Let me map:\n1. Убрать /EHsc из Linux CXXFLAGS — DONE (line 99: no /EHsc in Linux branch). ✓\n2. Заполнить Linux-флаги — DONE (lines 83-97). ✓\n3. COMMON_API в 4 заголовках — DONE (all four headers have #ifdef _WIN32). ✓\n4. Переменные расширений EXE_EXT/OBJ_EXT — DONE (lines 35-36, 81-82). ✓\n5. Имена общих библиотек CPP_SHARED/C_SHARED — DONE (lines 135-141). ✓\n6. Правило сборки библиотеки: Windows grouped target, Linux single rule for .so — DONE (lines 308-331). ✓ (but with naming bug + no -fPIC)\n7. Пререквизиты t3/t4 через переменную — DONE (SHARED_CPP_ELEMENT, lines 335-341, used 350-360). ✓\n8. run_case.cpp exit-code #ifdef _WIN32 — NOT DONE. run_case.cpp unchanged.\n\nSo point 8 is missing. That's important — all exit-code tests would fail on Linux.\n\nLet me double check run_case.cpp more carefully. Yes, no #ifdef. So on Linux `std::system` returns wait status. The comparison fails. Point 8 NOT done.\n\nAlso she asked in her message \"3. линковка в линуксе происходит с самой .so, получается set path надо делать в команде линковки? чтобы .so Была найдена?\" — I've answered above.\n\nNow let me also reconsider Q1 more carefully to give a complete answer. Her claim: \"под линуксом не будет 'особых' объектников? т.к. никогда нет флага никакого в заголовочниках. а на винде будет geometry_cpp_dll.obj и geometry_cpp.obj т.к там ф-ции по-разному помечены. тогда блок динамических объектников я оберну в ifeq ($(OS), Windows_NT) т.к. это надо только под виндой\"\n\nSo on Linux she reuses the static objects (geometry_cpp.o) for the .so. This is correct regarding COMMON_API (no special marking needed). BUT:\n1. -fPIC issue: the static objects may not be PIC. Whether the .so links depends on the code. For these simple functions on x86-64 it would probably work, but it's not guaranteed/portable. [ФАКТ: requirement is -fPIC; МНЕНИЕ: likely works here on x86-64 but fragile]\n2. There's no separate compile needed for .so on Linux in terms of export marking — correct.\n\nActually, hold on. Let me reconsider -fPIC more carefully, because I want to be accurate, not alarmist.\n\nGCC on x86-64: default is not -fpic. But the generated code for the System V AMD64 ABI uses RIP-relative addressing for most global references (since it's a PC-relative ISA). When you compile a translation unit and reference a global variable defined in the same TU, with default settings, gcc may use RIP-relative addressing → position independent. If the variable is external, gcc would normally access via GOT? No — by default (non-PIC), gcc accesses external globals... hmm. Actually in the default code model `small`, references to external global variables without PIC use a 32-bit absolute relocation (R_X86_64_32) or PC-relative (R_X86_64_PC32)? \n\nLet me think about what actually happens. In practice, compiling without -fPIC and linking into a .so on x86-64:\n- Simple functions that only call other functions and use local variables: R_X86_64_PC32/R_X86_64_PLT32 relocations → these are fine for shared libs (text relocations resolved at load via PLT).\n- Accessing a global variable: with default, the compiler emits RIP-relative access, which is fine. BUT if the global is in the same TU and has internal linkage, it's fine too.\n- Taking the address of a function/global and storing into a global initialized pointer: needs absolute address → R_X86_64_64 (64-bit absolute) which is actually allowed in shared objects on x86-64? R_X86_64_64 is allowed in .so (it's a 64-bit absolute, used in GOT tables). Actually text relocations are the problem. R_X86_64_32S (32-bit sign-extended absolute) in .text → fails.\n\nGiven geometry.cpp and string_utils.cpp have no global data, no function pointers in static storage, they very likely compile to pure PIC-able code on x86-64. So the .so would link fine in practice on x86-64.\n\nHowever, I should still flag: the standard practice is to compile .so objects with -fPIC, and relying on \"it happens to work on x86-64\" is a portability trap (32-bit, other archs, or if code adds globals). Given the learning context and the mentor's standards (§4.1), I'd recommend adding -fPIC. But since she deliberately simplified (no special objects on Linux), the cleanest is to add -fPIC to the common Linux flags (or at least to objects that go into .so). \n\nHmm, but adding -fPIC to ALL objects (including executables) is slightly wasteful but totally fine for a learning project. Alternatively, note that this is the standard: shared library objects need -fPIC. I'll present it as a finding with a recommendation.\n\nNow let me also check: is there an issue with the `.so` rules not having proper prerequisites or the target mismatch. Yes, the naming mismatch I found. Let me be precise:\n\nLinux .so rules (lines 325-329):\n```\n$(BINDIR)/common/common_cpp.so: $(CPP_OBJ) | $(BINDIR)/common\n\t$(LINK) $(LINK_FLAGS) $(DLL) $(LINK_OUT)$(BINDIR)/common/common_cpp.so $^\n\n$(BINDIR)/common/common_c.so: $(C_OBJ) | $(BINDIR)/common\n\t$(LINK) $(LINK_FLAGS) $(DLL) $(LINK_OUT)$(BINDIR)/common/common_c.so $^\n```\n\nPrereqs of t3/t4 (lines 350-360): `$(SHARED_CPP_ELEMENT)` where on Linux SHARED_CPP_ELEMENT = `$(BINDIR)/common/libcommon_cpp.so` (line 339).\n\nMISMATCH: rules make `common_cpp.so`, prereqs want `libcommon_cpp.so`. On Linux, `make t3` → no rule to make `libcommon_cpp.so` → error.\n\nAlso on Windows: the grouped target makes `common_cpp.dll` and `common_cpp.lib`. SHARED_CPP_ELEMENT (Windows) = `common_cpp.lib` (line 336). ✓ matches. So Windows works, Linux broken.\n\nSo the answer to Q2 (\"this block seems useless\"): the block (CPP_SHARED/C_SHARED) is indeed currently unused/dead, BUT the naming of the shared lib is duplicated in 3-4 places, and one of them (the .so build rule) disagrees with the others (lib-prefix). The right fix is to make the variables the single source of truth and use them everywhere — then the mismatch would be impossible.\n\nActually, she might not have noticed the lib-prefix mismatch. Let me verify the exact strings again.\n\nLine 139: `CPP_SHARED := $(BINDIR)/common/libcommon_cpp.so` — lib prefix ✓\nLine 325: target `$(BINDIR)/common/common_cpp.so` — NO lib prefix ✗\nLine 339: `SHARED_CPP_ELEMENT := $(BINDIR)/common/libcommon_cpp.so` — lib prefix ✓\n\nYes, mismatch confirmed.\n\nSimilarly C:\nLine 140: `C_SHARED := $(BINDIR)/common/libcommon_c.so` ✓\nLine 328: target `common_c.so` ✗\nLine 340: `SHARED_C_ELEMENT := $(BINDIR)/common/libcommon_c.so` ✓\n\nSo on Linux: make t3 → wants libcommon_cpp.so → no rule → \"No rule to make target 'build/debug/common/libcommon_cpp.so'\". Confirmed bug.\n\nNow, is there any place that would even trigger building common_cpp.so? Nothing depends on it. So actually the .so rules are entirely unreachable on Linux, and the build fails at prereq resolution. Good — definite bug.\n\nAlso, let me check: on Linux, do the t3/t4 exe link commands include the full path to the .so? Yes: `$(SHARED_CPP_ELEMENT)` is in prereqs AND in `$^` of the link recipe. `$^` = all prereqs = `main.o libcommon_cpp.so`. So `g++ -o main main.o build/debug/common/libcommon_cpp.so`. That works for link-time (full path given).\n\nSo answer Q3: No, SET_DLL_PATH is not needed at link time, because you pass the full path to the .so directly. LD_LIBRARY_PATH is only needed at RUN time (loader). So SET_DLL_PATH belongs in test recipes (already there). Putting it in the link recipes is harmless but unnecessary — recommend removing from link.\n\nHmm wait, actually one more consideration for Q3: if instead of the full path you used `-lcommon_cpp`, then at link time the linker would need `-L` pointing to the dir OR LD_LIBRARY_PATH (gcc uses LD_LIBRARY_PATH? Actually the linker `ld` uses `-rpath` and `-L`, and it does search LD_LIBRARY_PATH? No — the *linker* doesn't search LD_LIBRARY_PATH; that's the runtime loader. At link time gcc searches `-L` dirs and standard dirs; the env var `LIBRARY_PATH` (not LD_LIBRARY_PATH) is used by gcc's collect2 for finding libs at link time.) So yes — with full path, no env needed at link time. Confirmed.\n\nSo summary of findings:\n\n1. **Q1 answered**: correct reasoning for Linux (no export-marking objects needed), but -fPIC missing → recommend adding. [ФАКТ -fPIC requirement; МНЕНИЕ - likely works here but fragile]\n\n2. **Q2 answered**: the CPP_SHARED/C_SHARED block is dead code, but it's the right idea — the bug is naming duplicated & inconsistent: .so rules produce `common_cpp.so` while prereqs want `libcommon_cpp.so` → Linux build breaks (\"No rule to make target\"). Recommend: single source of truth.\n\n3. **Q3 answered**: no, SET_DLL_PATH is not needed in link command — full path is passed to linker at link time. It's needed only at run time (tests). Currently present in link recipes (harmless but misleading).\n\n4. **run_case.cpp point 8 NOT done** — no #ifdef _WIN32 for exit code → all exit-code tests would fail on Linux.\n\n5. Also note: no -fPIC anywhere.\n\nAlso I noticed: `$(DEFINE)COMMON_STATIC` in static rules — fine on Linux.\n\nAnother point: the `CPP_OBJ`/`C_OBJ` variables on Linux (lines 322-323) reuse static objects. And SHARED_CPP_ELEMENT points to libcommon_cpp.so. But also: the .so depends on `$(CPP_OBJ)` which are the same .o files. Fine.\n\nAlso, what about `-MDd`... not relevant to Linux anymore.\n\nAlso the ASan on Linux: CONFIG_FLAGS debug = `-O0 -g -fsanitize=address`, LINK_FLAGS = `-fsanitize=address`. Good — both compile and link. ✓. But wait — for shared libs with ASan on Linux: when building a .so with -fsanitize=address, you need the runtime; when the .so is loaded into a process that also has ASan, it's fine. The exe is linked with ASan too. OK.\n\nNow, is there also the issue that on Linux the shared library objects must be PIC AND with ASan? ASan adds instrumentation; combined with the .so, it should be PIC. With ASan, globals get shadow mappings etc. ASan on x86-64 relies on a fixed shadow offset, which requires the shadow mapping — ASan works with PIC? ASan generally requires compiling with -fPIC for shared libs too. If not PIC, might have issues. But again probably works for x86-64. I'll fold this into the -fPIC recommendation.\n\nNow, since I'm in Plan Mode, I should NOT edit. I should present findings and a plan, and answer her 3 questions. Also I should probably update AGENTS.md journal? No — plan mode, no edits. I'll present the plan and ask.\n\nLet me also double check whether she wants me to verify the artifacts. She said \"сборка и тесты прошли см. артефакты\". The artifacts are in build/. Tests pass on Windows. Fine.\n\nLet me structure my answer:\n\n1. Verify what's done (1-8 mapping): points 1-7 done, point 8 (run_case) NOT done.\n2. Answer Q1 (Linux objects): correct reasoning, but -fPIC missing.\n3. Answer Q2 (CPP_SHARED dead): yes dead, but that's the symptom — naming duplicated in 3 places, and .so rules disagree (common_cpp.so vs libcommon_cpp.so) → Linux build would fail. Recommend unifying.\n4. Answer Q3 (SET_DLL_PATH in link): no — full path suffices at link time; LD_LIBRARY_PATH is runtime only. Currently in link recipes — harmless, can remove.\n\nLet me present the plan for the fixes. Since I'm in plan mode, I present and ask for confirmation.\n\nLet me be concise but complete. I'll mark [ФАКТ]/[МНЕНИЕ] and confidence per AGENTS.md §4.2.\n\nLet me write the response in Russian.\n\nActually, let me double check line numbers of the .so rules and the SHARED_CPP_ELEMENT once more from the read output:\n\nLines 320-331 (else branch of shared lib build):\n```\n320: else\n321: # ��� ������ ����� ������ .so (dll) � �� ����� .lib � ��������. � ���������� �������, ��� declspec\n322: CPP_OBJ := $(BINDIR)/common/geometry_cpp.o $(BINDIR)/common/string_utils_cpp.o\n323: C_OBJ := $(BINDIR)/common/geometry_c.o $(BINDIR)/common/string_utils_c.o\n324: (blank)\n325: $(BINDIR)/common/common_cpp.so: $(CPP_OBJ) | $(BINDIR)/common\n326: \t$(LINK) $(LINK_FLAGS) $(DLL) $(LINK_OUT)$(BINDIR)/common/common_cpp.so $^\n327: (blank)\n328: $(BINDIR)/common/common_c.so: $(C_OBJ) | $(BINDIR)/common\n329: \t$(LINK) $(LINK_FLAGS) $(DLL) $(LINK_OUT)$(BINDIR)/common/common_c.so $^\n330: (blank)\n331: endif\n```\n\nYes. Confirmed mismatch with libcommon_cpp.so in SHARED_CPP_ELEMENT (line 339).\n\nAlso note on Windows: CPP_SHARED defined at 136 as `common_cpp.dll`, and the grouped target builds common_cpp.dll + common_cpp.lib, and SHARED_CPP_ELEMENT (336) = common_cpp.lib. Consistent on Windows. \n\nSo on Linux the whole shared-lib path is broken at the dependency level. The plan should fix:\n- Use a single set of variables for shared lib names.\n- .so target should be `$(CPP_SHARED)` (= libcommon_cpp.so).\n- SHARED_CPP_ELEMENT should be derived or unified.\n\nActually, the cleanest: define names once, e.g. in the top block:\n```\nifeq ($(OS), Windows_NT)\n  CPP_SHARED := $(BINDIR)/common/common_cpp.dll\n  CPP_SHARED_LINK := $(BINDIR)/common/common_cpp.lib  # import lib for linking\n  C_SHARED := $(BINDIR)/common/common_c.dll\n  C_SHARED_LINK := $(BINDIR)/common/common_c.lib\nelse\n  CPP_SHARED := $(BINDIR)/common/libcommon_cpp.so\n  CPP_SHARED_LINK := $(CPP_SHARED)   # on Linux you link against the .so itself\n  C_SHARED := $(BINDIR)/common/libcommon_c.so\n  C_SHARED_LINK := $(C_SHARED)\nendif\n```\nThen .so/.dll rules use $(CPP_SHARED), and t3/t4 prereqs & link use $(CPP_SHARED_LINK). This unifies everything and kills both the dead block and the SHARED_CPP_ELEMENT duplication.\n\nFor -fPIC: add a `PIC_FLAGS` variable (empty on Windows, `-fPIC` on Linux) applied to the objects going into the shared lib. On Linux those are the same objects as static. Two options:\n(a) add $(PIC_FLAGS) to all Linux compiles (simplest; executables also PIC, slightly larger) — fine for this scale.\n(b) separate PIC objects for the .so on Linux (like the Windows _dll objects) — more \"correct\" but more rules.\n\nGiven she deliberately chose to reuse objects on Linux, option (a) is pragmatic. I'll present both, recommend (a) for now with a note that (b) is the textbook approach. Actually, hold on — on x86-64 it likely works even without -fPIC for this simple code. So the honest framing: [МНЕНИЕ, СРЕДНИЙ] it probably links on x86-64 without -fPIC, but it's not guaranteed and breaks if code grows globals; standard practice = -fPIC for .so objects. Recommendation: add PIC_FLAGS.\n\nFor run_case.cpp: fix with #ifdef _WIN32 or WEXITSTATUS. On POSIX: `real_code = WEXITSTATUS(std::system(...))` but WEXITSTATUS needs <sys/wait.h>. Alternative portable: \n```cpp\nint status = std::system(argv[1]);\n#ifdef _WIN32\nint real_code = status;\n#else\nint real_code = WEXITSTATUS(status);\n#endif\n```\nwith #include <sys/wait.h> on non-Windows. Simpler without WEXITSTATUS: `int real_code = status >> 8;` on POSIX (implementation-defined but glibc/musl do this). But WEXITSTATUS is the proper way.\n\nAlso note: std::system returns 0 if command succeeded (shell exit 0). On Windows returns the exit code as-is (cmd exit code). Fine.\n\nNow also: SET_DLL_PATH in link recipes — recommend removing from link (lines 351,354,357,360) and keeping only in test recipes. Actually wait, if removed from link, the link recipes become `$(LINK) $(LINK_FLAGS) $(LINK_OUT)$@ $^`. Fine.\n\nBut actually, is SET_DLL_PATH harmful in link? On Windows `set \"PATH=...;%PATH%\"` then link — the set takes effect in the cmd process; link runs; fine. On Linux `export LD_LIBRARY_PATH=...; g++ ...` — the export only affects that shell invocation; g++ runs; harmless. So harmless but useless. I'll note it.\n\nLet me also verify one more: the pattern rule `$(BINDIR)/%$(OBJ_EXT): %.cpp` — on Linux, OBJ_EXT=.o. Prereq `%.cpp` maps stem. For `t3_bbox_cpp/main.o` stem=`t3_bbox_cpp/main`, source `t3_bbox_cpp/main.cpp` ✓.\n\nBut wait — there's a subtle issue with the pattern rule and the explicit common rules on Linux for the SHARED .so objects. The explicit STATIC rules (264-274) build geometry_cpp.o etc. The pattern rule would ALSO match geometry_cpp.o. Since explicit rules take precedence over implicit (pattern) rules in make, the explicit one wins. Good — geometry_cpp.o gets -DCOMMON_STATIC. On Linux that's harmless. ✓\n\nAlso need to double check: does make complain that the explicit rule target `$(BINDIR)/common/geometry_cpp$(OBJ_EXT)` has a recipe and ALSO the pattern rule could match — no conflict; explicit wins.\n\nOK. Also — is there an issue that on Linux, `make all` (t1-t4) — t3/t4 need libcommon_cpp.so but the rule produces common_cpp.so → error. So Linux build fails at \"No rule to make target\". Even before running tests. So her claim \"сборка и тесты прошли\" is Windows-only (artifacts in build/ are Windows). She probably only tested Windows. I should gently note: on Linux it won't build yet, here's why.\n\nAlso, the naming: even if we keep `common_cpp.so` (no lib prefix), Linux CAN load/link a .so without lib prefix if you reference it by full path — that's valid. The lib-префикс is a convention for `-l` searching. Since they reference by full path, `common_cpp.so` would work. The bug is just inconsistency between the rule target and the prereq variable. But best to use lib-prefix consistently since we decided earlier to use lib-prefix (she chose `libcommon_cpp.so` in the variables). So unify on lib-prefix.\n\nNow let me also reconsider: should CPP_SHARED be the file name (with dir) or just the name? Currently it includes $(BINDIR)/common/. Fine.\n\nAlright. Let me also think about whether to raise the question about whether she wants me to just plan or actually verify by running. I'm in plan mode — can't run builds that modify. I could run `make -n` (dry run) on Linux? Can't — we're on Windows. Can't verify Linux here. So the plan stands on reasoning.\n\nLet me also check: does she have a Linux VM? From AGENTS.md journal: \"проверить корректность на виртуалке\" is in her TODO list. So she has a VM to test on.\n\nLet me write the response. Structure:\n\n## Статус пунктов 1–8\n- 1 ✓, 2 ✓, 3 ✓, 4 ✓, 5 ✓, 6 ✓(но с багом), 7 ✓, 8 ✗ (run_case не тронут)\n\n## Ответ на вопрос 1 (объектники на Linux)\n- рассуждение верное; но -fPIC\n## Ответ на вопрос 2 (CPP_SHARED блок)\n- да, мёртвый, и вот баг который он скрывает\n## Ответ на вопрос 3 (SET_DLL_PATH в линковке)\n- нет, не нужен; объяснение link-time vs run-time\n\n## План правок\n1. Унифицировать имена библиотеки в одну группу переменных\n2. -fPIC\n3. run_case exit code\n4. убрать SET_DLL_PATH из link\n\nAsk for confirmation.\n\nLet me keep it tight but educational. This is a teaching context — I should explain the WHY (link-time vs run-time resolution is a great teaching point).\n\nLet me write.",
