This is dead code that must be removed.\n\nActually let me double check there are two definitions. Line 140: `CPP_SHARED := $(BINDIR)/common/common_cpp.dll`. Line 317: `CPP_SHARED := $(BINDIR)/common/common_cpp.dll`. Yes, twice. The block at 139-145 is now redundant.\n\nHmm, but is it harmful? With `:=`, the second definition overrides. So functionally the first is ignored. But it's confusing and violates \"one source of truth\" — someone reading might think the first is used. Must remove 139-145.\n\nNow her questions:\n\n**Question 1: \"это и есть 'одна точка истины'?\" and \"под линуксом объектники будут собираться с флагом -DCOMMON_EXPORTS и т.д. в зависимости от задачи. но это просто будет неиспользуемый define, да? т.к. потом в заголовочниках на случай линукса перед ф-циями пусто. это ок что будет висячий define?\"**\n\nWait — she's asking whether on Linux the objects will be compiled with `-DCOMMON_EXPORTS`. Let me look. On Linux, the shared lib uses `$(CPP_OBJ)` = geometry_cpp.o/string_utils_cpp.o — these are built by the STATIC rules (268-278) with `$(DEFINE)COMMON_STATIC`, not COMMON_EXPORTS. So on Linux the .so objects are the static ones with COMMON_STATIC defined, NOT COMMON_EXPORTS. So her question \"объектники будут собираться с флагом -DCOMMON_EXPORTS\" — actually NO. On Linux the .so is built from the SAME objects as static (geometry_cpp.o etc.), compiled with -DCOMMON_STATIC. COMMON_EXPORTS is only used on Windows for the _dll objects.\n\nHmm wait, but she asked hypothetically \"в зависимости от задачи\" — maybe she means: on Windows, the _dll objects are compiled with COMMON_EXPORTS. On Linux there are no separate objects. So actually on Linux nothing is compiled with COMMON_EXPORTS at all. COMMON_EXPORTS never appears on Linux.\n\nBut her underlying concern: \"если бы\" — would a define like COMMON_EXPORTS/COMMON_STATIC that's unused on Linux be a problem? Answer: it's harmless. On Linux the headers have `#else #define COMMON_API` (empty) regardless of COMMON_STATIC/COMMON_EXPORTS. So defining them is a no-op. A \"hanging define\" is fine — it's just a macro definition that nothing consumes. `-DCOMMON_STATIC` on Linux: nothing checks it (the header checks `#ifdef _WIN32` first). Harmless.\n\nActually wait, more precisely: on Linux, the static rules use `$(DEFINE)COMMON_STATIC` = `-DCOMMON_STATIC`. The header on Linux ignores it (because `#ifdef _WIN32` is false → COMMON_API empty). So yes, it's an unused define. It's OK.\n\nBut note: this is slightly wasteful/confusing but not wrong. Could add `COMMON_FLAG` empty on Linux, but that's extra complexity. Since COMMON_STATIC only matters on Windows, and it's harmless on Linux, leaving it is fine. Alternatively she could argue it's noise. It's a judgment call. My take: harmless, leave it, it's not worth another variable. Actually there IS already the COMMON_FLAG target-specific variable! For t1-t2: `COMMON_FLAG := $(DEFINE)COMMON_STATIC` and t3-t4: empty. Wait but the static object rules 268-278 hardcode `$(DEFINE)COMMON_STATIC` — they don't use COMMON_FLAG. So the static common objects always get COMMON_STATIC even on Linux. Fine.\n\nSo answer to Q1 second part: On Linux, objects for .so are the SAME static ones (geometry_cpp.o, with -DCOMMON_STATIC), there's no -DCOMMON_EXPORTS on Linux at all. The unused define is harmless. Confirm.\n\nAlso, first part: \"одна точка истины?\" — Yes, the new block at 311-330 is the single source of truth for shared lib names. BUT she must delete the old duplicate block at 139-145, otherwise there are two definitions (the second wins, first is dead). Point this out.\n\n**Question 2: about -fPIC explanation. \"то есть в отношении so нельзя применить абсолютные адреса, т.к. изначально вообще неясно, какие они будут? и тогда решение - исп. GOT и относительно обращаться к данным. ещё почитала в другом месте, .so грузится в память к основному процессу а не в память отдельного процесса, так?\"**\n\nHer understanding is mostly right. Let me refine:\n- \"нельзя применить абсолютные адреса, т.к. изначально неясно, какие они будут\" — Yes, correct. The .so file is written once (on disk) but loaded at different addresses in different processes at runtime. Since the file is shared (that's why it's called shared library!) across many processes, the same bytes must be executable at whatever address the loader chooses. So the code must not contain absolute addresses that assume a fixed load address.\n- \"решение - исп. GOT и относительно обращаться к данным\" — yes, GOT for global data, PLT for functions (in older dynamically-linked setups), RIP-relative for local stuff.\n- \".so грузится в память к основному процессу а не в память отдельного процесса, так?\" — Correct! This is an important conceptual point. A shared library is not a separate process; it's mapped into the SAME address space as the calling process. Each process that uses the .so maps it into its own address space (possibly at different virtual addresses per process). So there's ONE file on disk, but many mappings at many different addresses. That's exactly why code must be position-independent: the same file bytes get loaded at different addresses in different processes.\n\nThis is a great teaching moment: shared = shared across processes, the same physical code pages are shared via mmap, but each process's virtual address where they appear can differ.\n\nAlso worth noting: on modern x86-64, the standard is actually to default to PIC, and with copy relocations removed (modern distros compile everything with -fPIC by default, even executables are PIE). So -fPIC is the norm.\n\nLet me also check the run_case.cpp include issue I raised last time: line 6 uses `#ifdef __linux__` for `#include <sys/wait.h>` but the usage is in `#else` (non-Windows). I flagged it. Did she fix it? Line 6: `#ifdef __linux__` ... `#else` uses WEXITSTATUS. It's still `#ifdef __linux__`. So the micro-fix is not applied yet. Should I re-flag? Yes, but keep it brief. Actually, for their two target platforms it works. But the correct guard is `#ifndef _WIN32` to include sys/wait.h. Minor.\n\nLet me also verify: is there anything else to plan? The main remaining work:\n1. Remove dead duplicate block at 139-145 (CPP_SHARED/C_SHARED redefined).\n2. Confirm the scheme is otherwise good.\n\nWait — also check: is `CPP_OBJ` on Linux (line 323) referencing geometry_cpp.o which is built by static rules with COMMON_STATIC. And `.so` rule at 345 builds `$(CPP_SHARED)` = libcommon_cpp.so from `$(CPP_OBJ)`. Good. And t3/t4 link against `$(CPP_SHARED_LINK)` = $(CPP_SHARED) on Linux. Good, single truth.\n\nOn Windows: CPP_SHARED_LINK = common_cpp.lib, grouped target builds both dll and lib. t3/t4 link with the lib. Good.\n\nOne more check: the first block at 139-145 defines C_SHARED for Windows as common_c.dll but later block at 319 also. So yes duplicate.\n\nAlso is there any remaining reference to the old SHARED_CPP_ELEMENT/SHARED_C_ELEMENT? Let me check. Earlier grep showed lines 336-341 had SHARED_CPP_ELEMENT. Now it seems replaced by CPP_SHARED_LINK. Let me verify no leftover SHARED_CPP_ELEMENT.\n\nLet me grep.\n\nActually I should grep to be sure. Let me do a read-only grep.\n\nLet me also double check whether the block at 139-145 still exists and confirm dead. Yes lines 139-145 shown above.\n\nLet me grep for SHARED_CPP_ELEMENT and SHARED_C_ELEMENT to ensure they're removed.",
