 Makefile only handles task 1 (t1_dist_matrix_cpp and t1_dist_matrix_c) with:\\n  - CONFIG variable (debug/release)\\n  - MSVC toolchain vars\\n  - T1 obj/exe targets, pattern rules for obj\\n  - run_case.exe tool for exit-code tests\\n  - 6 exit-code tests + norm string test per language for t1\\n  - phony targets: all, t1, test, format, format-check, clean\\n  - exit-code variables USAGE/DATA/NO_INPUT/IO_FAIL\\n\\n**What's missing for t2-t4:**\\n- t2_passport: uses string_utils (static). Tests with Russian literals → findstr encoding dance (DEFtoOEM/OEMtoDEF via powershell). Multi-input tests (echo name & echo vertexes).\\n- t3_bbox: uses string_utils + geometry as DLL (COMMON_EXPORTS → common_cpp.dll/common_c.dll). Tests with Russian literals + stderr checks.\\n- t4_filter: uses string_utils + geometry as DLL. Tests with Russian literals + stderr checks.\\n\\n**Key architectural considerations:**\\n\\n1. **Static vs DLL for common module**: t1 and t2 use static linking (`/DCOMMON_STATIC`), t3 and t4 use DLL (`/DCOMMON_EXPORTS` for the dll build, import lib for main). The Makefile currently only handles the static geometry for t1. For t3/t4 we need DLL build rules: compile with `/DCOMMON_EXPORTS`, link `/DLL /OUT:common_cpp.dll`, produce .lib (import) + .exp; main links against import lib; at runtime PATH needs to include build dir for DLL.\\n\\n   Wait — but there's an important subtlety. In the bat files, t2 uses static string_utils, and t1 uses static geometry. t3/t4 use DLL for both string_utils and geometry. So we have the same source compiled two ways: static (t1/t2) and DLL-export (t3/t4). That means the same .cpp source produces two different .obj files with different flags. The Makefile must handle this: e.g., `build/debug/common/geometry_cpp.obj` (static, for t1) vs `build/debug/common/geometry_cpp_dll.obj` (exported, for t3/t4). Or separate directories.\\n\\n ...\n\nC:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\inobitec_stud\\dialog_logs\\2026-08-15-make-t2.json:\n  Line 2