          "text": "Now I have the full picture. Let me answer the two questions.\n\n**Question 1:** She applied my scheme — the block at 311-330 defines CPP_DLL_OBJ/C_DLL_OBJ/CPP_OBJ/C_OBJ + CPP_SHARED/CPP_SHARED_LINK/C_SHARED/C_SHARED_LINK in one place. She also put objects there. And she says \"this is one source of truth?\" — yes.\n\nBut wait — there's a duplication now: `CPP_SHARED`/`C_SHARED` is defined BOTH at lines 139-145 (old block) AND at lines 317-329 (new block). That's a leftover! Lines 139-145 are the OLD block that defines CPP_SHARED/C_SHARED. The new block at 317-329 redefines them. So there's still duplication of CPP_SHARED/C_SHARED definitions. The old block at 139-145 is dead/duplicated — need to remove it.\n\nLet me double check: \n- Line 139-145: old ifeq block defines CPP_SHARED and C_SHARED.\n- Line 311-330: new ifeq block defines CPP_DLL_OBJ, C_DLL_OBJ, CPP_SHARED, CPP_SHARED_LINK, C_SHARED, C_SHARED_LINK (Windows) or CPP_OBJ, C_OBJ, CPP_SHARED, CPP_SHARED_LINK, C_SHARED, C_SHARED_LINK (Linux).\n\nSo CPP_SHARED/C_SHARED are defined twice. With `:=`, the later definition wins. In make, later assignments override earlier. So the values at 317-329 take effect. The old block 139-145 is dead code — should be removed per §4.1 п.9. This is exactly the kind of leftover that the mentor catches. I must point this out.\n\nAlso there's another subtlety: the DYNAMIC objects section (280-294) is inside `ifeq ($(OS), Windows_NT)` — and uses `$(DEFINE)COMMON_EXPORTS`. On Linux, there are no separate dll objects (she uses CPP_OBJ = geometry_cpp.o / string_utils_cpp.o — the SAME objects as static). Good.\n\nNow her question: \"под линуксом объектники будут собираться с флагом -DCOMMON_EXPORTS и т.д. в зависимости от задачи. но это просто будет неиспользуемый define, да?\"\n\nWait — on Linux, the .so is built from CPP_OBJ = geometry_cpp.o and string_utils_cpp.o, which are built by the STATIC rules (lines 268-278) with `$(DEFINE)COMMON_STATIC`, NOT COMMON_EXPORTS. So on Linux, the objects for .so are compiled with -DCOMMON_STATIC. And COMMON_STATIC on Linux — since headers have `#ifdef _WIN32` guarding, on Linux COMMON_API is empty regardless of COMMON_STATIC or COMMON_EXPORTS. So yes, -DCOMMON_STATIC is an unused define on Linux. Harmless.\n\nActually wait — she asked about -DCOMMON_EXPORTS specifically. On Linux there is no COMMON_EXPORTS anywhere (the dynamic object rules are Windows-only). The .so uses the STATIC objects (compiled with -DCOMMON_STATIC). So her premise \"объектники будут собираться с флагом -DCOMMON_EXPORTS\" is slightly off — on Linux they're compiled with -DCOMMON_STATIC (from the STATIC section). Either way, both defines are unused on Linux since COMMON_API ignores them. So the answer: yes, it's a dangling define, harmless. \n\nBut is it OK? Yes. It's a no-op macro. There's a philosophical point: a define that nothing reads is harmless but \"noise\". Actually it does matter in one sense: the define doesn't hurt. And there's a subtle thing — you could argue it's slightly misleading, but not wrong. The cleaner approach would be not to define COMMON_STATIC on Linux, but it's truly harmless and adds complexity to distinguish. I'll confirm it's fine and explain why.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. On Windows:\n- static objects (t1-t2): -DCOMMON_STATIC → COMMON_API empty → functions are plain extern\n- dll objects (t3-t4): -DCOMMON_EXPORTS → COMMON_API = __declspec(dllexport)\n- main t3-t4: no define → COMMON_API = __declspec(dllimport)\n\nOn Linux:\n- the .so is built from the SAME static objects (geometry_cpp.o compiled with -DCOMMON_STATIC). COMMON_API empty. Symbols exported by default from .so. Works.\n- main t3-t4: no define → COMMON_API empty on Linux (else branch). Works.\n- So -DCOMMON_STATIC is defined on Linux but never read (headers skip due to _WIN32 check). Harmless dangling define. ✓\n\nSo the answer is yes, confirmed.\n\n**Question 2:** She asks about the -fPIC explanation:\n- \"то есть в отношении so нельзя применить абсолютные адреса, т.к. изначально вообще неясно, какие они будут?\" — Yes, essentially correct. The .so is loaded at some address chosen by the loader at runtime, unknown at build time. Actually more precisely: it's not that \"unknown\" — it's that it differs between processes, and the file on disk is a single artifact shared across processes. So absolute addresses baked into the file would break.\n- \"решение - исп. GOT и относительно обращаться к данным\" — Yes, GOT for data, and PC-relative addressing.\n- \".so грузится в память к основному процессу а не в память отдельного процесса, так?\" — YES. This is correct and important. A .so is NOT a separate process. It's loaded (mapped) into the address space of the calling process. Each process that uses the library maps it into ITS OWN address space. The physical code pages can be shared (that's the memory savings — \"shared library\"), but the virtual addresses where they appear can be different in each process. So the code must be position-independent precisely because the mapping address differs per process.\n\nLet me answer precisely: the loader maps the .so into the process's virtual address space; the same .so file can be mapped at different virtual addresses in different processes; that's why code inside can't assume a fixed address.\n\nI should be precise: \"грузится в память основного процесса\" — yes. A dynamic library is not a separate process — it's loaded into the address space of the process that uses it. The dynamic loader (ld.so) maps the file's segments into the virtual memory of the current process.\n\nAlso GOT: The compiler generates code that references data via GOT (Global Offset Table) using PC-relative addressing to reach the GOT entry, and the GOT entry holds the actual address, which the loader fills at load time (relocations). For functions, PLT (Procedure Linkage Table) for lazy binding.\n\nNow, also should note: on x86-64, most instructions are naturally RIP-relative, so simple code may work without -fPIC, but global data access needs GOT when PIC. That's what I said earlier.\n\nLet me also check the run_case include issue — she said \"run case cpp поправила\". She added `#ifdef __linux__` include sys/wait.h, and `#ifdef _WIN32`/`#else` WEXITSTATUS. The include is under `__linux__` but usage under `#else` (non-Windows). I flagged that inconsistency earlier. It's still there: include guarded by `__linux__`, but WEXITSTATUS used under `#else`. For the two target platforms it's fine, but strictly it should be `#ifndef _WIN32` for include. I should mention this briefly since I flagged it before and it wasn't fully addressed. Actually she said \"run case cpp поправила\" — she did the WEXITSTATUS part. The include guard inconsistency remains. Let me point it out briefly.\n\nActually wait, let me re-read her message: \"3. run case cpp поправила. тоже кратко расскажи почему старшие биты на линуксе - это статус ожидания(?) и почему так сделано\". So she wants explanation of wait status. I already explained it in the previous turn partially. Now she asks again briefly. I'll give the brief version.\n\nLet me now also verify: in the new scheme, is the .so rule correct? Lines 345-349:\n```\n$(CPP_SHARED): $(CPP_OBJ) | $(BINDIR)/common\n\t$(LINK) $(LINK_FLAGS) $(DLL) $(LINK_OUT)$(CPP_SHARED) $^\n```\nOn Linux, CPP_SHARED = libcommon_cpp.so. Target = libcommon_cpp.so. Recipe: g++ -shared -o libcommon_cpp.so geometry_cpp.o string_utils_cpp.o. Good. And t3/t4 depend on CPP_SHARED_LINK = $(CPP_SHARED) on Linux. Good — single source of truth. \n\nOn Windows, grouped target: `$(CPP_SHARED) $(CPP_SHARED_LINK) &: ...` where CPP_SHARED = common_cpp.dll, CPP_SHARED_LINK = common_cpp.lib. Good.\n\nBut: there's a remaining duplication: lines 139-145 old block still defines CPP_SHARED/C_SHARED. Must be removed. Let me point that out clearly.\n\nAlso, there's now duplicate variable names across ifeq blocks — CPP_OBJ vs CPP_DLL_OBJ. The Linux block defines CPP_OBJ (line 323), Windows defines CPP_DLL_OBJ (line 314). These are only used in the respective branches. OK.\n\nAlso note: the DYNAMIC objects block (280-294) is wrapped in ifeq Windows_NT, and it uses `geometry_cpp_dll$(OBJ_EXT)`. Good. The .so on Linux uses static objects with PIC_FLAGS. Wait — PIC_FLAGS is only in the STATIC object rules (268-278), which build geometry_cpp.o etc. Those are used both for static t1-t2 and for the .so on Linux. So PIC objects are used for exe too — that's the tradeoff we discussed; fine.\n\nSo the answer to \"это и есть одна точка истины?\" — yes, now names are defined once per platform in block 311-330 and used in rules + prereqs. BUT the leftover block 139-145 still redefines CPP_SHARED/C_SHARED — dead duplicate, must remove. Since later `:=` wins, functionally harmless but it's exactly the \"mёртвый код\" the mentor flags.\n\nLet me also double check that there's no OTHER place where CPP_SHARED is used that I missed. From grep: lines 140,141,143,144 (old block), 317-329 (new block), 338,339,341,342,345,346,348,349 (rules). So the old block 139-145 is purely dead now.\n\nNow, one more consideration for Q1: she asks \"под линуксом объектники будут собираться с флагом -DCOMMON_EXPORTS и т.д. в зависимости от задачи. но это просто будет неиспользуемый define, да?\"\n\nClarify: On Linux the .so is built from the STATIC objects (geometry_cpp.o etc., compiled with -DCOMMON_STATIC, because they come from the STATIC rules 268-278). COMMON_EXPORTS is never used on Linux — the dynamic object rules are Windows-only. So:\n- For t1-t2 objects: -DCOMMON_STATIC (unused on Linux) ✓ harmless\n- For the .so: same objects, -DCOMMON_STATIC (unused on Linux) ✓ harmless\n- main t3-t4: COMMON_FLAG empty → no define. ✓\n\nSo yes, all defines are unused on Linux because the headers branch on _WIN32 first. Harmless. Confirm.\n\nNow let me structure the answer:\n\n**Q1:**\n1. The new block 311-330 IS the single source of truth — good. Confirm the pattern: names defined once, used in rules and prereqs.\n2. BUT: leftover dead block 139-145 still redefines CPP_SHARED/C_SHARED. Remove it. It's a duplicate definition (dead, because later `:=` wins). This is exactly the §4.1 п.9 issue.\n3. About -DCOMMON_EXPORTS on Linux: clarify — on Linux the .so is built from static objects with -DCOMMON_STATIC, not COMMON_EXPORTS. COMMON_EXPORTS never appears on Linux. Both defines are unused on Linux since headers check _WIN32 first. Harmless dangling defines. Confirm it's OK.\n\n**Q2:**\n- Yes, absolute addresses can't be baked into .so because loader maps it at runtime-determined virtual addresses, and the same file maps at different addresses in different processes.\n- Solution: PC-relative + GOT (data), PLT (functions), loader fills GOT at load.\n- .so loads into the address space of the calling process — correct, not a separate process. Explain briefly.\n- Brief note: same .so in two processes → two virtual mappings (possibly different addresses), but shared physical pages.\n\n**run_case explanation (from previous message she asked #3 but the message numbering...)**: Actually in this message she has \"1.\" and \"2.\" and mentions \"3. run case cpp поправила\" — wait, she numbered 1., 2., 3., 4. — four items, where 3 is run_case and 4 is \"убрала\" (removed SET_DLL_PATH from link). So I need to address:\n- 1: scheme + dangling define question\n- 2: fPIC understanding + object question + PIC explanation\n- 3: run_case fixed + explain wait status bits\n- 4: removed\n- plus the extra question about CRT/ASan.\n\nWait, in THIS message she wrote:\n1. (makefile scheme + висячий define)\n2. (fpic explanation + question about PIC in non-so objects + \"расскажи про позиционно независимые объектники\")\n3. (run_case — explain wait status)\n4. (убрала — SET_DLL_PATH)\n+ доп вопрос про CRT и ASan.\n\nOK so I need to answer all of these. Let me be concise but complete.\n\nNote: I already answered the run_case wait-status question in the previous turn (the \"почему старшие биты\" was explained). She asks again briefly. I'll give a short recap.\n\nAlso I flagged the include guard inconsistency — she should use `#ifndef _WIN32` for the include. Let me remind briefly.\n\nAlso the PIC explanation — she wants \"кратко расскажи про позиционно независимые объектники - для чего это надо и как это устроено внутри\". I'll give a compact explanation.\n\nLet me also confirm her understanding: \".so грузится в память к основному процессу а не в память отдельного процесса, так?\" — yes. Emphasize: that's the KEY insight — a shared library is not a separate process; it's code+data mapped into the process's address space. Same physical pages can be shared between processes, but virtual addresses differ.\n\nNow, since this is plan mode, I should present findings and a plan, not edit. The remaining fixes:\n1. Remove dead block 139-145 (duplicate CPP_SHARED/C_SHARED).\n2. run_case: change include guard `#ifdef __linux__` → `#ifndef _WIN32` for consistency.\n\nEverything else looks good. Let me present.\n\nLet me be careful about the \"висячий define\" — I should confirm it's OK but also note the cleaner alternative: on Linux, COMMON_STATIC is meaningless; you could drop -DCOMMON_STATIC on Linux, but that adds complexity for zero gain. Keep it — harmless. Actually, wait: is there a cleaner reason to keep it? The objects are shared between static and .so on Linux, and both use COMMON_STATIC define. Dropping it would require distinguishing. Not worth it. Confirm harmless.\n\nLet me write the response in Russian, concise, with [ФАКТ]/[МНЕНИЕ] labels and confidence.",
