        {
          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "Two things:\n1. Answer the sub-question about why globals and arrays break without -fPIC.\n2. The run_case.cpp: the include is now inside the #else block, inside main, in the middle of the function. Is that OK?\n\nLet me answer both.\n\n**Why globals and arrays break without -fPIC:**\n\nWithout -fPIC, the compiler generates code that references global variables with absolute addressing (or with 32-bit relocations against the symbol). Specifically:\n- On x86-64, accessing a global variable normally uses RIP-relative addressing (PC-relative) which is fine even in shared libs IF the symbol is local. But for externally visible symbols, the compiler without -fPIC uses... hmm, actually let me think carefully.\n\nThe real technical detail: without -fPIC, GCC generates absolute (or GOT-free) references to global symbols. On x86-64:\n- A reference to a global variable like `extern int x; ... x++;` compiled without -fPIC generates a 32-bit PC-relative relocation `R_X86_64_PC32` against `x`. In a shared library, this is a problem because it's a *text relocation*: the linker must put a fixed address there, but in a shared object the address isn't known until load time, and the dynamic linker would need to patch .text (which is read-only/executable, and patching it at load time is expensive and breaks page sharing).\n- With `-fPIC`, the compiler routes global data access through GOT: `x` accessed via a GOT entry. The GOT is in .data/.got section (writable), which the dynamic linker CAN patch at load time. The code does `mov rax, [rip+got_offset]` to get the actual address of `x` from GOT, then accesses `x`.\n\nSo the reason globals specifically matter:\n1. A global variable that is *defined* in the same translation unit — its address is known at link time, but for shared lib, at load time it's relocated, and the relocation is a *link-time* absolute address baked in. Actually for a global defined in the same file, non-PIC uses a 32-bit absolute (R_X86_64_32) or PC-relative; either way it needs to be resolved at load, and if placed in .text, the dynamic linker cannot patch it (text relocations are disabled/unsupported on modern systems — glibc/musl with -Wl,-z,relro and especially with hardened defaults, and on x86-64 glibc refuses text relocations by default).\n2. An *extern* global: address not known even at link time, must be resolved via GOT/PLT — hence the \"recompile with -fPIC\" error.\n3. Arrays: taking the address of an array, or indexing a global array, may generate an absolute relocation because the compiler computes `&arr[i]` as a constant base+offset — the base address of `arr` is absolute. Similarly for string literals / static data.\n\nWhy do functions typically \"just work\"? Calls use relative addressing (`call rel32`) which is position-independent by nature — the distance between caller and callee is fixed regardless of where the code is loaded (as long as everything in the same module stays together). That's why simple code without globals often links fine. Also, function *addresses* taken and stored in data need GOT/PLT, but calls themselves are relative.\n\nThe precise error: `relocation R_X86_64_32S against `.data' can not be used when making a shared object; recompile with -fPIC`.\n\nLet me give a clean explanation with an analogy. Also mention the load-time vs link-time relocation and text relocations.\n\nAlso clarify: even with a global defined locally, without -fPIC, GCC can emit absolute address; with -fPIC it emits GOT access. And why arrays especially: `arr[i]` with `arr` as a global/static — the address computation.\n\nActually let me be careful and precise about x86-64 default code model. With `-fPIC` vs default:\n- Default (no PIC) code model small: references to global symbols use 32-bit PC-relative `R_X86_64_PC32` for data... hmm. Actually on x86-64, GCC without -fPIC for a *defined* global in same TU: accesses it with RIP-relative addressing `mov eax, [rip+disp]` where disp is a 32-bit PC-relative relocation (R_X86_64_PC32). This is position-independent IF the symbol is local (resolved by linker to a fixed offset within the module). For shared library, the linker can resolve local symbols at link time with a relative offset — this actually works without text relocations! That's why simple functions work.\n\nBut for an *external* symbol (defined in another module), without -fPIC, GCC generates... a GOT-free reference? No. For external data symbols without -fPIC, on x86-64, GCC generates a reference that the linker resolves via copy relocation or... this gets complicated.\n\nLet me simplify to the level that matters and is correct:\n\nThe core issue: **the address of a global symbol is not known until load time** (the .so can be loaded at any address; and for extern globals, in any process). Without -fPIC, the compiler bakes the assumption of a fixed address into the instruction stream (.text). Relocations against symbols in .text are \"text relocations\" — the dynamic linker would have to patch executable pages at load time. Modern Linux refuses text relocations (or they defeat page sharing, break W^X, etc.).\n\n- Calls between functions: PC-relative (distance-based) — work everywhere → that's why functions-only code often links fine.\n- References to globals (read/write) and taking addresses of arrays/globals: need the *absolute address* → text relocation → fail without -fPIC. With -fPIC, the address is fetched from GOT (a data section that IS patched at load time).\n\nArrays especially: because `&arr[0]` / `arr[i]` often requires materializing the absolute base address of `arr` as a constant; and string literals/static const data too.\n\nLet me also verify the exact error message. GCC/ld: \"relocation R_X86_64_32S against symbol `x' can not be used when making a shared object; recompile with -fPIC\". Yes.\n\n**run_case.cpp include placement:**\n\nCurrently:\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib>\n#include <iostream>\n#include <print>\n#include <string>\n\nint main(...) {\n    ...\n    int status = std::system(argv[1]);\n#ifdef _WIN32\n    int real_code = status;\n#else\n#include <sys/wait.h>\n    int real_code = WEXITSTATUS(status);\n#endif\n    ...\n}\n```\n\nIs `#include` in the middle of a function OK? Technically, `#include` is processed by the preprocessor before compilation — the text of `<sys/wait.h>` is pasted at that exact point, in the middle of main, before `int real_code = WEXITSTATUS(status);`. Since headers typically contain declarations, macro definitions, etc., pasting them inside a function body is *mostly* tolerated but is bad practice and risky:\n- If the header declares anything at file scope (functions, variables, types) — it would be illegal inside a function body → compile error. `sys/wait.h` mostly defines macros (`WEXITSTATUS` is a macro) and typedefs/types. Macros are fine anywhere. typedefs inside function scope are legal. struct/enum definitions at block scope are legal in C but in C++? C++ allows local class definitions, so fine. But some headers use `static const` variables at namespace scope → would become local static? No, `static const` inside a function is a block-scope static — that changes semantics but usually compiles. Also headers often use `extern` declarations — illegal in function body.\n- In practice `<sys/wait.h>` on glibc is macro-heavy and might work, but it's fragile, non-idiomatic, and clang-tidy / style would flag it.\n\nCorrect approach: put `#ifndef _WIN32` + `#include <sys/wait.h>` at the top with the other includes. The `#ifdef _WIN32/#else` for the code can stay in main, OR keep both in sync. The cleanest:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib>\n#include <iostream>\n#include <print>\n#include <string>\n#ifndef _WIN32\n#include <sys/wait.h>\n#endif\n\nint main(...) {\n    ...\n    int status = std::system(argv[1]);\n    #ifdef _WIN32\n    int real_code = status;\n    #else\n    int real_code = WEXITSTATUS(status);\n    #endif\n    ...\n}\n```\n\nSo answer: technically compiles