kslashes? GNU make on Windows sets CURDIR with backslashes? Actually it can have mixed. This is used for PATH on Windows: `set \"PATH=$(CURDIR)/$(BINDIR)/common;%PATH%\"`. `$(BINDIR)` = `build/debug`. `$(CURDIR)` = e.g. `C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\inobitec_stud`. So PATH = `C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\inobitec_stud/build/debug/common;...`. Mixed slashes. Windows generally accepts forward slashes in PATH. It works (tests are green). OK, not a bug.\n\n4. **Issue: line 99 comment** — comment mentions `-Wl --gc-sections` with space between `-Wl` and `--gc-sections` which is wrong syntax, but it's a comment, and the actual LINK_FLAGS is empty. This is just a comment documenting future option. Minor.\n\n5. **Issue: format-check uses `**/*.cpp` glob.** The `$(wildcard **/*.cpp)` — in GNU make, `**` is treated same as `*` (two stars don't have special meaning). So `**/*.cpp` matches files in one directory level deep only — actually `*` matches any characters including `/`? No — in make's wildcard, `*` matches any characters, INCLUDING `/`! So `**/*.cpp` actually matches... hmm. In make wildcard, `*` matches any sequence of characters, and it does match `/` too. Actually GNU make's wildcard `*` matches `/`. So `**/*.cpp` = `*` + `*` + `/` + `*.cpp` = effectively `*`+`/`+`*.cpp` = matches any single-level path with a slash... Actually since `*` matches `/` too, `**/*.cpp` would be equivalent to matching... Let me think. `**/*.cpp` where `*` matches any chars including `/`: the pattern `**/*.cpp` → two `*`s then `/` then `*.cpp`. First `**` can match `t1_dist_matrix_cpp`, then `/`, then `*.cpp` matches `main.cpp`. So it matches `t1_dist_matrix_cpp/main.cpp`. But would it match `common/geometry.cpp`? `**` matches `common`, `/`, `*.cpp` matches `geometry.cpp`. Yes. Would it match files two levels deep like `a/b/c.cpp`? `**` matches `a/b`? The pattern is `**/*.cpp` = `**` + `/` + `*.cpp`. For `a/b/c.cpp`: `**` = `a`, `/` = `/`? No — the `/` in the pattern is literal. `**` would need to match `a/b` to leave `/c.cpp` matched by `/` + `*.cpp`? `**` matches `a/b`, then literal `/`, then `*.cpp` matches `c.cpp`. Since `*` can match `/`, yes. So `**/*.cpp` matches all .cpp files at any depth that have at least one directory. But NOT `hello.cpp` in root (no slash). Hmm interesting. Actually this is a known gotcha. But there's `hello.cpp` in root. Would `format` miss it? `**/*.cpp` requires a `/` in the path. `hello.cpp` has no slash → not matched. So format-check would NOT check `hello.cpp`. \n\n   Hmm, but wait — is that a real concern? Let me verify: GNU make wildcard `**/*.cpp`. Actually I recall that GNU make's `*` does match `/`, making `**` unnecessary and `**/*.cpp` equivalent to `*/*.cpp` essentially matching any file with at least one directory component. Root-level .cpp files (like hello.cpp) won't be matched. This means format-check might not format hello.cpp. But hello.cpp — is it still in the repo? Yes, hello/ dir and hello.cpp? Let me check. In the root listing earlier: `hello/` directory. And there's `main.obj`, `vc140.pdb` in root — build artifacts from earlier manual builds. Actually those are junk in the repo root (main.obj, vc140.pdb). Wait — that's worth noting: root has `main.obj` and `vc140.pdb` — build artifacts that should be gitignored and removed. Let me check .gitignore. Actually earlier listing of repo root showed: `main.obj`, `vc140.pdb` files at root. Those are stale build artifacts. That's a repo-hygiene issue (§4.1 п.2). Worth flagging.\n\n   Let me check .gitignore to see if they're ignored.\n\n6. **Issue: `all` builds t1-t4 but doesn't include `test`. Fine, that's by design.**\n\n7. **Issue: phony targets and `all` echo \"COMPILED\" even when nothing rebuilt** — cosmetic.\n\n8. **Line 251: pattern rule for obj uses `$(OBJ_OUT)$@` for /Fo: and `-o`.** On MSVC `/Fo:build/debug/.../main.obj`. OK. On Linux `-o build/debug/.../main.o`. OK.\n\n9. **Issue: STATIC common objects get PIC_FLAGS on Linux.** These are also linked into t1/t2 executables. PIC code in executables — works but slightly less efficient. Already discussed and accepted. OK.\n\n10. **Real issue candidate: the `.so` objects on Linux come from the STATIC rules (geometry_cpp.o) which compile with `-DCOMMON_STATIC`, and with `PIC_FLAGS`. Good — that's the same object used both in t1/t2 static linking AND in the .so. Wait — but is `geometry_cpp.o` used in BOTH t1 (static) and .so? Yes. That's fine.**\n\n    But — important: on Linux, `CPP_OBJ` = `geometry_cpp.o string_utils_cpp.o`. These are the STATIC-compiled objects. The .so rule (line 335-336) links them with `$(LINK) $(LINK_FLAGS) $(DLL) $(LINK_OUT)$(CPP_SHARED) $^`. LINK = g++. DLL = -shared. LINK_FLAGS for debug = `-fsanitize=address`. So `g++ -shared -fsanitize=address -o build/debug/common/libcommon_cpp.so geometry_cpp.o string_utils_cpp.o`. \n\n    Wait — ASan + shared library on Linux: when you link a .so with `-fsanitize=address`, the ASan runtime must be loaded first. For an executable that's fine (linked with ASan). The t3/t4 executables link with `$(LINK_FLAGS)` which includes `-fsanitize=address`. So the exe has ASan runtime; the .so also instrumented. At runtime the exe loads first with ASan. Should be OK.\n\n11. **Issue candidate: t3/t4 main.exe on Linux links against `$(CPP_SHARED_LINK)` = the .so directly.** The link command: `$(LINK) $(LINK_FLAGS) $(LINK_OUT)$@ $^`. `$^` = main.o + libcommon_cpp.so. So g++ links exe against .so. At runtime, LD_LIBRARY_PATH set in test recipes. But what about running `t3_bbox_cpp/main.exe` directly from the command line outside make? It won't find .so unless LD_LIBRARY_PATH set. That's inherent to the design (documented \"Запуск из корня\"). OK.\n\n    BUT — there's a subtle issue: at link time, when linking against a .so by full path, the linker records the SONAME or the path in DT_NEEDED. Without `-soname` flag, the DT_NEEDED will be the full path used at link time: `build/debug/common/libcommon_cpp.so` (relative). At runtime, the loader looks for that path relative to CWD. If you run from root, it resolves. With LD_LIBRARY_PATH set to `CURDIR/build/debug/common`, but DT_NEEDED contains a path with slash — for paths with slashes, the loader treats it as a path (relative/absolute), NOT searching LD_LIBRARY_PATH. Hmm! This is important.\n\n    Let me think. When linking `g++ ... /path/to/libcommon_cpp.so`, the linker records in DT_NEEDED the string... For a shared library specified by full path, ld records the SONAME if present, otherwise the filename as given? Actually: \"If the library has a SONAME, the SONAME is used as DT_NEEDED. Otherwise, the path as given is used (the filename portion if -l was used with search, or the full path if the full path was given).\" Actually ld records: if the file has SONAME, use SONAME; else use the pathname as it appeared (minus directory if found via -l search path, keeping full path if given directly). Hmm, need to be careful.\n\n    Actually the rule: DT_NEEDED records the SONAME if present; otherwise the path used to find the library. Since they pass full relative path `build/debug/common/libcommon_cpp.so`, DT_NEEDED = `build/debug/common/libcommon_cpp.so`. At runtime, since it contains a `/`, loader uses it as a path relative to CWD. Running from project root → works. LD_LIBRARY_PATH is NOT consulted for entries with `/`. So the SET_DLL_PATH on Linux... the `export LD_LIBRARY_PATH` — is it actually needed? If DT_NEEDED is the relative path and tests run from root, it resolves directly without LD_LIBRARY_PATH. But it's harmless.\n\n    However, since `$(CURDIR)` is used in SET_DLL_PATH for Linux: `export LD_LIBRARY_PATH=$(CURDIR)/$(BINDIR)/common:$$LD_LIBRARY_PATH`. And in link recipe for .so, they don't add `-Wl,-rpath`. So at runtime when running from root, DT_NEEDED relative path works. OK, not a bug per se. But worth noting as a fragility: if they ever run the exe from a different dir, it breaks. Already noted in journal.\n\n12. **Bigger issue candidate: on Linux, the .so is linked with `$(LINK_FLAGS)` which in release is empty. Fine.**\n\n13. **Issue: t1_cpp_norm.ok uses `$(FC)` = fc on Windows / diff on Linux. `$(call WCMD,$(BINDIR)/.../t1_test1 tests/expect/t1_test1)` → on Windows backslash-converts. On Linux diff compares CRLF vs LF? The output file from t1 has CRLF? On Linux, program writes `\\n` only (no CRLF conversion). The expect file `tests/expect/t1_test1` — was created on Windows, might have CRLF or LF. If it has CRLF and Linux output has LF, diff fails!** This is the known \"diff vs CRLF\" issue they noted in journal (\"fc/diff. diff может быть чувствителен к CRLF\"). It's already a known open item. Worth re-flagging as still open.\n\n    Let me check the encoding of expect files. Earlier journal noted expect files have CRLF (0D 0A). On Linux, program output would be LF-only (unless... no CRLF translation on Linux). So diff would flag every line different. This is a real Linux-blocker for norm tests. But it's already in their TODO list (\"проверить корректность на виртуалке\", \"fc/diff CRLF\"). So known.\n\n14. **Issue: check_contains compares byte strings for substring. On Linux, the expect files in CP1251 with Russian text — but program on Linux outputs... the source is CP1251 encoded, so the literal bytes are the same CP1251 bytes in the executable (GCC doesn't transcode string literals). So output bytes = CP1251 = matches expect file bytes. OK for substring match. But t1_test1 (numbers only) and t4_test1 (numbers) use FC/diff, which is line-based and CRLF-sensitive.**\n\n    Actually wait — does t1_norm use check_contains? No, t1_norm uses `$(FC)` (line 402-403). Only numbers. So CRLF matters.\n\n15. **Issue: `$(RUN_CASE)` used as a prerequisite in many test rules (e.g., line 385 `$(RUN_CASE)`) while recipe uses `$(RUN_CASE_CMD)`.** On Linux, RUN_CASE = `$(BINDIR)/tools/run_case` (no .exe). RUN_CASE_CMD = same. OK consistent. On Windows RUN_CASE has .exe, RUN_CASE_CMD = backslash version. Prerequisite uses forward-slash .exe path — matches build rule target `$(BINDIR)/tools/run_case.exe` (forward slash). OK.\n\n    Wait, but t2_cpp tests use `$(RUN_CASE_CMD)` as prerequisite (line 429: `$(T2_CPP_EXE) $(RUN_CASE_CMD) ...`). That's inconsistent — using the _CMD variant in prerequisites. On Windows, RUN_CASE_CMD = backslash path while the actual target is forward slash. A prerequisite with backslashes won't match the built file target → make considers it a separate file that doesn't exist → \"No rule to make target 'build\\debug\\tools\\run_case.exe'\"? Hmm, but tests pass on Windows. Let me check: line 429 t2_cpp_norm uses `$(RUN_CASE_CMD) $(FIND_SUBSTR_CMD)`. If RUN_CASE_CMD = `build\\debug\\tools\\run_case.exe` and make looks for that as a target... there IS a rule for `build/debug/tools/run_case.exe` (forward slash). On Windows, make normalizes? GNU make on Windows treats `build\\...` and `build/...` — it may canonicalize. Actually GNU make on Windows converts backslashes in targets to forward slashes internally? There's `$(subst /,\\)` explicitly used for command execution because cmd needs backslashes. But targets are matched... GNU make on Windows does do some path normalization. Given tests are green, it evidently works (make compares paths case-insensitively and treats / and \\ equivalently in many cases on Windows). This is fragile but working. It's a minor consistency nit: prerequisites should use `$(RUN_CASE)` not `$(RUN_CASE_CMD)`. Worth a small note.\n\n16. **Issue: `IO_FAIL := 74` exit code is defined but never used in tests.** Not a bug — just unused constant. Minor.\n\n17. **Issue: `.gitignore`** — should check. Root has main.obj, vc140.pdb (stale artifacts). Let me check git status to see if they're tracked/ignored.\n\nLet me check git status and .gitignore. Also check whether the dead CPP_SHARED block at 139-145 was removed (she said she removed it). Looking at current file: lines 138-141 are the COMMON_FLAG target-specific, NOT the dead CPP_SHARED block. The old block at 139-145 (from previous version) is GONE. Good — she removed it. The shared names now only defined at 302-320. \n\nLet me verify: is there any other duplication? Line 317-319: CPP_SHARED/CPP_SHARED_LINK defined in Linux else branch; line 307-310 in Windows. Good. Single source.\n\nNow, potential real issues to report:\n\nA. **`format-check`/`format` wildcard misses root-level .cpp** — `$(wildcard **/*.cpp)` requires at least one directory level. hello.cpp at root wouldn't be matched. Actually is hello.cpp still in the repo? Let me check. Earlier root listing: `hello/` dir exists. Is there a hello.cpp at root? The listing showed `hello/` directory but I didn't see hello.cpp file at root. Let me check. Actually the listing: `.clang-format .editorconfig .git/ .gitignore 00-temy.md ... hello/ ... main.obj ... vc140.pdb`. There's a `hello/` directory, so hello.cpp is probably in hello/. But there's also possibly main.obj/vc140.pdb at root (stale artifacts). Let me verify with git status and check .gitignore.\n\nB. **Stale artifacts in repo root: `main.obj`, `vc140.pdb`** — if not gitignored, they'd be tracked. Let me check.\n\nC. **CRLF vs diff on Linux** — known open item, re-flag.\n\nD. **Consistency: prerequisites use both `$(RUN_CASE)` and `$(RUN_CASE_CMD)`** — minor.\n\nE. **`$(FC)` on norm tests only for t1 and t4 test1-3, but t2/t3 norm use FIND_SUBSTR. On Linux diff CRLF issue applies to t1_norm and t4 test1-3.**\n\nF. **Comment typo at line 99: `-Wl --gc-sections` has space; actual would be `-Wl,--gc-sections`. But it's a comment. Minor.**\n\nG. **`all` target doesn't depend on `test`** — by design, fine.\n\nH. **Potential real bug: On Linux, t3/t4 executables link against the .so, but `make t3` target only depends on `$(T3_CPP_EXE)`/`$(T3_C_EXE)`. The exe depends on `$(CPP_SHARED_LINK)` = the .so. Good, so .so gets built. And the exe link rule uses `$^` which includes the .so path. Good.**\n\nI. **`SET_DLL_PATH` uses `$(CURDIR)/$(BINDIR)/common` — for Linux, `$(CURDIR)` is absolute, so LD_LIBRARY_PATH is absolute. Good.**\n\nJ. **The `.ok` files and test inputs are in `tests/` but the data generation (gen_cloud) isn't in the Makefile graph.** From journal: \"генератор gen_cloud — не в графе\". The current Makefile (750 lines) — does it have gen_cloud? Let me search. I didn't see gen_cloud in the read. The tests t3_test1/t3_test2/t3_test3 use `tests/input_data/t3_test1` (committed data files), not generated clouds. So mass tests (10/100/10⁶ from gen_cloud with seeds) are NOT in the Makefile. This was flagged before as \"наставник убрал\" per journal. Hmm, actually the journal says \"массовых тестов нет — наставник убрал. ок.\" So this was a decision. OK, not to flag.\n\nLet me check git status and .gitignore for root artifacts, and hello.cpp location.\n\nLet me also double check: does `format` use `$(wildcard **/*.h)` missing `.hpp`? line 743: `**/*.cpp **/*.c **/*.hpp **/*.h` — all four. format-check line 746: `**/*.cpp **/*.c **/*.h **/*.hpp` — all four (order differs but fine).\n\nLet me verify .gitignore and root artifacts via git commands (read-only).",
