nd line: `build/debug/common/libcommon_cpp.so` (relative). At runtime, DT_NEEDED = that relative path → the loader looks for `build/debug/common/libcommon_cpp.so` relative to CWD. Since tests run from root with CWD=root, the relative path resolves! And LD_LIBRARY_PATH also covers it. Actually if DT_NEEDED is a relative path with slash, ld.so treats it as a path and tries it directly (not via LD_LIBRARY_PATH). Since CWD during make test is root, `build/debug/common/libcommon_cpp.so` resolves relative to root. OK it works. Fragile but works.\n\nThis is worth a note: without SONAME on the .so, the DT_NEEDED is the raw path; it works only because CWD=root. It would be more robust to add `-Wl,-soname,libcommon_cpp.so` and `-Wl,-rpath,'$$ORIGIN/../common'`. But that's an enhancement, and possibly beyond current scope. I'll mention it as a fragility note (the kind that \"найди ещё одно\" wants).\n\n15. **Let me check the `all` target: `all: t1 t2 t3 t4`. And `test: format-check ...`.** `format-check` runs clang-format --dry-run. On Linux VM, clang-format may not be installed → test fails. That's an environment dependency. But that's her setup. Fine.\n\n16. **`$(TOUCH)` on Windows = `type nul >`. In recipes like `&& $(TOUCH) $@` — `type nul > build/debug/test/xxx.ok` — creates empty file. But on Windows `$@` in the recipe — make expands `$@` to the target path with forward slashes `build/debug/test/t1_cpp_abc.ok`. `type nul > build/debug/test/t1_cpp_abc.ok` — cmd handles forward slashes in redirection OK. Fine.\n\n17. **A REAL subtle one: `$(call WCMD, ...)` used inside `$(RUN_CASE_CMD) \"...\"`. On Windows, the command string passed to run_case is backslash-converted. But `$(T1_CPP_EXE) < tests/input_data/t1_abc > $(NULLDEV)` — inside WCMD converts slashes in paths. But `2> $(NULLDEV)` on Windows = `nul`. Fine.\n\nOn Linux, run_case gets `$(T1_CPP_EXE) < tests/input_data/t1_abc > /dev/null 2> /dev/null` and std::system runs via `/bin/sh`. Redirection works. Exit code now v