          "text": "Let me analyze. t4_test1 expect has 0 CRLF pairs (17 bytes, likely LF). t1_test1 has 2 CRLF pairs (76 bytes). On Linux, the program outputs `\\n` only (no CR translation). So `fc`/`diff` on Linux would compare: program output (LF-only) vs expect file (CRLF for t1_test1). diff would report differences due to CRLF. That's a real cross-platform concern.\n\nBut wait — on Windows the program output redirected to file also produces CRLF (text mode). On Linux, redirect gives LF only. The expect file t1_test1 has CRLF. So on Linux, diff would fail for t1_norm test. This is a known TODO item in her journal (\"fc/diff. diff может быть чувствителен к CRLF, возможно нужно будет сделать тесты более универсальными\").\n\nLet me note that as a finding.\n\nNow let me look for more issues in the Makefile.\n\nKey observations from full review:\n\n1. **Line 95-100: Linux release LINK_FLAGS empty** — that's fine. Comment at line 99 is a TODO about -ffunction-sections. OK.\n\n2. **The `COMMON_FLAG` target-specific variable:** Line 139-141. For t1-t2 exes: `COMMON_FLAG := $(DEFINE)COMMON_STATIC`. For t3-t4: empty. But the pattern rules at 250-255 use `$(COMMON_FLAG)`. When make builds `main.obj` as a prerequisite of the exe, the target-specific variable propagates to all prerequisites. So main.obj of t1 gets COMMON_STATIC. But wait — the static common objects (geometry_cpp.obj) are built with explicit `$(DEFINE)COMMON_STATIC` in the recipe (line 259). And pattern rule would apply to `geometry_cpp.o`? No — the pattern rule `$(BINDIR)/%$(OBJ_EXT): %.cpp` would match `build/debug/common/geometry_cpp.o` from `common/geometry_cpp.cpp` — but the source is `common/geometry.cpp`, not `geometry_cpp.cpp`. So the explicit rule at 258 wins. Good.\n\n   But wait — there's a subtle issue: the pattern rule for `build/debug/common/geometry_cpp.o` — the target name is `geometry_cpp.o` while source is `geometry.cpp`. The explicit rule `$(BINDIR)/common/geometry_cpp$(OBJ_EXT): common/geometry.cpp ...` handles that. OK.\n\n3. **Line 314-315, 323-324: hardcoded `.obj`/`.o` in `CPP_DLL_OBJ`/`C_DLL_OBJ`/`CPP_OBJ`/`C_OBJ`.** In Windows branch, `CPP_DLL_OBJ` uses `.obj` literal (line 304). On Linux branch, `CPP_OBJ` uses `.o` literal (line 313). These are inside ifeq branches where OBJ_EXT is fixed (.obj on win, .o on linux). So it's consistent with the platform. But it's slightly inconsistent style — could use `$(OBJ_EXT)`. Since the branches are platform-specific, hardcoding is acceptable but inconsistent with the rest using `$(OBJ_EXT)`. Minor.\n\n4. **Line 304-305: `geometry_cpp_dll.obj` — but the DLL obj rules at 273-283 use `$(OBJ_EXT)`.** On Windows OBJ_EXT=.obj so it matches. OK.\n\n5. **The `.so` target on Linux (335-339): `$(CPP_SHARED)` = `libcommon_cpp.so`.** Built from `$(CPP_OBJ)` = static objects compiled with `-DCOMMON_STATIC` and `-fPIC`. Good — this is the design.\n\n   Wait, but there's a subtle problem: on Linux, `geometry_cpp.o` is used BOTH for static linking (t1/t2) AND for the .so (t3/t4). That's fine and intended.\n\n   BUT — the object files for the .so are compiled with `-DCOMMON_STATIC` define (from static rules, line 259). On Linux COMMON_API is empty regardless, so it doesn't matter. Good.\n\n6. **ASan on Linux debug + .so**: LINK_FLAGS = -fsanitize=address applied to both exe and .so link. Fine.\n\n7. **`format-check` uses `$(wildcard **/*.cpp ...)`** — the glob `**/*.cpp` in make — does `**` work in make wildcard? In GNU make, `**` is NOT recursive glob — `*` matches any chars including `/`? Actually in make's wildcard, `*` matches any characters including `/`? Let me recall: GNU make's wildcard function: `*` matches any string, and importantly `*` does match `/`. So `**/*.cpp` — two stars then slash. The first `*` matches `t1_dist_matrix_cpp`, the second `*`... hmm. Actually in make, `*` matches across `/` too. So `**/*.cpp` would match `t1_dist_matrix_cpp/main.cpp`? `**/` — first `*` matches `t1_dist_matrix_cpp`, second `*` matches empty, then `/`. Then `*.cpp`. So it matches files one level deep. It also would match `common/geometry.cpp`. What about `tests/run_case.cpp`? Yes. \n\n   But does it match `hello/hello.cpp`? Yes. So `format` covers all the task dirs and common and tests and hello and gen_cloud. Files at root level like... there are no root .cpp files (hello is in hello/). OK so format is fine. But `**` in make is just two `*`, so `**/*.cpp` matches `*/x.cpp` — one directory level. All source is one level deep. Fine.\n\n8. **CRLF in expect files vs `$(FC)`/`diff` on Linux.** t1_test1 has CRLF. On Linux, program output to a file uses LF only (no text-mode translation on Linux). `diff` would flag CRLF as differences. So `t1_cpp_norm` / `t1_c_norm` and `t4_cpp_test1/2/3` and `t4_c_test1/2/3` (which use `$(FC)`) would fail on Linux due to CRLF mismatch in expect files. This is a real cross-platform issue. The check_contains-based tests (FIND_SUBSTR) are substring-based, so CRLF doesn't matter there.\n\n   Actually wait — t4_test1 expect has 0 CRLF (LF only, 17 bytes). t1_test1 has CRLF (76 bytes, 2 pairs). Mixed. The output on Linux would be LF. t4_test1 expect is LF — matches. t1_test1 expect is CRLF — would differ on Linux. So this is a real issue for t1_norm tests specifically.\n\n   This is already noted in her journal as a known TODO item (\"fc/diff. diff может быть чувствителен к CRLF\"). I should re-flag it.\n\n9. **`clean` doesn't remove `$(CURDIR)`-independent** — fine.\n\n10. **`CONFIG ?= debug` and BINDIR.** When you run `make clean` it removes `build` dir entirely. OK.\n\n11. **Potential issue: `$(TOUCH)` on Windows is `type nul >` — used as `&& $(TOUCH) $@`.** The `.ok` target file path `$@` has forward slashes. `type nul > build/debug/test/t1_cpp_abc.ok` — cmd accepts forward slashes in path? Actually cmd handles `/` in paths mostly fine. And it's already been working. OK.\n\n12. **Important: `RUN_CASE` and `FIND_SUBSTR` are used as prereqs in test rules (e.g. line 385 `$(RUN_CASE)`), but recipes use `$(RUN_CASE_CMD)`.** On Windows, RUN_CASE has forward slashes (as a target), RUN_CASE_CMD has backslashes (for cmd execution). The prereq `$(RUN_CASE)` matches the actual target rule (built with forward slash). Good — that's the split they designed. In t2 tests they use `$(RUN_CASE_CMD)` as prereq (line 429) instead of `$(RUN_CASE)`! Let me check: line 429: `$(TESTDIR)/t2_cpp_norm.ok: $(T2_CPP_EXE) $(RUN_CASE_CMD) $(FIND_SUBSTR_CMD) tests/input_data/t2_input tests/expect/t2_norm | $(TESTDIR)`. \n\n    Here prereq uses `$(RUN_CASE_CMD)` (backslash version on Windows) as a PREREQUISITE. But the target rule builds `$(BINDIR)/tools/run_case.exe` (forward slashes). make compares target names literally. On Windows, `build\\debug\\tools\\run_case.exe` (from RUN_CASE_CMD) vs the built file `build/debug/tools/run_case.exe`. Since tests pass on Windows, make evidently treats them... hmm. Actually GNU make on Windows: file paths with backslashes vs forward slashes. make normalizes? In GNU make on Windows, `\\` is not a path separator for make's internal target matching — but the file does exist on disk either way. When make checks whether a prereq exists, it does a stat() on the name as given. On Windows, stat() with `build\\debug\\tools\\run_case.exe` works (backslashes accepted by Win32). So make sees the file exists, no rule needed (it's up to date if it exists and is not \"phony\"). Since the .ok rule's prereqs include RUN_CASE_CMD, make just checks existence/timestamp — the file was built earlier as `build/debug/tools/run_case.exe`, and `stat(\"build\\debug\\tools\\run_case.exe\")` finds the same physical file. So it works, but it's inconsistent: t1/t3/t4 use `$(RUN_CASE)` as prereq, t2 uses `$(RUN_CASE_CMD)`. This is an inconsistency (a defect in uniformity), though functionally OK on both platforms (on Linux they're identical). Worth flagging as a minor inconsistency.\n\n    Actually wait, is it a latent bug? If the tools are NOT yet built and make runs `make test`, for t2_cpp_norm it needs `$(RUN_CASE_CMD)` = `build\\debug\\tools\\run_case.exe`. Is there a rule with that exact target name? The rule at line 364 builds `$(BINDIR)/tools/run_case.exe` (forward slash). Target names are matched literally by make. `build\\debug\\tools\\run_case.exe` vs `build/debug/tools/run_case.exe` — these are different strings! So make might say \"No rule to make target 'build\\debug\\tools\\run_case.exe'\". BUT the file exists on disk (built by the forward-slash rule earlier), and make's implicit behavior: if a target is mentioned as prereq but no rule exists and the file exists, make uses the file as-is (it's up to date). So it works only because run_case.exe was already built. On a clean clone, `make test` — order of prereq evaluation... make would try to build all T1 tests first, which build run_case.exe via the forward-slash rule (t1 tests use RUN_CASE forward slash). Then when it gets to t2, the file exists. So it works by lucky ordering! If t1 tests were removed, t2 would fail on Windows with \"No rule to make target\". That's a real latent bug / inconsistency. Worth flagging.\n\n    Actually, let me double-check: make treats the target `build\\debug\\tools\\run_case.exe` — no rule exists for it. make looks for implicit rules (%.exe etc). There's no pattern. So \"No rule to make target\". Unless file exists. If run_case.exe was built previously in the same make invocation (via forward slash rule), the file exists on disk. make checks: for a prereq with no rule, if file exists and is up-to-date relative to the target, fine. Actually make only errors \"No rule to make target\" when the file does NOT exist. If it exists, it's treated as an existing file (though make may warn). Actually make does warn: \"warning: overriding...\" no. Let me recall: GNU make, if a prerequisite has no rule and doesn't exist → \"No rule to make target\". If it exists → fine, no rule needed. So on clean, first invocation: t1 tests are listed BEFORE t2 tests in `test` target. make processes prereqs left to right; T1_CPP_TESTS builds run_case.exe first. Then t2 sees the file exists → OK. So it works. But it's fragile and inconsistent. Flag as minor.\n\n13. **`$$LD_LIBRARY_PATH` in SET_DLL_PATH (line 502):** `export LD_LIBRARY_PATH=$(CURDIR)/$(BINDIR)/common:$$LD_LIBRARY_PATH`. In make, `$$` escapes to `$` for shell. Good. On Linux, `export X=... && cmd` — works in sh.\n\n14. **`SET_DLL_PATH` on Linux: does the runtime even need it?** The exe links against the .so by full path at link time, but at runtime the loader needs to find the .so. DT_NEEDED will contain \"libcommon_cpp.so\" (soname) — since the .so has no SONAME set... Actually when linking against a .so by path, the linker records DT_NEEDED = the soname if present, else the path given? By default, if no -soname, the DT_NEEDED entry is the path as given on the link command line? Actually ld records DT_NEEDED = the filename (basename) or the path? If you link with full path to .so, DT_NEEDED records the path as given (the full path) unless the .so has a SONAME. If the .so has no SONAME, the loader records the path you specified. So DT_NEEDED = `build/debug/common/libcommon_cpp.so` (relative). At runtime, loader looks for that relative path → relative to CWD. If you run the exe from the project root (which tests do via make), it resolves. And LD_LIBRARY_PATH also contains it. So it works. But this is a subtle thing — the exe's DT_NEEDED is a relative path baked in, so running from elsewhere breaks. This is the \"запуск из корня\" constraint. OK, already known.\n\n    Actually — worth noting: if no SONAME, linking by full path bakes the full path (or the given path) into DT_NEEDED. Then LD_LIBRARY_PATH isn't even consulted for that entry (entries with a slash are used directly). So SET_DLL_PATH might be redundant — but harmless. And it also means if you move the build dir, it breaks. This is a deeper topic; probably beyond scope but could mention as a \"найди ещё одно\".\n\n15. **`$(CPP_DIRS)` order-only prereq on pattern rules (line 250):** `| $(CPP_DIRS)` lists ALL cpp dirs, so building any obj triggers mkdir for all of them — fine (cheap, idempotent). OK.\n\n16. **Pattern rule `$(BINDIR)/%$(OBJ_EXT): %.cpp | $(CPP_DIRS)`** — this also matches `build/debug/tools/run_case.obj`? No, tools have explicit rules. And it would match `gen_cloud/main.o` if gen_cloud were built via pattern — but gen_cloud isn't in the Makefile at all. Wait — is gen_cloud in the Makefile? Earlier there was GEN_CLOUD variable and rule in old versions, but current version — grep found no GEN_CLOUD. So gen_cloud is not in the Makefile anymore. Interesting — the task requires gen_cloud for mass tests (sizes 10/100/10⁶). The journal said \"массовых тестов нет — наставник убрал\" — actually the journal said on 2026-08-18 review: \"Массовых тестов нет — наставник убрал. ок.\" Hmm, that was a review note. Actually reading again: \"5. Референсов нет.\" and point 1 \"Массовые тесты отсутствуют — gen_cloud не в графе сборки... наставник убрал\". So it seems the mentor removed mass tests? Not sure. Let me not re-open; it was discussed. Actually the journal says \"Массовых тестов нет — наставник убрал. ок.\" — so it's a deliberate decision. OK, skip.\n\n17. **Missing: `t3_bbox_ref.ps1` and `t4_filter_ref.ps1` at root** — these are reference scripts (per §4.9 эталоны на лету). Not in Makefile graph. But that was deliberate (reference computed on the fly, not stored). The journal notes \"референсов нет\". These ps1 files exist but aren't used by make. Hmm — the norm tests use `tests/expect/*` committed files, which contradicts §4.9 п.3 (\"Эталоны генерятся на лету, в репо не хранятся. Замороженный снапшот выхода — не эталон корректности\"). Wait — `tests/expect/t1_test1` etc ARE committed files. Is that a violation? The journal mentioned \"ref-файлы не трогаем, эталоны ps1/литералы\" and she chose to keep literals for t1. This was already decided/discussed. Not re-opening.\n\n18. **Line 57: `CXXFLAGS := $(CXXSTD) $(WARN) /EHsc $(CONFIG_FLAGS)`** on Windows — has `/EHsc` inline, not a variable. On Linux CXXFLAGS lacks it. Fine.\n\n19. **`PIC_FLAGS` placed after `$(DEFINE)COMMON_STATIC` and before `$(CXXFLAGS)`** — order irrelevant. OK.\n\n20. **`$(BINDIR)/common/geometry_cpp$(OBJ_EXT)` rule compiles with `$(DEFINE)COMMON_STATIC` but the DLL version compiles with `$(DEFINE)COMMON_EXPORTS` only on Windows.** On Linux there's no separate DLL obj — the .so uses the static objs. Fine.\n\n21. **Potential real issue: on Linux, t3/t4 exe links against `.so` — but the link rule `$(LINK) $(LINK_FLAGS) $(LINK_OUT)$@ $^` uses `$^` which includes `$(CPP_SHARED_LINK)` = the .so path. Good. But the executable's runtime search — LD_LIBRARY_PATH needed. SET_DLL_PATH is only in TEST recipes, not in the link recipe — that's fine (link-time doesn't need LD_LIBRARY_PATH, the .so path is given directly).**\n\n    Wait — the journal previously asked about this and resolved: link-time doesn't need SET_DLL_PATH because the .so is given by full path. And run-time tests have it. Good.\n\n22. **Missing prerequisite: t3/t4 exe link rule depends on `$(CPP_SHARED_LINK)`** — on Windows that's the `.lib` (import lib), which is a grouped target with the `.dll`. Good — if lib is missing, group rebuilds. On Linux, CPP_SHARED_LINK = the .so itself. Good.\n\n23. **`clean` uses `$(RMDIR) build`** — on Windows `rmdir /s /q build`. If build doesn't exist → error, but make continues? No, make stops. Minor.\n\n24. **`all` phony target echoes \"COMPILED\" but doesn't depend on `test`.** Fine.\n\n25. **Potential issue: `.PHONY` includes `format`/`format-check`/`clean` but the `test` target depends on `format-check` — if clang-format isn't installed, test fails. That's by design (format cleanliness required).\n\n26. **Encoding issue with Makefile in CP1251 and git** — known.\n\n27. Let me reconsider the most impactful \"find one more\": The **run_case.cpp on Windows still returns `int real_code = status;`** — on Windows `std::system` returns the exit code as given? Actually on Windows, `std::system` returns the exit code of the command as reported by the CRT (`_wsystem` returns the exit status). Yes it's the exit code. Good.\n\n    But what about the run_case on Windows when the command fails to start? Returns -1 or whatever. Edge, fine.\n\n28. **One more real check — the `.so` on Linux uses objects compiled with `-fsanitize=address` (debug) AND `-fPIC`.** Linking a shared lib with ASan requires the ASan runtime to be loaded first. When the exe (also ASan-instrumented, linked with -fsanitize=address) loads the .so, fine. The test recipe runs exe with LD_LIBRARY_PATH. ASan runtime comes from the exe. Should be OK.\n\n29. **`$(TOUCH) $@` on Linux is `touch`** — creates the .ok file. Good.\n\n30. Now a subtle one: **t4 tests use `$(RUN_CASE_CMD)` as prereq too** (e.g., line 573). Same as t2 — the backslash version. Same latent inconsistency.\n\n31. **t1_cpp_norm uses `$(FC)` which on Windows is `fc`, on Linux `diff`.** Comparing `$(BINDIR)/t1_dist_matrix_cpp/t1_test1` against `tests/expect/t1_test1`. The program output on Linux: the numbers are formatted with fixed precision — should match byte-for-byte except line endings (expect has CRLF, Linux output LF). On Linux diff fails → t1_cpp_norm and t1_c_norm break on Linux. This is the CRLF issue. Confirmed real for t1 (expect has CRLF).\n\nSo the concrete NEW findings to report:\n\n**Finding A (most important): CRLF in committed expect files breaks `$(FC)`/`diff` tests on Linux.** t1_test1 expect has CRLF (2 pairs). On Linux the program writes LF; `diff` sees mismatch → t1_cpp_norm / t1_c_norm fail on Linux despite correct program. t4_test1 expect is LF-only (0 CRLF) so t4 test1/2/3 (also use `$(FC)`) are fine. Fix options: (a) make expect files LF, (b) normalize before diff, (c) use check_contains (substring) instead of fc/diff for these, (d) on Linux pipe through `tr -d '\\r'`. This was flagged in her journal as an open item (\"fc/diff. diff может быть чувствителен к CRLF\") — so confirm it's still open and now concrete.\n\n**Finding B (inconsistency, latent): t2/t4 test rules use `$(RUN_CASE_CMD)`/`$(FIND_SUBSTR_CMD)` as *prerequisites*, while t1/t3 use `$(RUN_CASE)`/`$(FIND_SUBSTR)`.** On Windows the _CMD variants contain backslashes, which don't match any rule target (built with forward slashes). Works only because t1 tests build the tools first and the files exist; on a fresh build if t1 were skipped, make would say \"No rule to make target 'build\\debug\\tools\\run_case.exe'\". Fix: use `$(RUN_CASE)`/`$(FIND_SUBSTR)` in prereqs consistently (the _CMD variant is for recipes only).\n\n**Finding C (minor): hardcoded `.obj`/`.o` in `CPP_DLL_OBJ`/`C_DLL_OBJ` (304-305) and `CPP_OBJ`/`C_OBJ` (313-314) instead of `$(OBJ_EXT)`.** Works because they're inside platform branches, but inconsistent with everything else using `$(OBJ_EXT)`.\n\n**Finding D (observation, deeper): DT_NEEDED on Linux.** The .so is linked without `-soname`; the exe links against it by full path, so DT_NEEDED bakes the relative path `build/debug/common/libcommon_cpp.so`. Runtime resolution depends on CWD=repo root; LD_LIBRARY_PATH in SET_DLL_PATH is actually redundant for entries containing '/'. If build dir is moved, exe breaks. Proper fix: `-Wl,-soname,libcommon_cpp.so` at .so link + `-Wl,-rpath` or LD_LIBRARY_PATH. This is deeper and maybe beyond scope — mention as observation, medium priority.\n\nLet me verify Finding D with a quick read — is there -soname? grep found none. Right, no soname, no rpath. On Linux ld: when linking against a .so given by path, if the library has no SONAME, DT_NEEDED = the path as given on the command line. So yes, DT_NEEDED = relative path. That means it works from root. And LD_LIBRARY_PATH is not consulted for entries containing '/'. So SET_DLL_PATH export is effectively a no-op for the .so found via path. Hmm — but wait, DT_NEEDED contains slash → loader treats as path → resolves relative to CWD. The tests run from root (make from root), so resolves. OK. So the finding: LD_LIBRARY_PATH is redundant; the real mechanism is CWD. Worth mentioning.\n\nLet me also double check Finding B more rigorously — does GNU make on Windows treat `build\\debug\\tools\\run_case.exe` as existing file? Yes, stat works with backslashes. So the .ok rule's prereq `build\\debug\\tools\\run_case.exe` exists → fine. And if it doesn't exist yet and there's no rule → error. The `test` target lists T1 tests first, which create it. So it works by order. Actually — even more subtle: make processes all rules at once; when it needs `build\\debug\\tools\\run_case.exe` (backslash) as prereq of t2_cpp_norm.ok, and no rule matches that exact name, but the file exists after t1 builds it... make checks existence at update time, not load time. make builds prerequisites in order; by the time it builds t2_cpp_norm.ok, the file exists. So no error. Confirmed latent only. \n\nAlso, is there a scenario where the tools DON'T get built? If user runs `make t2` only (not test), t2 target = exes, doesn't need tools. Fine. If user runs `make t4` etc, same. Only `test` builds tools. Fine.\n\nActually, there's one more: `make -j` parallel could race? t1 builds run_case while t2 also needs it — both reference different names (forward vs backslash). With -j, make might try to build t2_cpp_norm.ok before run_case.exe is built (t1's forward-slash rule). If make checks prereq `build\\debug\\tools\\run_case.exe` (backslash) and it doesn't exist and no rule → error even with -j. But without -j, order saves it. Not critical but a real latent bug. Worth flagging with medium confidence.\n\nLet me now also verify the wildcard format claim quickly — does `**` work in make? Let me not run it; I'm confident: in GNU make, `*` in wildcard matches any chars including `/`, and `**` = `*` `*`. Actually careful: `$(wildcard **/*.cpp)` — `*` matches any sequence of characters (including `/`? or not?). In make, the glob implementation: `*` matches any sequence of chars including `/`? Historically GNU make's wildcard uses its own glob that treats `*` as matching `/` too... Actually the GNU make manual says the wildcard function uses the shell's glob semantics where `*` does NOT match `/`? Hmm. Let me think. Actually the manual: \"The wildcard characters `*`, `?` and `[..]` match filenames.\" In make, `*` does match `/` because make's glob is not shell-based. I recall `$(wildcard **/*.cpp)` in make: the first `*` can match `t1_dist_matrix_cpp`, then second `*`... wait pattern `**/*.cpp`: chars `*`,`*`,`/`,`*`,`.cpp`. If `*` matches `/` too, then `**/*.cpp` = `*/*.cpp` effectively matching one directory level (since `**` can also be `*` matching across `/`). This is a known ambiguity. In practice, on GNU make, `$(wildcard **/*.cpp)` DOES match files in subdirectories one level deep and possibly deeper. The current repo: all .cpp are one level deep (t1_..., common/, tests/, gen_cloud/, hello/). So format works. Not worth flagging.\n\nLet me also verify: is `hello/hello.cpp` covered by format? Yes, one level. Is `gen_cloud/main.cpp` covered? Yes. Good.\n\nNow let me also check whether `format-check` includes Makefile/README — no, only c/cpp/h/hpp. Fine.\n\nLet me finalize findings. I should present the review concisely. The user asked \"найди хоть ещё одно\" — find at least one more issue. I have concrete ones. Let me present them ranked, with [ФАКТ]/[МНЕНИЕ] and confidence, and propose fixes. I'm in plan mode so no edits.\n\nLet me present:\n\n1. CRLF in t1_test1 expect breaks `fc`/`diff` norm tests on Linux (t1_cpp_norm, t1_c_norm). t4_test1 is LF, fine. [ФАКТ, ВЫСОКИЙ] — already in her TODO list, now concrete.\n\n2. t2/t4 use `$(RUN_CASE_CMD)`/`$(FIND_SUBSTR_CMD)` (backslash paths) as *prerequisites*, t1/t3 use `$(RUN_CASE)`/`$(FIND_SUBSTR)` (forward). On Windows no rule matches backslash names — works only because t1 builds tools first (order dependency; breaks with `-j` or if t1 removed). Fix: use non-CMD vars in prereqs everywhere. [ФАКТ, СРЕДНИЙ]\n\n3. Hardcoded `.obj`/`.o` in CPP_DLL_OBJ/C_DLL_OBJ/CPP_OBJ/C_OBJ instead of `$(OBJ_EXT)`. [ФАКТ, ВЫСОКИЙ — works but inconsistent]\n\n4. No `-soname` on .so → DT_NEEDED bakes relative path; LD_LIBRARY_PATH is redundant; exe tied to CWD/root and build-dir location. Deeper topic. [ПРЕДПОЛОЖЕНИЕ, СРЕДНИЙ]\n\nMaybe also: `clean` fails if build/ missing? minor, skip.\n\nActually let me double-check finding 2's claim about t3. Line 506: `$(TESTDIR)/t3_cpp_empty_case.ok: $(T3_CPP_EXE) $(RUN_CASE) $(FIND_SUBSTR) tests/input_data/...` — t3 uses `$(RUN_CASE)` (forward). t1 uses `$(RUN_CASE)`. t2 uses `$(RUN_CASE_CMD)` (line 429). t4 uses `$(RUN_CASE_CMD)` (line 573). So it's t2 and t4 that use _CMD in prereqs. Confirmed.\n\nAlso FIND_SUBSTR_CMD in prereqs for t2/t4 (line 429, 573). Confirmed.\n\nGood. Let me write the review. Concise but complete. Mark confidence and categories. Ask her to decide which to fix.",
