          "text": "The user found that expect files have CRLF everywhere. She asks: can I rewrite the bytes directly?\n\nI'm in plan mode — read-only. I cannot edit files. But I can plan how to do it, and answer her question.\n\nQuestion: can she rewrite bytes directly? Yes, options:\n1. `clang-format`? No, that's for source.\n2. PowerShell to strip CRLF -> LF in the expect files.\n3. git has a mechanism: `.gitattributes` with `* text=auto` or `text eol=lf` — but that affects future checkouts, not current files. Actually setting `*.txt text eol=lf` would normalize on next checkout. But that changes repo config.\n4. Edit each file and save with LF in FAR Editor or editor.\n\nActually the simplest and most robust: use a command to strip `\\r\\n` → `\\n` in all `tests/expect/*` files. Since she's on Windows with PowerShell, she could do it. But I'm in plan mode so I can only plan.\n\nAlso important consideration: WHY does the expect file have CRLF? The program output when redirected through cmd on Windows gets CRLF (CRT text mode translates \\n to \\r\\n). So on Windows, the program produces CRLF output, and the expect files were created by redirecting output → CRLF. So on Windows the `$(FC)` comparison matches because both sides CRLF.\n\nOn Linux, program produces LF only, expect files still CRLF → mismatch.\n\nOptions to fix cross-platform:\n(a) Normalize expect files to LF. But then on Windows, `fc` comparing program output (CRLF) vs expect (LF) would FAIL on Windows! Because on Windows program output has CRLF. So normalizing to LF fixes Linux but breaks Windows. Hmm.\n\nWait — actually, does the program output CRLF on Windows? When stdout is redirected to a file via cmd `> file`, the CRT's stdout in text mode converts \\n → \\r\\n. So yes, output file has CRLF on Windows. So `fc` on Windows compares CRLF output vs expect. If expect becomes LF, fc fails on Windows.\n\nSo the simple \"convert expect to LF\" breaks Windows. This is the real trap — it's a cross-platform test issue, not just \"fix the file\".\n\nOptions:\n1. Compare through a normalization step on both platforms: strip CR before comparing. E.g., use a C++ test exe that reads both files and compares after normalizing line endings. That's the robust cross-platform way.\n2. Use `$(FIND_SUBSTR)` (check_contains) which does substring matching — CRLF-insensitive in the sense that it searches for a substring. But for whole-file comparison (t1 norm, t4 test1-3) substring isn't full comparison. Though check_contains checks expected is a substring of result — for full line comparison it's not exact.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider. The task's policy §4.9: \"Сравнение вещественных в тестах: расстояния и центроиды — float-арифметика; эталон и результат сравниваются с фиксированным форматом вывода (3 знака — тогда `fc` честен)\". And the whole-file comparisons (t1_norm, t4_test1-3) use FC/diff.\n\nThe cleanest fix: a line-ending-normalizing comparison. Options:\n- On Linux use `diff <(tr -d '\\r' ...)` — but process substitution is bash-ism, not portable.\n- Use a helper exe (like check_contains but full compare after normalizing CRLF). Could extend check_contains or make it byte-compare after stripping \\r.\n- Or: use `$(FC)` on Windows as-is, and on Linux use `diff` after stripping CR via a portable one-liner.\n\nActually there's a simpler robust approach: make the comparison a C++ exe (per §4.9 п.2: logic longer than one-liner → test-exe). check_contains already reads files as bytes and does substring. Could add a mode for exact comparison ignoring \\r. But that changes tests/check_contains.cpp.\n\nHmm, but wait — what does the program actually output? t1_test1 output is a matrix of numbers (no Russian). t4_test1-3 are filtered points (numbers). So these are pure ASCII/numbers. The CRLF issue is only about line endings.\n\nAlternative pragmatic approach: The simplest fix that keeps both platforms honest: **strip `\\r` from the output and expect, then fc/diff**. On Windows: `fc` compares after... no.\n\nActually let me reconsider. Windows `fc` — does fc treat \\r\\n vs \\n as equal? No, fc is byte-ish comparison of lines; CRLF vs LF would differ.\n\nThe truly robust approach: compare normalized content. A small portable way without C++: \n- On Linux: `diff <(tr -d '\\r' < out) <(tr -d '\\r' < expect)`. Process substitution is bash-specific; GNU make runs `/bin/sh` which on many systems is dash — no process substitution. So not portable.\n- Could write temp normalized files: `tr -d '\\r' < out > out.norm` etc. That's two commands — a multi-step recipe. Per §4.9 п.2, logic longer than one-liner should be a test-exe.\n\nSo the cleanest per course rules: extend/modify `check_contains` (or add a new tool) that compares two files byte-for-byte after stripping `\\r` (or normalizing line endings). That's the \"exe-тест\" approach. It's cross-platform, no shell-isms.\n\nAlternatively: keep expect in LF and make the comparison via check_contains with an \"exact match ignoring line endings\" mode.\n\nActually — hold on. Let me reconsider the whole picture. There are two comparison mechanisms in the Makefile:\n1. `$(FIND_SUBSTR)` / check_contains: substring match (used for t2, t3, t4 error messages). CRLF doesn't matter there as long as the substring itself doesn't include line endings. Wait, but if expect contains CRLF and result contains CRLF, substring matches. On Linux result has LF, expect has CRLF — if the expect substring spans a line ending, it wouldn't match. For single-line substrings (like \"Фигура «звезда»: 6 вершин.\" without trailing \\r\\n in expect?), need to check. Actually check_contains reads both as bytes; if expect file ends with CRLF and result has just LF, and expected is the full line content without trailing newline — matches. So substring tests are mostly OK but fragile.\n\n2. `$(FC)` / fc-diff: full file compare for t1_norm and t4_test1-3.\n\nSo the CRLF problem is specifically for the FC-based full compares.\n\nWhat's the best plan? Let me present options with tradeoffs:\n\nOption A: Normalize expect files to LF AND normalize on compare for both platforms. Since program output on Windows is CRLF and on Linux is LF, the compare must normalize both sides. So a byte-normalizing compare is the real fix. On Windows fc can't normalize, so we need check_contains-style exe.\n\nActually simplest: add a \"normalize line endings then compare exactly\" to check_contains, and switch the FC-based tests to it. This is one exe change + changing `$(FC)` usage in ~6 recipes. Cross-platform, honest.\n\nOption B: Keep it platform-specific: on Windows keep fc (expect stays CRLF), on Linux use a command that strips \\r from both then diff. Multi-line recipe or temp files → violates one-liner rule, and Linux recipe needs bash-isms. Not great.\n\nOption C: Don't compare files at all; keep fc for numbers but make programs output LF-only? Can't control Windows CRT text mode easily; actually could use binary mode (setmode) but that's messy.\n\nOption D: `.gitattributes` — set `text=auto` or `eol=lf` for expect files so git normalizes to LF in the repo. But then on Windows checkout they'd be LF (if eol=lf), and program output CRLF → fc fails on Windows. If `text=auto`, git normalizes to LF in repo but checks out CRLF on Windows — so Windows keeps CRLF, Linux gets LF. That actually solves it on BOTH platforms! Because:\n- On Windows: repo stores LF, checkout gives CRLF (core.autocrlf default true / .gitattributes text=auto), program outputs CRLF, fc compares CRLF vs CRLF → match.\n- On Linux: checkout gives LF (git on Linux doesn't do CRLF conversion by default), program outputs LF, diff compares LF vs LF → match.\n\nWait but the files are already committed with CRLF bytes. Adding `.gitattributes` `*.txt text=auto` (or `* text=auto`) would cause git to normalize. But existing CRLF files in the working tree/index: git would show them as modified (because normalization renormalizes). You'd need `git add --renormalize .` to fix the index. This is a repo-level change — the standard Git way to handle line endings. But the course context: it's a learning project; the mentor's AGENTS.md mentions encoding (CP1251) but not line endings policy explicitly. Actually AGENTS says \".editorconfig\" exists and mentions charset issues.\n\nHmm, but there's a subtlety: the source files are CP1251. git text detection with `text=auto` would treat .cpp/.h as text (fine). But these files are CP1251, not UTF-8 — git's eol handling works on bytes for \\r\\n regardless of encoding, so fine.\n\nHowever, is adding .gitattributes too \"advanced\" for this stage? It's a legit industry practice. But it also changes behavior for ALL text files (source files). If source files are committed with CRLF and someone with autocrlf variations checks them out — could cause churn. \n\nGiven the learning stage and the specific problem (expect files), the pragmatic minimal fix:\n\nOption E: Rewrite the expect files' bytes to LF directly (she asked \"могу ли переписать прямо сами байты?\"). YES — but it only fixes Linux and breaks Windows fc. Because on Windows the program outputs CRLF. So that's NOT a fix by itself.\n\nWait, is that actually true? Let me verify: does the program on Windows output CRLF to a redirected file? The C/C++ programs use std::print/printf writing \\n. When stdout is redirected to a file via cmd, the CRT default text mode translates \\n → \\r\\n. Yes. So output files on Windows have CRLF. This was confirmed earlier in the dialog (from 2026-08-18 log: \"When stdout is redirected to a file, CRT in text mode translates \\n to \\r\\n. So output files have CRLF. The expect files also have CRLF. Good, they match.\").\n\nSo on Windows, expect must be CRLF to match fc. If she converts expect to LF, Windows tests break.\n\nTherefore the correct answer to her question: converting bytes to LF alone will break Windows. The real solution must normalize both sides at comparison time, OR use .gitattributes to make each platform's checkout match its output.\n\nHmm, but wait — actually let me reconsider. There's an alternative: what if the comparison normalizes the RESULT's line endings to match the expect? Or what if we make both sides LF by having the program output LF even on Windows? \n\nActually the cleanest, most course-appropriate fix: a compare exe that strips \\r from both files then byte-compares. This is symmetric, cross-platform, and per §4.9 п.2 it's a test-exe (logic beyond one-liner). It also removes the fc/diff platform variable entirely — one comparison tool for everything. That's elegant: the `$(FC)` variable disappears, replaced by check_contains (or a new `check_equal` mode).\n\nLet me think about what's minimal:\n- Modify tests/check_contains.cpp to optionally do exact compare after stripping \\r? Or add a separate tool `check_files.cpp`?\n- Actually check_contains does substring. For t2/t3/t4 error messages substring is right. For t1_norm and t4_test1-3, we want exact equality after line-ending normalization.\n\nOption: extend check_contains with a flag (argv) for \"exact, ignore \\r\". Or new tool. Keeping one tool with two modes is fine.\n\nBut this is a bigger change than \"rewrite bytes\". Given the user is learning, I should present the tradeoff clearly and let her decide. The question she asked is narrower: \"can I rewrite bytes directly?\" \n\nI must answer: Yes, you can, but rewriting to LF alone is a half-fix — it will pass on Linux but break `$(FC)` on Windows, because on Windows the program writes CRLF to files (CRT text-mode translation), so the comparison needs CRLF on both sides.\n\nThen present options:\n1. Normalize both sides at compare time (check_contains extended, or small exe) — robust cross-platform; FC/diff variable becomes unnecessary.\n2. `.gitattributes` `text=auto` (+ renormalize) — makes git give CRLF on Windows checkout and LF on Linux checkout, matching each platform's output. Minimal code change (none), but adds repo config and needs care with CP1251 source files.\n3. Keep fc/diff but make recipes platform-specific normalization — violates one-liner rule, meh.\n\nActually, let me reconsider option 2 more carefully because it's elegant. With `.gitattributes` containing `* text=auto`:\n- The expect files (and all text) are stored LF in repo (normalized), checked out CRLF on Windows (core.autocrlf or git config), LF on Linux.\n- On Windows: program output CRLF (text mode) → fc vs CRLF expect → match. ✓\n- On Linux: program output LF → diff vs LF expect → match. ✓\n- But: requires `git add --renormalize` to fix existing committed CRLF files, and all other text files (source .cpp/.h CP1251) get normalized too — could cause big diff churn and encoding concerns. Since sources are CP1251, git eol conversion still works (it operates on CRLF bytes, not encoding). But a large renormalization diff is noisy.\n\nGiven the stage (makefile layer, learning), I lean toward option 1 (compare exe that normalizes) as most honest and self-contained, OR a minimal targeted approach.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider what's truly minimal and aligned with the existing design. The `$(FC)` comparisons are for full-file equality of NUMBER outputs (t1_norm, t4 test1-3). These don't have Russian text. The simplest robust approach that requires the least new machinery:\n\nModify `check_contains.cpp` to accept a mode: substring (current) OR exact-compare-with-ignored-CR. Then in the Makefile, replace `$(FC)` recipes with `$(FIND_SUBSTR_CMD)` calling the new mode. The `$(FC)` variable (fc/diff) can then be removed entirely — one less platform variable.\n\nBut hold on — this changes test tool semantics and touches ~6 recipes + 1 tool file. It's a moderate change.\n\nAlternatively the very minimal: since program output on Windows is CRLF and on Linux LF, and expect files are CRLF:\n- Keep expect CRLF. On Linux, before diff, strip CR from the OUTPUT file: `diff out_norm expect` where out_norm = output with \\r removed. On Windows, no normalization needed. But making recipe platform-conditional is messy.\n\nHonestly, the exe-based compare is the cleanest and matches the course's stated approach (§ 4.9 п.2: \"логика длиннее — пишется тестом на C/C++\"). I'll recommend it, but present the byte-rewrite option and its pitfall, plus .gitattributes as the \"git-native\" alternative.\n\nAlso I should double check: are there other expect files that are full-compared via FC besides t1_test1 and t4_test1/2/3? From the Makefile: FC used in t1_cpp_norm, t1_c_norm (401, 423... actually 402/424), and t4_cpp_test1/2/3, t4_c_test1/2/3. Let me verify which expect files. t1_test1 (numbers matrix), t4_test1/2/3 (filtered points). So expect files: t1_test1, t4_test1, t4_test2, t4_test3. Only these four are full-compared. The user said \"везде где в expect-данных есть переносы строк там 0D 0A\" — but only these 4 matter for FC. The t2/t3/t4 error-message expects use check_contains (substring), where CRLF only matters if the expected substring includes a newline. Let me check if any expect used with FIND_SUBSTR contains \\r\\n within the searched substring. Those are usually single-line messages without trailing newline in expect. E.g., t3_empty_case = \"Пустые данные отсутствуют\" (26 bytes, no CRLF per earlier log). t4 radius expects are single-line messages. So substring tests are likely fine. But to be thorough I should verify the byte content of the four FC-affected expect files.\n\nLet me check the four files' bytes (read-only, allowed).\n\nAlso check whether the program output on Windows truly has CRLF — I can verify by looking at an existing output file in build/ if present.\n\nLet me do these read-only checks.\n\nActually, I'm in plan mode; I can run read-only bash. Let me check the four expect files' line endings, and check a build output file.\n\nThen present the plan with the recommendation and ask her which option.\n\nLet me also reconsider: she asked \"я могу как-то перепис