        {
          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "Now this is a very important finding! Let me analyze:\n\nFor t4_test1:\n- expect: `3.000 4.000 5.000` (17 bytes, NO trailing newline)\n- output on Windows: `3.000 4.000 5.000\\r\\n` (19 bytes, WITH CRLF)\n\nThese DON'T match! `fc` compares bytes... wait, but the test passes? Let me think. The t4_cpp_test1 test uses `$(FC)` which on Windows is `fc`. fc on Windows... Hmm, `fc` does line-based comparison and it normalizes? Actually `fc` on Windows has options like `/b` for binary comparison. By default, fc does a comparison that... let me recall. `fc` by default ignores... no, fc compares line by line, and if the only difference is trailing newline at end of file? Actually the file content differs: one has trailing `\\r\\n`, the other doesn't.\n\nWait, but the tests pass (\"всё зелёное\"). So how? Let me reconsider. For t4_test1, expect has no trailing newline, output has `\\r\\n`. fc would report \"no differences encountered\"? Actually fc might treat a missing trailing newline as equal if lines match. Hmm, actually fc on Windows: \"If files differ only in whether the last line has a trailing newline, fc reports no differences\"? I'm not sure.\n\nActually, `fc` (file compare) on Windows does a line-by-line text comparison. It may ignore the trailing newline difference. Let me check. Actually the FC output was redirected to `$(NULLDEV)` so we can't see. The test passes, so fc returns 0.\n\nHmm wait — actually, let me reconsider. The expect file has no trailing newline. The output has CRLF. fc does a text-mode comparison. In text mode, fc might treat the last line without newline as matching a last line with newline. Actually, fc compares lines; a file ending without newline vs with newline — I believe fc reports differences in that case. But the test passes, so either fc returns 0 or the test .ok files are stale (from before changes and make didn't rerun because prerequisites unchanged... no, the .ok depends on input data and expect which are unchanged, and exe unchanged → make sees up-to-date).\n\nWait, actually the .ok files depend on `$(T4_CPP_EXE) $(RUN_CASE) ... $(FIND_SUBSTR_CMD) tests/expect/t4_test1 tests/input_data/t4_test1`. If none of these changed since the .ok was created, make won't rerun the test. So the test result could be stale! But the user said \"сборки и тесты сделала - всё зелёное\". Hmm.\n\nBut this is a plan-mode conversation; the important thing for answering the user's question: **can she rewrite bytes directly?**\n\nThe key issue: the expect files contain CRLF, and on Linux the program outputs LF only. On Windows, the program output to a file via `>` redirection goes through the CRT in text mode, converting `\\n` to `\\r\\n`. So on Windows, output has CRLF. The expect files have CRLF (matches on Windows). On Linux, output has LF, expect has CRLF → `diff` would flag difference.\n\nSo if she just rewrites the expect bytes to LF-only, then on WINDOWS the output would be CRLF but expect LF → `fc` would differ! And on Linux output LF, expect LF → diff matches.\n\nSo the naive \"convert all expect files to LF\" fix breaks Windows. That's the crucial insight to give her.\n\nWait — but let me double check: does the Windows program really output CRLF to the redirected file? The build artifacts show yes: `build\\debug\\t4_filter_cpp\\t4_test1` has `0D 0A`. And t1 too. So on Windows the output files have CRLF.\n\nSo the real solution is NOT to change the stored expect files, but to make the comparison CR-insensitive, OR to normalize both sides at comparison time.\n\nOptions:\n1. **Normalize both sides before comparing** — e.g., run through `tr -d '\\r'` or use a C++ helper that strips CR from both files, or `sed`. But that's per-recipe complexity, and on Windows there's no `tr`. Could use `powershell` but that's heavy. Better: extend `check_contains` or create a compare tool that strips `\\r` from both sides then compares. Actually check_contains does substring matching, which is inherently CRLF-insensitive for substring (if the substring doesn't include the newline). But for the full-file equality tests (t1_norm, t4_test1-3), they use `$(FC)`/`diff`, not check_contains.\n\nWait, t1_norm uses FC. t4 test1-3 use FC. t2_norm uses FIND_SUBSTR. t3 uses FIND_SUBSTR.\n\nSo for the FC-based tests, the comparison is byte-exact (fc) or line-based (diff). On Windows fc compares with CRLF normalization? Let me think about fc. Actually `fc` on Windows does text comparison and is somewhat tolerant? I'm not 100% sure. But the evidence: expect has no trailing newline for t4, output has trailing CRLF, and the test passes on Windows. So fc must be treating these as equal (ignoring trailing newline). Actually fc compares line by line, and a trailing newline difference at EOF is often ignored by fc. Hmm, but CRLF inside — both have CRLF for interior lines (t1 both have CRLF interior). t4 single line: expect no newline, output CRLF → fc ignores trailing newline difference → equal. OK so on Windows fc works.\n\nOn Linux, `diff` is strict: CRLF vs LF differ on every line. So t1_norm and t4 test1-3 would fail on Linux.\n\nSo the robust fix: make the comparison CR-insensitive on both platforms. Best approach within current stack: extend `check_contains` (FIND_SUBSTR tool) to do an equality/contains comparison after normalizing line endings, OR create a small C++ tool `check_equal` that strips `\\r` from both files and compares. Then use it for t1_norm and t4 test1-3 instead of `$(FC)`.\n\nActually simpler: check_contains already reads both files as bytes and checks substring. For full-file equality with CR-normalization, we could add a mode. But that changes the tool semantics.\n\nAlternatively, keep fc/diff but normalize the program output before comparing: pipe output through a CR-stripper. On Windows, cmd: no `tr`. Could use `findstr /V` hmm no. Could use PowerShell one-liner but that's heavy and per AGENTS rules \"логика длиннее однострочника — это тест-exe\". A one-liner PowerShell might be OK but adds platform branch.\n\nThe cleanest per course policy (§4.9 п.2: \"логика длиннее однострочника — пишется тестом на C/C++\"): a small compare exe that normalizes CR. This is the right architectural answer.\n\nSo my answer to \"я могу как-то переписать прямо сами байты?\":\n- Yes, you can rewrite bytes, e.g., with PowerShell: read bytes, strip 0x0D. But NAIVE conversion to LF-only would break Windows fc tests, because on Windows the program writes CRLF to the redirected file (CRT text mode). So the expect must match the platform's output, or the comparison must be CR-insensitive.\n\nWait, actually — is that fully right? On Windows, `>` redirection in cmd opens the file in text mode for the child's stdout, and the CRT converts \\n → \\r\\n. Yes, confirmed by artifacts.\n\nSo the right fix: compare in a CR-insensitive way. Options for the plan:\nA. Extend check_contains.cpp to support exact equality after stripping '\\r' (and optionally '\\n' normalization). Then switch t1_norm and t4 test1-3 from `$(FC)` to this tool.\nB. Add a dedicated `check_equal` tool (or `cmp`-like) that strips CR. Same idea, separate binary.\nC. Keep fc on Windows and diff with `tr -d '\\r'` on Linux — but that's platform branch in recipes, less clean, and the output file still has CRLF on Windows so fc is fine; on Linux `diff <(tr -d '\\r' < out) <(tr -d '\\r' < expect)` — process substitution is bash-specific, not sh-portable. Could do `tr -d '\\r' < out > out.norm && diff out.norm expect`. That's multiple steps — beyond one-liner, against policy.\n\nSo recommend A/B: extend the existing tool. Since check_contains is \"contains substring\", and t2/t3 use contains semantics, and t1/t4 need equality semantics — maybe add a mode flag, or add a new small tool `check_equals.cpp`. Given AGENTS \"одна идиома\"... Adding a second tiny tool is consistent with existing pattern (run_case, check_contains). Actually, cleaner: make check_contains support both: argv form `check_contains file1 file2` (substring) and maybe `check_contains --equal file1 file2`? Hmm, changes existing callers.\n\nActually simplest robust: a NEW tool `check_equals.cpp` that strips `\\r` from both and requires exact equality, returns 0/1/74. Then t1_norm and t4 test1-3 use `$(FIND_EQUALS)`. This keeps check_contains semantics unchanged.\n\nAlternatively normalize the expect files to LF AND change the comparison for Windows. But Windows output CRLF → expect LF → fc fails. Unless the tool strips CR.\n\nSo the normalization must happen at comparison time, not by rewriting stored bytes. Rewriting bytes to LF only helps Linux but breaks Windows. That's the key answer to her question.\n\nBUT — there's a subtlety: does fc on Windows actually pass currently? She said tests are green. t4_test1: expect 17 bytes no newline, output 19 bytes with CRLF. fc presumably returns 0. Let me not over-verify; the point stands.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider whether rewriting bytes could work if done smartly: keep the stored expect files as-is (CRLF), and on Linux only, strip CR from the program output before diff, OR strip CR from the expect before diff. Since Linux has `tr`, a one-liner `tr -d '\\r' < out | diff - <(tr -d '\\r' < expect)`? Process substitution needs bash. `sh` may not have `<()`. Could write: `tr -d '\\r' < out > out.tmp; tr -d '\\r' < expect > exp.tmp; diff out.tmp exp.tmp`. Multi-line recipe — beyond one-liner policy. Hmm, but it could be a single shell line with `&&`: `tr -d '\\r' < a > a.tmp && tr -d '\\r' < b > b.tmp && diff a.tmp b.tmp`. That's a one-liner with &&. But on Windows, `tr` doesn't exist. So it needs ifeq branch: Windows uses `$(FC)` (works), Linux uses the tr-chain. That's acceptable per policy (portable single-line through variables). Actually it becomes a recipe with platform differences — the FC variable is already platform-split (fc vs diff). Could define a `NORMALIZE_AND_COMPARE` variable... getting complicated.\n\nCleanest per course: a small exe. I'll recommend that, but present options and let her choose.\n\nAlso she asked specifically: \"я могу как-то переписать прямо сами байты?\" — answer: yes, technically (PowerShell bytes), but it's the WRONG fix because it trades Linux-pass for Windows-fail. Explain why (CRT text mode on Windows → output CRLF). The real fix is CR-insensitive comparison.\n\nLet me also verify t1: output 78 bytes (3 CRLF), expect 76 bytes (2 CRLF). Wait — output has 3 CRLF (3 lines), expect has 2 CRLF (76 bytes, 2 newlines). But they should be equal for the test to pass! Output: 3 lines each ending CRLF. Expect: 2 CRLF means... last line no trailing newline. So expect = 3 lines, last without trailing newline (76 bytes), output = 3 lines, last with CRLF (78 bytes). fc ignores trailing newline → passes on Windows. diff on Linux: first two lines identical (CRLF vs CRLF? no wait — on Linux the output would be LF-only, expect is CRLF). \n\nOn Linux: program output LF-only (no CR translation on Linux). Expect has CRLF. diff sees `0.000 ... \\n` vs `0.000 ... \\r\\n` → every line differs → fails.\n\nSo yes, confirm the analysis.\n\nNow, plan options:\nOption A (recommended): add small `tests/check_equals.cpp` that strips `\\r` from both files and does exact byte comparison; build it like run_case/check_contains (platform-split rule); replace `$(FC)` usages in t1_norm (2 rules) and t4 test1-3 (6 rules) with `$(FIND_EQUALS)`; keep expect files as-is (CRLF in repo). This is CR-insensitive and works on both platforms, and aligns with \"тест-exe\" policy.\n\nWait, but t1_norm and t4 test1-3 currently pass on Windows via fc. Switching them to check_equals is consistent and removes the fc dependency entirely (then `$(FC)` variable can be removed). Actually FC is used only in t1_norm and t4 test1-3 (both c and cpp) = 8 usages. If we replace all, `$(FC)` becomes unused → can remove the variable. Clean.\n\nOption B: keep fc/diff but make comparison CR-insensitive via a platform-split one-liner. More fragile.\n\nOption C: rewrite expect bytes to LF and on Windows strip CR from output too — more moving parts, no.\n\nI'll recommend A. Need to check check_contains semantics to mimic style. check_contains reads bytes, does substring. check_equals would read both, strip \\r, compare equal. Exit codes: 0 equal, 1 differ, 74 io fail (matches its style: returns r1.error() which is 1 for open fail... actually it returns 1 on open fail; AGENTS said io_fail 74 for tools earlier? She changed to 1 for all helper tools per journal 2026-08-13 \"во всех служебных задачах код любой ошибки = 1\"). So new tool: return 1 on any error. Fine.\n\nActually, simpler and reusing existing: could extend check_contains with an \"--equal\" mode. But separate tool is clearer and matches existing two-tool pattern (run_case, check_contains). Hmm, but \"повторила дважды — вынеси\" — the two tools share file-reading logic (file_to_bytes_string). A third tool would repeat that. Could refactor... that's scope creep. For plan, propose: add mode to check_contains (one tool, two modes) OR new tool. Let me propose extending check_contains since it already reads files; add argv mode. Actually check_contains argv is (file1 file2) with substring semantics. Changing semantics per flag complicates. New tool with duplicated file-reading (few lines) is acceptable at this scale; but §4.1 п.9 \"повторила дважды — вынеси\" — reading code would be in 2 tools (check_contains, check_equals). If a third appears, refactor. Two is borderline.\n\nAlternative cleaner: create `tests/check_files.cpp` that does equality with CR-strip, and it's fine.\n\nHmm, actually, maybe simplest: since FC is only used where output should equal expect EXACTLY (modulo trailing newline), and check_contains already does \"expected is substring of result\", we could just switch t1_norm/t4 test1-3 to check_contains with expect content... but t4 expect \"3.000 4.000 5.000\" is substring of result \"3.000 4.000 5.000\\r\\n\" → check_contains would pass! And t1 expect (full matrix, no trailing newline) is substring of result (matrix + trailing newline) → passes! So actually, converting the FC-based tests to FIND_SUBSTR (check_contains) would WORK and is CR-insensitive... BUT it's weaker: substring means a result with extra garbage could pass. For t1 matrix, expect is the whole matrix; a program outputting extra lines would still pass (substring). That weakens the test. Better exact equality.\n\nAlso for t4_test3 (empty expect): check_contains has special handling: if expected empty and result empty → 0; if expected empty and result non-empty → 1. That works for empty-output case. OK.\n\nBut exact equality is stronger. For t4 test1-3, output should be EXACTLY the filtered points, one per line. Substring test would allow extra lines. For task 4 correctness, exact is better. So recommend exact-equality tool with CR-strip.\n\nSo final plan:\n1. Add `tests/check_files.cpp` (or extend check_contains with an equality mode) — exact byte equality after removing `\\r` from both files. Return 0/1/1 (io fail→1).\n2. Add build rule for it (platform-split, mirroring run_case/check_contains) + variable `FIND_EQUALS` (and `_CMD` variant).\n3. Replace `$(FC)` in t1_cpp_norm, t1_c_norm, t4_cpp_test1/2/3, t4_c_test1/2/3 with `$(FIND_EQUALS)`/`$(FIND_EQUALS_CMD)`.\n4. Remove `$(FC)` variable (fc/diff) — no longer used.\n5. Leave expect files as-is (CRLF fine now).\n\nActually wait — do we even need to strip \\r in the tool if expect is substring-style? For equality: on Windows output has CRLF, expect has CRLF → equal (both have CRLF; but trailing newline mismatch: output t4 has CRLF, expect no trailing → after stripping \\r: output \"x\\n\", expect \"x\" → still differ on trailing newline!). Hmm. So equality must also handle trailing newline difference. \n\nIf we strip `\\r` only: output t4 = \"3.000 4.000 5.000\\n\", expect = \"3.000 4.000 5.000\". Not equal (trailing \\n). So need to strip trailing newline too, or normalize. Better: strip `\\r` then strip trailing `\\n` from both, or compare after trimming trailing whitespace/newlines. Simplest robust: compare after removing all `\\r` and a single trailing `\\n`. Or: strip `\\r`, and also treat missing/extra trailing `\\n` as equal.\n\nHmm, this is getting into design. The cleanest semantics: normalize line endings (convert \\r\\n → \\n), then also allow the last line's trailing newline to be optional. Actually simplest: after removing \\r, remove a trailing \\n from both strings, then compare. On Linux output: \"...\\n\" → remove trailing \\n → \"matrix\" ; expect CRLF stripped → \"...\\r\\n\"→\"...\\n\"→ remove trailing \\n → \"matrix\". Equal. t4: output \"...\\n\" → \"...\"; expect \"...\" → equal. \n\nBut what if result legitimately ends without newline on Linux and expect has it? Both get normalized. OK.\n\nFor multi-line: t1 has 3 lines; both normalized same way → equal.\n\nSo tool: read both as bytes; build normalized string = remove all bytes 0x0D; then if ends with 0x0A remove it; compare equal.\n\nAlternatively simpler: just compare with `\\r` and trailing `\\n` removed. Good.\n\nAlternatively — a much simpler idea: don't add a tool. Instead, make the compare via check_contains (substring) which already works, accepting slightly weaker test. But task says exact compare desirable. I'll present both and let her decide, but recommend the exact tool.\n\nActually, let me reconsider option: keep fc/diff but normalize on the fly only for Linux, and keep fc on Windows. On Windows fc passes currently (even with trailing-newline diff, fc tolerates). So only Linux branch needs `tr -d '\\r'`. Linux `diff` recipe becomes: `diff <(tr -d '\\r' < out) <(tr -d '\\r' < exp)`. But `<()` is bash; make uses `/bin/sh` which on Ubuntu is dash → no process substitution. So `tr -d '\\r' < out > out.tmp && tr -d '\\r' < exp > exp.tmp && diff out.tmp exp.tmp` — a one-liner with && (allowed: \"логика длиннее однострочника — это тест-exe\", this IS essentially one command line though multi-command). Hmm, borderline. It's per-recipe platform branch: could define variable `FC_RUN` = `fc ...` on Windows / `tr-chain` on Linux. Getting messy. The exe-tool is cleaner.\n\nI'll recommend the exe tool. Present plan, ask her choice.\n\nGiven plan mode, I must present plan and not edit. Let me write concise answer.\n\nLet me also confirm check_contains.cpp content for style (already read earlier). It uses file_to_bytes_string, returns 1 on open fail. Good.\n\nI'll propose adding a mode to check_contains actually to avoid duplicating file-reading — \"повторила дважды\". Check_contains could accept an extra optional arg `--equal`. But it changes existing usage. Adding optional arg: `check_contains file1 file2 [--equal]`. In equal mode: strip \\r and trailing \\n, compare equal. Existing calls unaffected. That's DRY. But mixing modes in one tool with flag is fine and avoids duplication. Hmm. Or new tool duplicating ~15 lines. Given AGENTS emphasis on not duplicating, extending check_contains is more idiomatic per their rules.\n\nActually — the semantics of the two modes differ (substring vs equality), but they share \"read file to bytes\" and \"print diagnostic on mismatch\". Could refactor check_contains into shared helpers... scope. \n\nDecision for plan: propose extending `check_contains` with an equality mode (optional third arg or a mode via argv[1]?). Actually simplest: keep check_contains as-is for substring tests, and create check_equal. The duplicated part is tiny (file read, ~10 lines). But rule §4.1 п.9 says if logic repeated twice → extract. Two tools = 2 repetitions. Marginal. \n\nAlternatively — even simpler and zero new code: use `$(FC)` on both platforms but ensure the OUTPUT also matches. The mismatch is trailing newline + CRLF. What if we just make expect files match what Windows produces (they already do CRLF; but t4 expect has NO trailing newline while output HAS trailing CRLF — yet fc passes!). On Linux, output has trailing LF and CRLF→LF. If we convert expect files to LF (strip \\r), then:\n- Linux: output LF (with trailing \\n), expect LF → but trailing newline: output has trailing \\n, expect t4 has none → diff would flag? diff compares lines; a trailing newline difference: diff may report \"\\ No newline at end of file\". So would fail too unless expect also gets trailing newline.\n\nThis is why a normalization tool is the robust answer. OK, go with tool recommendation.\n\nLet me present. Also answer her literal question: \"могу ли я как-то переписать прямо сами байты?\" Yes — PowerShell byte manipulation is possible, but explain that rewriting bytes is not the right fix because Windows output is CRLF (CRT text mode), so LF-only expects would break fc on Windows. The correct fix is CR-insensitive comparison.\n\nI should present this clearly and ask decision. Keep it focused.",
