          "text": "The user asks a very good technical question: instead of building a comparison utility (their own fc), could we make the CRT output stream binary (byte-oriented) instead of text mode, so that CRLF translation doesn't happen at all — i.e., the program writes LF always, and then the etalon could be LF too.\n\nThis is a real, legitimate design question. Let me think carefully.\n\nOn Windows, the C runtime `stdout` FILE* defaults to text mode. When redirected to a file, `\\n` → `\\r\\n`. On Linux there's no such translation. So:\n- Windows: output file has CRLF\n- Linux: output file has LF\n\nIf we set stdout to binary mode (`_setmode(_fileno(stdout), _O_BINARY)` on Windows), then `\\n` stays `\\n`, output file has LF on both platforms. Then etalon in LF works everywhere.\n\nBUT — there are important caveats:\n\n1. **It's a Windows-only call.** `_setmode`/`_O_BINARY` is MSVC-specific (in `<fcntl.h>` and `<io.h>`). On Linux it doesn't exist. So it needs `#ifdef _WIN32` guards in every main.cpp. That's 8 programs (t1-t4 C and C++) or... actually the programs are the ones whose output gets compared (t1, t3, t4 — stdout). t2 prints text to stdout too. Actually all four tasks print to stdout. So 8 mains would need the call.\n\n2. **It changes ALL output behavior**, not just tests. If someone runs the program interactively and redirects to file, they'd get LF only. That's actually fine and arguably more predictable. But on Windows console, binary mode may change behavior with console output? Actually `_setmode(_fileno(stdout), _O_BINARY)` when stdout is a console — binary mode on console can be problematic (no CR translation at all, which for interactive terminal you actually want). Hmm. Actually for console output, text mode \\n→\\r\\n is what makes terminals render properly. In binary mode writing \\n to a console might not produce line breaks... Actually on Windows console, writing LF in binary mode does move to next line (the console interprets LF). Let me think. In binary mode, no translation happens: \\n is written as-is (0x0A). The console handles 0x0A as line feed (moves down but not carriage return?). Actually Windows console treats LF (0x0A) as line feed only, CR (0x0D) as carriage return. In text mode, CRT converts \\n to \\r\\n so console shows proper new line at column 0. In binary mode writing just \\n gives \"down one line, same column\" — ugly but tolerable for simple output.\n\n   So unconditionally setting binary mode could make interactive console output slightly off. But for this project, the programs are run via redirection in tests, and interactively they print few lines. It's a real consideration though.\n\n3. **stderr too?** The diagnostic messages go to stderr. In tests, stderr is redirected to files and compared via FIND_SUBSTR (substring), which is CR-insensitive effectively (substring match of expected text). Actually check_contains does `result.find(expected)` — if expected has no CRLF and result has CRLF, the substring still matches because expected is a contiguous byte sequence within result. So stderr is fine either way.\n\n4. **Is binary mode \"the right fix\"?** The tradeoff:\n   - Pros: programs produce identical bytes on both platforms → etalons can be LF-only → no need for a compare utility. Single source of truth, portable etalons.\n   - Cons: touches all 8 main files with `#ifdef _WIN32` + includes (`<fcntl.h>`, `<io.h>`), adds platform-specific code into the programs themselves (pollutes the \"pure\" program logic with test/OS plumbing), and changes console behavior subtly.\n\n5. **Alternative view — the philosophy.** The task programs are supposed to be pure console tools. Making stdout binary is a legitimate, known technique (`_setmode(_fileno(stdout), _O_BINARY)` is a documented MSVC idiom, e.g., for outputting UTF-8 bytes or binary data). Many real programs do this. It's actually arguably cleaner because it makes output deterministic across platforms — which is exactly what you want for diff-based testing.\n\n6. **But the etalon/CRLF problem also exists at the source level**: the etalons currently have CRLF because they were *generated* on Windows by redirecting output. If we switch to binary mode, we'd need to regenerate/normalize all etalons to LF. So we still touch the etalon files.\n\n7. **There's a subtlety with the tests themselves**: the Makefile tests run `prog > file`. With binary stdout, `file` gets LF. The compare utility (check_equal) or diff then compares against LF etalon. Fine.\n\n8. **Also stdin?** For t2/t4 the program reads stdin. In text mode on Windows, reading a CRLF file gives you \\n (CR removed). In binary mode you'd get \\r\\n. t1-t4 parse input via `std::cin`/`getline`/`scanf` — text-mode stdin stripping CR is usually *helpful*. If we set stdin to binary, we might break input parsing (the `\\r` at end of lines would break parsing — e.g., t2 reads a name via getline; if CR is retained, name becomes \"звезда\\r\"). So we should NOT touch stdin. Only stdout. Actually the question is only about output. So `_setmode(_fileno(stdout), _O_BINARY)` only. Input stays text mode. Good — but worth noting explicitly, because a naive \"make it binary\" on both stdin/stdout would break input.\n\n9. **std::print/std::cout on Windows with MSVC**: `std::print` goes through stdio/ostream; text-mode translation applies at the CRT FILE* level. So binary mode on stdout affects std::print output too. Yes.\n\nNow — which is better for the course/learning context?\n\nLet me weigh:\n- The compare utility (check_equal) keeps programs pure and puts platform logic in the test tooling. It's \"own fc\". It also handles the trailing-newline difference generically.\n- Binary stdout makes programs platform-deterministic but injects `_WIN32`-specific code into all 8 mains, plus changes console UX slightly, plus etalons still need normalization, plus needs to be careful not to touch stdin.\n\nActually there's an even deeper point: even with binary stdout, the etalon files still have CRLF in the repo (they were captured on Windows). So you STILL need to normalize the etalons to LF. So the \"no etalon change\" benefit is illusory — either way you normalize etalons to LF. Wait: with check_equal utility, you DON'T need to normalize etalons (the utility strips CR from both sides). With binary stdout, you DO need to normalize etalons (because output is LF and etalon must be LF to diff cleanly — or you still use the utility). \n\nSo actually the two are complementary, not competing:\n- binary stdout: makes program output deterministic (LF on both platforms)\n- check_equal: makes comparison robust to line-ending differences (works whether output is CRLF or LF)\n\nGiven the etalon files are already CRLF and committed, and there's a real question of \"which is less code and more robust\", I think the honest answer is:\n\nThe utility approach is more robust and self-contained: it doesn't require touching 8 program files, doesn't change console behavior, and doesn't require normalizing committed etalons (though normalizing is nice anyway). The binary-mode approach is a legitimate alternative that makes outputs canonical, but it spreads `_WIN32` guards into every program and has the stdin gotcha.\n\nBut the user's idea is actually a really good one in a different framing: if stdout were binary, then the *source of truth* for outputs is canonical LF, and the whole class of CRLF problems disappears at the root rather than being patched at comparison time. For a teaching project about \"layers\", this is a nice thing to understand. But I should be critical and point out the downsides.\n\nLet me also mention: there's `std::ios::sync_with_stdio` and `std::cout` — but the simplest is `_setmode`. Also on C side, `setvbuf` doesn't control translation; translation is controlled by the mode of the FILE. For C: `_setmode(_fileno(stdout), _O_BINARY)` too.\n\nAlso: another subtle thing — when stdout is a pipe or console vs file, the default mode: CRT sets stdout to text mode always unless... Actually stdout is always text mode by default in MSVC CRT regardless of where it points. So redirecting to file gives CRLF.\n\nWait, one more: does MSVC text mode actually do \\n→\\r\\n on redirect? Yes — confirmed by the user's own byte dumps: output files have 0D 0A.\n\nNow let me also address: \"идея с утилитой хороша, это свой fc\" — yes, confirm. And answer the binary-mode question honestly: possible, but I'd argue the tradeoff isn't worth it for this project; the utility is the better structural fit. But present the tradeoff clearly and let her decide. Actually — let me think about which I'd recommend.\n\nArguments for binary stdout:\n1. Canonical output: program behaves identically on both OSes. This is genuinely valuable — it means etalons are portable, CI can be one etalon set.\n2. Removes the need for CR-stripping in comparison forever.\n3. It's a documented real-world idiom.\n\nArguments against (for THIS course stage):\n1. Touches 8 programs with platform-specific plumbing — the \"pure geometry logic\" gets polluted with OS concerns. In the course's philosophy (layers), the OS-specific bits are supposed to be isolated (like WCMD in make, _WIN32 in headers). Spreading `_setmode` into every main is the opposite of isolation.\n2. stdin gotcha — must be careful to only set stdout.\n3. Console UX: in binary mode, console output loses \\r — lines \"staircase\" visually when run interactively (LF without CR on Windows console moves down but not to column 0). Minor but real.\n4. You still have to normalize the committed CRLF etalons anyway (they're CRLF now). So it doesn't save the etalon normalization work.\n5. check_equal is only ~30 lines, in one place, uses existing pattern (check_contains), and makes the comparison robust regardless of platform translation — including the trailing-newline case that binary mode does NOT solve (binary mode doesn't make the program emit a trailing newline consistently; actually it would be LF either way... the trailing newline issue is separate: etalon t1_test1 has no trailing newline, program emits one; fc ignores it, diff would flag it; check_equal strips it).\n\nSo actually: binary mode solves CRLF but does NOT solve the trailing-newline-vs-etalon mismatch (t1_test1 etalon ends without newline while program emits one; on Linux diff would flag \"\\ No newline at end of file\"). So even with binary mode you'd need either etalon regeneration or the normalize-in-comparison. check_equal handles both CRLF and trailing newline in one place. That's a strong argument.\n\nLet me verify the trailing newline claim: build/debug/t1_.../t1_test1 is 78 bytes ending 0D 0A (3 CRLF). Etalon is 76 bytes, 2 CRLF, last line no trailing newline. fc passed on Windows (user said green). So fc tolerates the difference. diff would not. check_equal with \"strip trailing \\n\" handles it. Binary mode alone does not — the program would still emit a trailing \\n (3 LF) and etalon has 2 LF no trailing. diff would still flag. So you'd need to normalize etalons regardless, or strip trailing newline in comparison.\n\nTherefore: check_equal is strictly more complete. Binary mode is complementary but not sufficient alone.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: could combine binary stdout + LF etalons + diff? Then t1_test1 etalon must be regenerated to include trailing newline (3 LF lines). That means regenerating all etalons on Linux or hand-editing. Doable but touches all etalon files.\n\nThe cleanest minimal change: check_equal utility that normalizes both sides (strip CR, strip trailing newline), keep etalons as-is, replace $(FC) usages. Done. Zero program changes.\n\nI should also mention: if she likes the canonical-output idea, a middle path — set binary mode via the *build* rather than in code? There's no compiler flag to make stdout binary; it's a runtime CRT mode. So it must be in code. No make flag.\n\nAlso worth mentioning `std::print` — no.\n\nNow, the user asked a direct question: \"можно ли сделать выходной поток crt байтовым а не текстовым чтобы не было беды с crlf?\" Answer: yes, `_setmode(_fileno(stdout), _O_BINARY)` on MSVC (needs `<io.h>` and `<fcntl.h>`), `#ifdef _WIN32` guarded, and it DOES eliminate CRLF. But: (a) it must be stdout-only (don't touch stdin or you'll break input parsing); (b) it doesn't fix the trailing-newline etalon mismatch; (c) it spreads _WIN32 code into 8 programs; (d) console rendering slightly degrades (staircase). So for the course's layering philosophy and minimal blast radius, check_equal is the better fit; binary mode is a legit alternative worth knowing.\n\nI'm in plan mode, read-only. I should present the plan and ask her decision. Let me also confirm the check_equal design details.\n\nLet me propose the concrete plan:\n\nOption chosen (pending her input): check_equal utility.\n\nDesign of check_equal.cpp:\n- Reads both files as bytes (reuse pattern from check_contains.cpp).\n- Normalize: remove all 0x0D bytes, then if result ends with 0x0A, remove it (one trailing newline tolerance). Actually better: strip trailing '\\n' from both, and maybe also handle multiple trailing newlines? Keep it simple: strip all trailing CR/LF? Hmm. The etalons and outputs both end with exactly one line break (or none). Simplest robust rule: after removing CR, trim trailing newlines from both, compare. That handles both \"no trailing newline\" etalon and \"with trailing newline\" output.\n- Return 0 if equal, 1 if differ (with diagnostic), 1 (or 74?) if io fail. check_contains returns 1 on io fail. For consistency with course §4.9 exit codes — io fail 74 is defined. check_contains returns r1.error() which is 1. Hmm, per journal she decided all helper tools return 1 on any error. Keep 1.\n\nVariables in Makefile: FIND_EQUAL := $(BINDIR)/tools/check_equal$(EXE_EXT), FIND_EQUAL_CMD (backslash variant). Build rule mirroring run_case/check_contains (Windows: obj+exe in one step; Linux: single). Prereqs use FIND_EQUAL; recipes use FIND_EQUAL_CMD.\n\nReplace $(FC) usages:\n- t1_cpp_norm.ok (401-403): replace FC with FIND_EQUAL_CMD.\n- t1_c_norm.ok (423-425): same.\n- t4_cpp_test1/2/3 (624-639), t4_c_test1/2/3 (695-710): same.\n\nAfter replacement, $(FC) variable becomes unused → remove from both platform blocks (fc / diff).\n\nWait — is FC used anywhere else? Only in those 8 recipes (grep earlier showed FC in t1_norm and t4 test1-3). Yes.\n\nThen optionally: remove FC variable. And document that etalons stay CRLF (fine, since compare normalizes).\n\nAlso: the t4_test3 etalon is empty (0 bytes) — check_equal must handle empty==empty → 0, empty vs non-empty → 1. check_contains already has that logic. Mirror it.\n\nMicro-consideration: `check_equal` reads both files fully into memory — fine for test-scale.\n\nAlso should I propose normalizing the etalons to LF as a bonus? It's optional cleanliness. The utility makes it unnecessary. But there's an argument: if etalons are canonical LF, then the repo is cleaner and diff-based reading (human) is nicer. But it also creates a big diff churn. I'd leave etalons as-is to keep the change minimal; the utility is the single normalization point. Could mention as optional.\n\nLet me also double-check: is there any Windows console issue with check_equal? No, it reads files, no stdout translation involved in its comparisons.\n\nNow — the question of whether binary mode is \"correct answer\" vs utility. I'll present the honest tradeoff and recommend the utility, but I should respect that she's exploring. Per anti-sycophancy rules, I give my real opinion: utility is better for THIS project; binary mode is a real technique but brings more platform spread than it saves, and doesn't cover the trailing-newline case.\n\nLet me also note the stdin pitfall clearly and the console staircase pitfall.\n\nLet me write the answer in Russian, concise, with [ФАКТ]/[МНЕНИЕ] labels and confidence. In plan mode — no edits. End with a question: confirm going with utility (and whether separate file or flag in check_contains), and whether to drop $(FC).\n\nAlso she asked \"по идее это свой fc\" — confirm yes.\n\nOne more: does making stdout binary affect `std::print` on MSVC? std::print writes via stdio; translation applies at CRT level. Yes binary mode makes it emit \\n. Fine.\n\nAlso on C side the equivalent: `_setmode(_fileno(stdout), _O_BINARY)` works the same (io.h). Same call.\n\nLet me structure the answer:\n\n1. Answer the question: yes, possible, how (code snippet), what it does.\n2. Two caveats that make it incomplete for our case (stdin pitfall; trailing newline not solved; console staircase).\n3. Compare with utility: recommendation.\n4. Concrete plan for check_equal (design + Makefile changes).\n5. Question: confirm, and separate file vs flag.\n\nKeep it tight.",
