output exactly those points. But wait — equality has the CRLF/trailing-newline problem. If we normalize (strip CR, strip trailing newline) in check_equal, then equality on stdout works.\n- For stderr diagnostics — equality would require the *entire* stderr to match the expected. But stderr for error cases may contain the diagnostic plus possibly other content. Actually in these cases the expected file IS the full diagnostic (they redirect 2> to the result file, and the program writes only the diagnostic to stderr). So equality might actually work there too... BUT there's the trailing-newline issue: program writes \"Пустые данные отсутствуют\\n\" to stderr, expected file is \"Пустые данные отсутствуют\" (no newline). Equality with trailing-newline stripping would handle it.\n\nHmm, but wait — would equality break on stderr files that contain CRLF vs the fact that these are single-line? If we normalize in check_equal (strip \\r, strip trailing \\n), then single-line diagnostics would match equality fine.\n\nSo the honest answer: technically you *could* use one tool for everything IF you normalize line endings in it. But there's a semantic difference worth keeping:\n- substring = \"the expected appears somewhere in the result\" — the right check when you don't want to over-constrain (e.g., when result may legitimately contain more, or when you only care about a fragment). This is why check_contains was built originally.\n- equality = \"result is exactly the reference\" — the right check when the program's output should be byte-deterministic.\n\nFor their test suite, stdout tests (t1 matrix, t3 bbox, t4 filter) are deterministic — equality is right (modulo line endings). stderr tests (error diagnostics) — the expected files currently are the full diagnostics, so equality would also pass *now*, but substring is more robust to future formatting changes (e.g., if a diagnostic gets a prefix, substring still passes, equality fails).\n\nActually — I should be careful and honest here. Let me look at what the user is really proposing: \"не сравнивать сами файлы на равенство везде\" — use check_equal everywhere. The question is whether that's a good idea.\n\nArguments for keeping both (substring for diagnostics):\n1. Semantic difference: \"contains\" vs \"equals\". For diagnostics, you often want tolerance (line numbers, OS-specific message bits, extra context). For data output, you want exactness.\n2. If stderr ever contains more than one message or grows, equality breaks even though the key diagnostic is present.\n3. The expected files for error cases were designed as fragments.\n\nArguments for one tool (equality everywhere + normalization):\n1. Simpler — one tool, one variable, less duplication.\n2. If expected files are full diagnostics anyway, equality gives *stronger* tests (catches unexpected extra output in stderr).\n3. Avoids the \"two tools that both read files\" duplication.\n\nActually, let me reconsider what check_contains is used for and whether equality would break any current test. Let me check the hex of the error expect files. Earlier log showed:\n- t3_empty_case expect: \"Пустые данные отсутствуют\" — 26 bytes, no trailing newline\n- t3_not_digit_case: \"Строка 1. Нечисловое данное: 3 2 bam\"\n\nThe program's stderr output would be e.g. \"Строка 1. Нечисловое данное: 3 2 bam\\n\" (with trailing newline). With equality + trailing-newline strip + CR strip, \"Строка 1. Нечисловое данное: 3 2 bam\" == \"Строка 1. Нечисловое данное: 3 2 bam\" → matches. So equality would work for the current tests.\n\nBUT — there's a risk: what if stderr for these cases contains more lines? E.g., t3 might print multiple diagnostic lines? If the program prints exactly one line, fine. Actually the test writes stderr to a file and compares with expected. If the program adds any other output to stderr (debug prints), equality catches it (good for correctness) but substring wouldn't. \n\nHmm, so actually equality is *stronger*. The original reason for substring (from journal): \"находит подстроку, т.к. там могут быть какие-то дополнительные вещи\". Let me recall the actual reason from the dialog logs... In check_contains.cpp comments: \"проверка, что целевой файл содержит подстроку из файла-ожидания\". The reason was encoding flexibility and partial matching.\n\nI think the honest answer is: you *could* unify into one normalized-equality tool and it would currently pass all tests, AND it would be stronger (catches extra stderr content). But the tradeoff: you lose the tolerance that substring gives, and one semantic concept. My recommendation: keep the two distinct because they check different contracts (diagnostic presence vs data exactness), but if she wants to reduce duplication, she could add the equality mode as a flag to the existing check_contains (reusing file-reading). That answers her earlier question too.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider. There IS a real technical reason the two differ in the tests: the FC-based tests (t1_norm, t4 test1-3) compare **stdout data files** — these need equality. The FIND_SUBSTR tests (t2 norm, t3/t4 stderr) — these need... actually let me check what t2_cpp_norm does: it writes program output (stdout) to t2_norm file, then check_contains compares with expect/t2_norm. So that's stdout equality by substring. Hmm — t2 norm stdout is \"Фигура «звезда»: 6 вершин.\\n\". Expected file has... let me check if it has trailing newline. If expected is \"Фигура «звезда»: 6 вершин.\" without trailing newline, substring matches. Equality with strip-trailing-newline also matches. So either works.\n\nSo genuinely, for the current suite, equality-after-normalization would work everywhere. The decision is about semantics and robustness:\n\n- **Keep substring for diagnostics**: reasoning — diagnostics may legitimately gain prefixes/context; you test \"this key message is there\", not \"stderr is exactly this\".\n- **Equality for data output**: stdout should be exactly the reference (extra line = bug).\n\nI'll recommend: two tools (or one tool two modes). Actually, given she asked \"почему не сделать из check_contains check_equal\", I think the cleanest answer that respects her instinct to reduce duplication: **make check_contains support two modes** — substring (default, current) and equality (new, via a flag or second arg). Reuse the file-reading. Then FC tests use the equality mode, diagnostics use substring mode. One tool, one build rule, one variable set, but two call modes. This directly addresses her question.\n\nBut hold on — there's a subtlety about whether to make equality the *only* mode. Let me think about the risk of equality on diagnostics more concretely. Consider t4_radius_empty: program run with no args → stderr gets usage message \"Укажите радиус...\" expected file is that message. If the program also printed to stderr the same message twice or with a trailing explanation — equality fails, substring passes. For a *teaching* project, stronger is arguably better (catches accidental extra output). But it also makes tests brittle to intentional diagnostics expansion (adding e.g. a hint line). \n\nI'll present this as a tradeoff and let her decide, but give a clear recommendation.\n\n**Question 2: input-data CRLF — will it break input on Linux?**\n\nThis is a sharp question. On Linux, the programs read from stdin via `std::cin`/`getline`/`scanf`/`fgets`. The input files (tests/input_data/*) are in the repo. If they contain CRLF:\n- **C++**: `std::cin` with text mode — the `\\r` is NOT automatically stripped by iostreams on Linux (no CRT text-mode translation). On Windows MSVC, the CRT *does* translate CRLF→LF on input (text mode). On Linux, no translation. So a CRLF input file would feed `звезда\\r` as part of the string on Linux! `getline` would give \"звезда\\r\". That WOULD break t2 (name matching / emptiness checks) and potentially number parsing.\n\nWait, but actually — does the program use getline that strips \\r? No, getline stops at \\n, keeping \\r as the last char of the line. On Windows, text-mode stdin translates \\r\\n → \\n, so the program never sees \\r. On Linux, the program would see \\r. This is a REAL cross-platform issue.\n\nLet me check: t2 reads name via getline, then trims whitespace (trim_str trims spaces, maybe not \\r). t3/t4 parse_point parses \"x y z\" — with \\r at end of \"z\", from_chars/sscanf might parse \"5.0\\r\"? sscanf %lf would parse 5.0 and stop at \\r (ok if it stops at \\r as non-numeric, fine). from_chars for double — \"5.0\\r\" parses 5.0, stops at \\r, fine if the parser ignores trailing. Actually from_chars would consume \"5.0\" and leave \\r. So numbers probably fine. But t2 name with \\r — trim_str probably only trims spaces, so \"звезда\\r\" stays with \\r → then comparison or emptiness... Actually t2 just echoes the name back: \"Фигура «звезда\\r»: 6 вершин.\" — the \\r would appear in output → the norm test comparison would fail on Linux! Or might render weirdly.\n\nSo YES — CRLF in input_data files is a real potential problem on Linux. Let me verify the actual bytes of input_data files.\n\nLet me check tests/input_data files for CRLF.\n\nI should check this with a read-only command.\n\nAlso the fix options:\n1. Normalize input files to LF in repo (like expect files).\n2. In check_* tools, no — input goes directly to the program.\n3. Have the programs strip \\r (portable line reading) — but that's touching programs, against isolation principle somewhat, though line-ending-tolerant input reading is actually good practice.\n4. Same normalization approach as outputs.\n\nActually the cleanest: normalize the input_data files to LF, and have the comparison tools normalize. But the program itself is the one consuming input — the repo files must match what the program expects. If we normalize input files to LF, then on Linux program reads LF (fine), on Windows CRT translates CRLF→LF anyway even if... wait, on Windows if the file is LF-only, CRT text mode reads it fine (LF stays LF). So LF input files work on both platforms! CRLF input files break Linux. So: **normalize input_data to LF** is the fix.\n\nLet me verify which input files have CRLF. Also check whether the programs would actually break — the safest is to check what the input files contain and how the programs parse.\n\nLet me run read-only checks:\n1. Check CRLF in all tests/input_data files.\n2. Check CRLF in all tests/expect files.\n\nThen present findings.\n\nActually, I should present this carefully. Let me do the byte checks first.\n\nLet me also reconsider: are there Russian-text input files? t2_input contains \"звезда\" in CP1251 + CRLF probably. That's the dangerous one.\n\nLet me run the checks.",
