        {
          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "Now I have real data. Let me analyze both questions.\n\n**Question 1: Why not make check_contains into check_equal and compare files for equality everywhere?**\n\nThe key difference: check_contains does SUBSTRING matching — it checks that the expected file content is a *substring* of the result file. This is used for stderr diagnostics where the exact output may include more (like \"Строка 1. ...\" line numbers that may vary, or the full diagnostic might have context). Let me think about why substring was chosen.\n\nLooking at usage:\n- t2 norm: checks `Фигура «звезда»: 6 вершин.` is in the output\n- t3/t4 stderr cases: checks diagnostics are present\n\nSubstring is used because:\n1. The stderr output may contain variable parts (line numbers, actual values that differ) — exact equality would be too strict\n2. Actually wait, let me look at the expect files. `t3_not_digit_case` = 36 bytes = \"Строка 1. Нечисловое данное: 3 2 bam\". Hmm, this IS the exact message including line number. So equality might actually work for these too...\n\nActually let me think about WHY substring was chosen over equality originally. From check_contains.cpp comments: \"берёт file1 file2, ищёт подстроку file2 в file1\". The reason for substring: stderr diagnostics may have trailing newlines, and possibly the program output to stderr might contain additional content or the message could be split across lines. Also the CRLF issue: on Windows, output to a redirected file is CRLF, and the expected file might be LF or CRLF — substring is robust to that because the expected content without line endings is found regardless.\n\nActually the real reason: the result file (stderr captured) may contain MORE than just the diagnostic — e.g., for t3 test1 the stdout has data AND we compare... no wait. Let me look at what's compared.\n\nFor t3: `t3_cpp_test1.ok` writes output to `$(BINDIR)/t3_bbox_cpp/t3_test1 2> $(NULLDEV)` — wait no. Let me re-read:\n\n```\n$(TESTDIR)/t3_cpp_test1.ok: ...\n\t$(SET_DLL_PATH) && \\\n        $(RUN_CASE_CMD) \"$(call WCMD,$(T3_CPP_EXE) < tests/input_data/t3_test1 > $(BINDIR)/t3_bbox_cpp/t3_test1 2> $(NULLDEV))\" 0 && \\\n        $(FIND_SUBSTR_CMD) $(call WCMD,$(BINDIR)/t3_bbox_cpp/t3_test1 tests/expect/t3_test1) && $(TOUCH) $@\n```\n\nSo stdout goes to t3_test1 file, then FIND_SUBSTR (check_contains) checks that `tests/expect/t3_test1` content is a substring of the program's stdout. The expected t3_test1 is 216 bytes with 3 CRLF — that's the full report \"N точек, min/max..., центроид, среднее расстояние\" formatted with 3 decimals. And the actual program output... the check is that expected is substring of result. If output formatting matches exactly, substring == equality here essentially (since expected is the full formatted output).\n\nWhy substring and not equality? Because:\n1. Formatting may differ in trailing newline (expected has CRLF, program on Linux outputs LF).\n2. The output might have additional trailing content.\n\nActually the fundamental reason: **substring matching sidesteps the line-ending problem AND the trailing-newline problem** — IF the expected content itself doesn't contain line endings that differ. But as we saw, t1_test1 and t3_test1 expects DO contain CRLF. So substring only works when both sides have matching line endings within the expected fragment.\n\nHmm wait — for t3_test1, expected has 3 CRLF pairs (3 lines, each ending CRLF). On Linux, program output has LF. The expected text with `\\r\\n` would NOT be found as substring in a result with `\\n`! So t3_test1 norm tests would ALSO fail on Linux. Same class of bug as t1/t4.\n\nSo actually the CRLF problem affects BOTH equality and substring when the expected spans multiple lines.\n\nNow question 1 is: why not make everything equality?\n\nThe answer: **the two tools check different contracts**:\n- `check_equal` (new): result == expected exactly (after line-ending normalization) — for program STDOUT data files where output should be byte-identical to a reference.\n- `check_contains` (existing): expected is *present* in result — for STDERR diagnostics where we want to verify \"the message appeared\" without requiring the whole stderr to match exactly (stderr may legitimately contain other content, line numbers may vary, etc.).\n\nConcretely in the Makefile:\n- stdout data tests (t1 norm, t3 norm test1-3, t4 norm test1-3): should be EQUALITY — the output must be exactly the reference.\n- stderr diagnostic tests (t3 empty/not_digit/too_few, t4 all the *_radius_*, symbol_coords, too_few_coords, empty_coords): should be CONTAINS — verify the diagnostic text is present in stderr. The program's stderr for error cases may include the diagnostic plus possibly more context, and we don't want to over-constrain.\n\nWait but do the stderr diagnostics in this project produce more than just the message? Looking at the task spec (§ 4.6): diagnostic to stderr with specific message. For t4 radius errors, the stderr file captured contains just the usage message. Could be equality too. But the DESIGN choice of substring for diagnostics is deliberate: it's more robust — if the program adds context (like a filename or prefix), the test still passes. And critically, it decouples from trailing newline: expected \"Пустые данные отсутствуют\" (no newline) is a substring of result \"Пустые данные отсутствуют\\r\\n\" (with CRLF) regardless of platform.\n\nSo the honest answer to Q1: No, don't make everything equality. Different purposes:\n- stdout data → equality (must be exact)\n- stderr diagnostics → contains (must just appear)\n\nIf you forced equality on stderr diagnostics, you'd fail when:\n1. program writes diagnostic with trailing newline but expected has none (or vice versa) — though normalization could fix\n2. program writes more than one line to stderr\n\nActually the cleaner design might be: check_equal normalizes line endings AND checks equality; keep check_contains for diagnostics. That's exactly the plan A we proposed. \n\nBut there's a subtlety: the user asks \"why not just make check_contains check equality everywhere\". The answer is the semantic difference. If ALL tests could be equality, we'd have one tool. But stderr diagnostics are better as \"contains\" because they're not byte-deterministic contracts.\n\nHmm, but let me reconsider. Are the stderr diagnostics byte-deterministic? Let me look: \"Строка 1. Нечисловое данное: 3 2 bam\" — includes the line number and the bad tokens, which are deterministic for a given input file. t4 radius messages — deterministic. So equality *could* work for the current tests IF line endings were normalized. But it's fragile: if a diagnostic changes, or if the test input changes line numbers, contains is more robust.\n\nThe real architectural answer: **equality is a stronger assertion than contains**. You use equality when the contract is \"output must be exactly this\" (data), contains when the contract is \"this fact must be reported\" (diagnostics). Making everything equality means every future diagnostic change breaks tests even when the message is still there. That's brittle.\n\nAlso there's a practical reason NOT to merge: the check_contains tool is ALSO used in the FC-replaced tests potentially. And merging would mean rewriting tool semantics + all call sites. The cost isn't worth it.\n\n**Question 2: input-data with CRLF — will it break on Linux?**\n\nKey insight: the input files are consumed by the PROGRAM (stdin), not compared. The program reads via `std::cin`/`getline` or `fgets`/`scanf`.\n\nOn Windows: when you do `prog < file`, the CRT opens stdin in text mode → CRLF translated to LF → program sees `\\n` only. Fine.\n\nOn Linux: the program reads the file as-is → sees `\\r\\n`. Now:\n- `std::getline` reads a line including `\\r` at the end? No — getline stops at `\\n`, so the line content ends with `\\r`. So `звезда\\r` instead of `звезда`!\n- `fgets` same: keeps `\\r` before `\\n`.\n- `scanf(\"%lf %lf %lf\")` — whitespace handling: `\\r` is whitespace in C locale, so scanf skips it fine. But wait — does `\\r` count as whitespace for scanf? Yes, `isspace('\\r')` is true, so `%lf` skips it. Numbers parse OK.\n\nSo the CRITICAL breakage is for **string input**: t2 reads a figure name (Russian word). On Linux with CRLF input, the name becomes `звезда\\r`. Then the program outputs `Фигура «звезда\\r»: 6 вершин.` and also the empty-check and trim might behave differently. `is_empty` on `звезда\\r` → not empty, fine, but the OUTPUT contains `\\r` → test comparison fails. Also `\\r` might not be trimmed by trim_str (depends).\n\nFor numeric inputs (t3, t4), `\\r` is whitespace for scanf/from_chars? `std::from_chars` — does it handle `\\r`? from_chars doesn't skip whitespace at all, it parses from the exact position. The parse_point code in geometry.cpp uses from_chars. Let me recall: parse_point parses \"x y z\" — if there's `\\r` at end of the line after z... from_chars for double stops at first non-numeric char, `\\r` would terminate parsing successfully (returns pointer at `\\r`). Actually for the last coordinate z followed by `\\r\\n`, from_chars parses z fine and stops at `\\r`. Then the code checks the remaining... depends on implementation. Could be OK or could fail on the check \"nothing left after\". Need to check.\n\nAlso t1 reads an integer N. `звезда`/numbers via what? t1 reads via `>>` probably (istream) — `\\r` skipped as whitespace, fine.\n\nSo the honest answer: **YES, CRLF in input_data can break t2 (string name) on Linux, and possibly t3/t4 if parsing is strict about trailing content.** Numbers usually tolerate `\\r` as whitespace, but t2's string definitely breaks because `\\r` becomes part of the string.\n\nWait — but actually let me reconsider. The input_data files currently have CRLF only for multi-line ones (t2_input: 1 CRLF, t2_*_vertexes, t3_test2/3, t4_test2). Single-line files have no CRLF (0 LF). t2_input is 9 bytes with 1 CRLF: that's `звезда\\r\\n`? 9 bytes = 6 bytes `звезда` (CP1251, 6 chars) + `\\r\\n` = 8... hmm 9. \"звезда\" in CP1251 is 6 bytes. Plus \\r\\n = 8. But file is 9 bytes. Maybe there's a space? Actually \"звезда\" = з-в-е-з-д-а = 6 bytes. 6+2=8, file is 9. So maybe \"звезда \" with trailing space? Let me not over-analyze; the point stands: CRLF exists in t2 input.\n\nSo the fix for Q2: normalize input_data to LF too (like expect), OR make programs strip `\\r` (bad — platform pollution), OR... Actually the cleanest: since the programs are given stdin, the robust cross-platform approach is that the input files should be LF (so both platforms see identical bytes, since Windows CRT text mode... wait no).\n\nHold on. If input file is LF-only:\n- Windows: `prog < file`, CRT text mode stdin reads LF-only file, translates LF→LF (no-op), program sees `\\n`. Fine.\n- Linux: program sees `\\n`. Fine.\n\nIf input file is CRLF:\n- Windows: CRT translates CRLF→LF, program sees `\\n`. Fine.\n- Linux: program sees `\\r\\n`. t2 breaks.\n\nSo the fix: **store input_data in LF**. Then both platforms work. This is the same class of fix as expect files (LF), BUT with one difference: expect files on Windows are compared by fc — the program's OUTPUT on Windows is CRLF (CRT text mode), so expect must be CRLF for fc to pass... unless we use the check_equal tool that normalizes!\n\nOh wait, this is the crux. Let me think through the whole cross-platform story:\n\n**stdin side (input_data files):**\n- Program reads stdin via CRT (Windows: text mode → CRLF→LF) or raw (Linux: no translation).\n- If input files are LF: both platforms fine.\n- If input files are CRLF: Windows fine (translated), Linux breaks (t2 string).\n- → **input_data should be LF.**\n\n**stdout side (program output → compared):**\n- Windows: program writes `\\n`, CRT text mode → `\\r\\n` in file.\n- Linux: program writes `\\n` → `\\n` in file.\n- So the actual output bytes differ between platforms (CRLF vs LF)!\n- If expect is CRLF: fc on Windows passes (both CRLF), diff on Linux fails (result LF, expect CRLF).\n- If expect is LF: diff on Linux passes, fc on Windows fails (result CRLF, expect LF).\n- → **No single line-ending for expect works with both fc and diff.** This is why we need check_equal with normalization (strip CR + trailing newline on both sides). THEN expect can be any consistent form (LF preferred).\n\nSo the asymmetry:\n- **Input**: LF is safe for both (CRT translation on Windows is one-directional CRLF→LF; a pure LF file is unaffected).\n- **Output/expect**: needs a normalizing comparator (check_equal/check_contains) because Windows adds CRLF that fc can't ignore but diff can't ignore either. Actually fc CAN ignore trailing newline differences but NOT mid-file CRLF vs LF... fc is line-based and compares lines; CRLF vs LF at line ends — fc might tolerate? fc does ignore end-of-line differences I think. But diff doesn't. Hence inconsistency → need our own tool.\n\nWait, actually let me reconsider fc behavior. fc (file compare) in cmd — it's line-oriented and I believe it does handle CRLF/LF differences (it splits on line boundaries). That's why t1_norm passes on Windows now (expect CRLF, output CRLF, both consistent anyway). The FC issue is really only about *trailing newline* on Windows and *everything* on Linux.\n\nOK so let me formulate the answer clearly.\n\n**Q1 answer**: Don't make everything equality. Two contracts:\n- stdout data files → equality (check_equal with normalization)\n- stderr diagnostics → contains (check_contains) — more robust, decouples from exact bytes/trailing newline, verifies \"message present\" not \"stderr exactly equals\"\n\nIf you forced equality everywhere, current diagnostic tests would likely still pass (messages are deterministic) but it's brittle: any extra stderr content, changed line number in message, or added context would fail. Contains is the right contract for \"did it report the error correctly\".\n\nAlso merging tools = one tool with two behaviors → if you merge into equality, you LOSE the contains capability, and stderr tests that currently rely on substring would need their expected files to be exact full stderr. And their expected files are just the message without trailing newline, so equality would fail on trailing newline unless normalized.\n\nActually — hmm, could check_equal with normalization handle contains too? No. They're different. Keep both. That's the answer.\n\n**Q2 answer**: Yes, CRLF in input_data can break Linux, specifically t2 (string name) — `\\r` becomes part of the string. Numbers (scanf) tolerate `\\r` as whitespace, but from_chars-based parsing might or might not. Let me check geometry.cpp from_chars behavior to be precise. Actually I should verify how parse_point handles trailing content.\n\nLet me read the parse_point implementation quickly to give a factual answer about t3/t4.\n\nActually, I'm in plan mode — I can read files. Let me read geometry.cpp and the relevant parse code.",
