          "text": "Now I have all the info. Let me analyze both questions carefully.\n\n**Question 1: Why not make check_contains into check_equal and compare files for equality everywhere?**\n\nKey insight: check_contains does SUBSTRING matching (expected is a substring of result), while the proposed check_equal does FULL-file equality (with normalization).\n\nWhy not use equality everywhere?\n\n- The substring tests are used for **stderr diagnostics** where the expected text is a fragment, and the program's stderr output may include more context (e.g., line number \"Строка 1.\" or values). Actually let me look. The expect files for t3_not_digit_case is \"Строка 1. Нечисловое данное: 3 2 bam\" — that's the full message. Hmm.\n\nActually let me think about what tests use check_contains vs FC:\n- FC (fc/diff): t1_norm, t4 test1-3 — these compare FULL stdout files (matrix output, filtered points).\n- check_contains: t2_norm (stdout \"Фигура «звезда»: 6 вершин.\"), t3 test1-3 (stdout report), t3/t4 stderr diagnostics.\n\nWait — t3 test1-3 use check_contains but compare the stdout report! Let me check: t3_cpp_test1: `$(RUN_CASE) \"prog < input > $(BINDIR)/t3_bbox_cpp/t3_test1 2> $(NULLDEV)\" 0 && $(FIND_SUBSTR_CMD) $(BINDIR)/t3_bbox_cpp/t3_test1 tests/expect/t3_test1`. So it compares the full stdout report with substring semantics.\n\nSo the design intent:\n- **FC / check_equal**: full file equality — for outputs that must match byte-for-byte (matrix, filtered points). Actually these are the outputs where exact equality matters.\n- **check_contains**: substring — for cases where the expected output is a message that may appear among other content, OR where the expected is a fragment of the actual output (diagnostics that include variable parts like line numbers).\n\nThe reason to keep substring for diagnostics: stderr messages may contain variable content (like actual bad values, line numbers). The expect file contains the fixed part. Full equality would fail when the diagnostic includes a variable portion.\n\nBut wait — looking at the t3_not_digit_case expect = \"Строка 1. Нечисловое данное: 3 2 bam\" (36 bytes, no CRLF) — that's the FULL message including line number and value. So equality could work there too, IF normalized.\n\nThe real reason substring is used: **robustness to line endings and to partial content**. The check_contains was originally built to handle the Russian-text matching through byte comparison where the exact full output might differ (e.g. trailing newline, CRLF). Substring sidesteps the line-ending problem — if expected has no newline and result has newline, substring still matches.\n\nSo the honest answer to Q1: You *could* use equality everywhere IF check_equal normalizes line endings (strips CR, trailing newlines). But there's a semantic difference:\n- `check_contains`: \"expected is present in result\" — tolerant, catches partial matches, good for diagnostics where the exact full stderr may vary (line numbers, values).\n- `check_equal`: \"result is exactly expected\" — strict, correct for data outputs (matrix, point lists) where we want to assert the whole output.\n\nWhy not collapse into one equality tool?\n1. **Different contract**: For diagnostics you often want \"message appeared\", not \"stderr byte-identical\". If the program adds context to stderr later (e.g., a second diagnostic line), equality breaks but substring survives. For data output, you want strict equality — substring would miss an extra line.\n2. **Robustness to encoding/newlines**: substring matching doesn't care about trailing newline or CRLF in the *result* — only the expected must appear. This is why check_contains was built in the first place (Russian text, CP1251, CRLF issues).\n\nBut actually — is there redundancy? For t3 test1-3, they use substring on the full stdout report. That's semantically \"the report must contain this exact block\". If the program prints extra lines, substring still passes. Is that intended? For a report, maybe you want exact. Hmm. But changing it is out of scope; the question is about *why not make one equality tool*.\n\nThe cleanest answer: keep both because they encode different assertions. But we could have ONE binary with two modes (equality vs contains) to avoid two source files. That's the earlier question she asked (separate file vs flag). \n\nActually, let me reconsider. The proposed design: check_equal for FC-replaced tests (t1_norm, t4 test1-3), check_contains stays for diagnostics. Could we use check_equal for t3 test1-3 too? Those are full-report outputs. If normalized equality, they'd work and be stricter. But t3 test1-3 expected files have CRLF (3 CRLF each)! So equality without normalization would fail on Linux. With normalization it'd work.\n\nSo the answer to Q1: \n- Yes, in principle one equality tool could replace both FC and (for full-file cases) substring. \n- BUT substring tests for *stderr diagnostics* should stay as substring: the expected message is a fragment and stderr may contain more. Making them equality = making the test brittle.\n- If we want to reduce tool count, make ONE binary `check_files` with two modes: `--contains` (current) and `--equal` (normalized equality). One build rule, one variable set, two call modes.\n\nThat's the balanced recommendation.\n\n**Question 2: input-data with CRLF — will it break Linux input?**\n\nFacts from the scan:\n- Multi-line input files with CRLF: t2_input (1 CRLF), t2_empty_vertexes (1), t2_fractional_vertexes (1), t2_nan_vertexes (1), t2_negative_vertexes (1), t3_test2 (2), t3_test3 (3), t4_test2 (2).\n- Single-line no-LF files: most t1/t4, t2_eof_vertexes etc.\n\nHow does the program read input on Linux?\n- t2 uses `std::getline(std::cin, name)` — on Windows, CRT in text mode strips `\\r` from CRLF so name = \"звезда\" (no CR). On Linux, getline keeps `\\r`! So name = \"звезда\\r\". Then `trim_str(name)` — does it trim `\\r`? trim_str likely trims whitespace. `\\r` is whitespace (isspace('\\r') = true). If trim_str uses isspace or checks '\\r', it'd trim. Let me check string_utils.cpp trim_str. \n\nActually I should check trim_str. If it trims isspace, then \"\\r\" is trimmed → OK. If it only trims ' ' (space), then \\r remains → output would be \"Фигура «звезда\\r»: ...\" which breaks the norm test comparison.\n\nLet me check string_utils.cpp.\n\nFor t3/t4: parse_point uses `std::isspace(*ptr_start)` to skip whitespace, and `\\r` IS isspace. So for point parsing, `\\r` at end of line is skipped as whitespace → OK on Linux. The last coordinate parsed, then ptr points at `\\r` or `\\n`, and the check `!isspace(*ptr) && *ptr != '\\0'` — `\\r` is isspace → OK. So parse_point tolerates CRLF.\n\nWait, but the line reading: how does t3 read lines? Via getline into string, then parse_point(string_view). getline keeps `\\r` at end on Linux → parse_point sees \"x y z\\r\" → after parsing z, ptr at '\\r', isspace → OK. So t3/t4 input CRLF is TOLERATED on Linux. \n\nFor t2: name line \"звезда\\r\" → getline gives \"звезда\\r\" → trim_str. Need to check trim_str. And vertexes line \"6\\r\" → from_chars parses int: from_chars for int stops at `\\r` (not digit) → ptr != end → error? Let's see: from_chars(vertexes.data(), ..., V) with \"6\\r\" → parses 6, ptr points at '\\r'. The check: `ptr != vertexes.data() + vertexes.size()` → ptr points at '\\r', not at end → condition true → data error! So **t2 vertexes line with CRLF would break on Linux** because from_chars doesn't skip trailing whitespace, and trim_str on vertexes — does it trim trailing `\\r`? If trim_str trims whitespace including \\r, then vertexes becomes \"6\" before from_chars → OK.\n\nSo it hinges on trim_str. Let me check string_utils.cpp/.hpp trim_str implementation. From earlier read of string_utils.hpp: `COMMON_API void trim_str(std::string& s);`. Need the impl.\n\nLet me read string_utils.cpp.\n\nActually, the key insight for Q2: **input CRLF is only a problem if the program doesn't tolerate trailing \\r**. t3/t4 parse_point uses isspace → tolerates. t2 depends on trim_str. If trim_str uses isspace (C-style) or std::isspace, \\r is trimmed → tolerates. If it manually checks ' ' and '\\t', \\r survives → breaks.\n\nAlso note: the Windows CRT text mode strips \\r on input, so on Windows the program never sees \\r. On Linux it does. So the CRLF in input is a real cross-platform asymmetry, just like output.\n\nThe robust fix for input: normalize input_data to LF (like we discussed for expect). Then both platforms see identical bytes. OR make programs strip \\r — but that's polluting programs with platform concerns (the mentor's isolation principle). Better: input files in LF.\n\nActually wait — even simpler and more correct: if the repo stores input in LF, Windows CRT text mode passes LF through unchanged (no CR to strip), Linux reads LF natively. Both platforms identical. So: **normalize all input_data and expect to LF** is the consistent policy.\n\nSo the answer to Q2: YES it can break, but let me verify which cases actually break by checking trim_str. And then the fix: normalize input files to LF.\n\nLet me read string_utils.cpp to check trim_str.\n\nAlso I should double check: are there input files whose LAST line lacks a newline (like t2_eof_vertexes 13 bytes no LF)? That's a test case for EOF at vertexes, intended. Fine.\n\nLet me read string_utils.cpp.",
