 \n\n  Wait, but the check at line 34: after parsing j-th, it checks `ec != errc || (!isspace(*ptr) && *ptr != '\\0')`. For the last coordinate (z), ptr points at '\\r', isspace true → passes. Good. So t3/t4 tolerate CRLF input on Linux.\n\n  BUT: what about lines where after the last coordinate there's nothing? E.g., single-point files t3_test1 (11 bytes, LF=0) — no newline at all. Fine.\n\n  So actually — **input-data CRLF does NOT break t2/t3/t4 on Linux**, because:\n  - t2: trim_str trims \\r.\n  - t3/t4: isspace('\\r') = true, parse_point skips it.\n  \n  Hmm wait, is isspace('\\r') true? In the C locale, isspace returns true for: space, \\t, \\n, \\v, \\f, \\r. Yes, \\r (carriage return) IS whitespace in C locale. Confirmed [ФАКТ].\n\n  But there's a subtlety: parse_point passes `static_cast<unsigned char>(*ptr_start)` to isspace — good, avoids UB for negative chars (CP1251 Cyrillic bytes are > 127, but those appear in the name not coordinates; coordinates are ASCII digits/dot/space).\n\n  Actually wait — one more case: t2 empty_name test. t2_empty_name = 1 byte. What byte? Probably \\n (empty line). On Linux getline reads \"\\n\" → name = \"\" → empty check triggers → exit 65. Fine either way.\n\n  And t2_input \"звезда\" (with CRLF): is there any case where trim_str is NOT applied? Line 29 trim_str(name) is applied always. Good.\n\nSo my answer to Q2: **No, CRLF in input-data will NOT break on Linux** — and I can prove it from the code: t2 trims \\r via trim_str (\" \\n\\t\\r\" set), t3/t4 skip \\r via isspace in parse_point (isspace('\\r') is true in C locale). \n\nBUT — this deserves the anti-sycophancy check: am I sure? The risk points:\n1. t2: trim_str uses find_first_not_of(\" \\n\\t\\r\") — includes \\r. ✓\n2. t3/t4: parse_point's while loop skips `std::isspace` — \\r is isspace. ✓ And the post-parse check uses isspace too. ✓\n3. BUT: what about `getline` on Windows vs Linux for the \\r? On Windows text mode, \\r\\n → \\n, so no \\r ever seen. On Linux