NOT break anything today (proven from code), but I'd still normalize input_data to LF for uniformity — it removes reliance on \\r-tolerance and matches the \"repo stores LF\" policy. Low priority, hygiene.\n\nNow let me formulate the answers concisely.\n\nFor Q1, the answer structure:\n- The two tools check DIFFERENT contracts. Substring = \"message present in output\" (stderr diagnostics). Equality = \"output is exactly the data\" (stdout data).\n- Don't collapse to equality everywhere: diagnostics tests assert presence, and the expected files are message fragments (sometimes without the values/line numbers that vary). If you forced equality, the expected would have to be the complete exact stderr, which is brittle.\n- However, there IS an inconsistency to fix: t3 test1-3 (stdout data) currently use substring (check_contains) when they should be exact — same class as t1_norm/t4 test1-3. So the plan: equality tool (check_equal) for t1_norm, t3 test1-3, t4 test1-3; keep substring (check_contains) for t2/t3/t4 diagnostic messages.\n- Also note: t3 test1-3 expect files have CRLF → they'd break on Linux with substring too (expected has \\r\\n, output has \\n). So they MUST move to the normalized equality tool anyway.\n\nWait, that's an important point. t3 test1-3 currently use FIND_SUBSTR. On Linux: output = LF, expect = CRLF → substring fails. So t3 test1-3 are ALSO broken on Linux currently, and the fix (equality + normalize) handles them. Let me verify: is that right? The expect t3_test1 has 3 CRLF. The program output on Linux has LF only. check_contains: result.find(expected). expected contains \"\\r\\n\" sequences; result has \"\\n\". So find returns npos → fail. Yes, t3 test1-3 break on Linux today. Unless... the expected substring is looked for, and the \"\\r\\n\" in expected won't match \"\\n\" in result. Correct.\n\nHmm wait — but actually, do the CRLF in expected correspond to line boundaries in the output? The bbox report is multi-line: \"Количество точек: N\\nmin: ...\\n...\" Each line ends \\n. Expected has each line ending \\r\\n. So expected = \"Количество точек: 10\\r\\nmin ...\\r\\n...\" and result = \"... \\n ...\". find fails. Yes broken.\n\nSo this actually strengthens the plan: the CRLF bug affects t3 test1-3 (via substring) just as much as t1/t4 (via fc). All three need the normalized equality comparator.\n\nSo the updated plan:\n1. check_equal (or check_files with --equal mode) — normalizes CR + trailing newlines, exact comparison.\n2. Replace fc AND the t3 test1-3 substring with equality.\n3. Keep substring for diagnostic messages (t2 norm, t3/t4 error cases), because those are presence-assertions. But also normalize CR there? The diagnostic expect files have 0 CRLF (single line, no newline) per the scan — so they currently work on Linux via substring (expected has no newline, output has the message + \\n, find matches). So diagnostic substring tests are fine as-is. Good — that confirms: keep them substring, no change needed.\n\nActually let me double check t2_norm expect: 26 bytes, LF=0, CRLF=0. \"Фигура «звезда»: 6 вершин.\" presumably, no newline. On Linux output = \"Фигура «звезда»: 6 вершин.\\n\" → substring find succeeds. ✓ Works on Linux already. Good.\n\nSo the only stdout-data tests needing the equality tool: t1_norm (fc), t3 test1-3 (substring, broken on Linux), t4 test1-3 (fc). \n\nLet me also reconsider: should we just use the equality tool (normalized) for t3 test1-3 too? Yes — that's the fix.\n\nNow, one more consideration for Q1's deeper question: \"почему не сделать из check_contains check_equal и не сравнивать сами файлы на равенство везде?\" — the honest answer: because check_contains is used for presence-assertions where the expected is a fragment, and the program output legitimately differs (trailing newline, and for some cases the expected omits variable parts). If we made it equality, we'd need the expected files to be byte-exact complete stderr/stdout, and tests would become brittle to incidental changes. Equality is right for data (must be exactly the reference), substring is right for diagnostics (the message must appear).\n\nLet me now write the response. Keep it structured, [ФАКТ]/[МНЕНИЕ] labels, concise but complete. Both questions answered. Also confirm the plan updates.\n\nFormat: this is plan mode, I should present analysis + updated plan and ask for confirmation.\n\nLet me also double-check: does t3_test1 expect actually have CRLF? From scan: t3_test1 bytes=216 LF=3 CRLF=3. Yes. And t3_test2: bytes=216 LF=3 CRLF=3. t3_test3: bytes=223 LF=3 CRLF=3. These are the full bbox reports (3 lines each). So yes.\n\nAlright, write the answer.\n\nOne more: the user asked Q2 \"есть и input-data. там тоже возможны CRLF - это не сломает вход на линуксе?\" Answer: No, today it doesn't break, and here's the code-level proof. But recommend normalizing input to LF anyway for uniform policy. Actually, let me be careful — should I recommend it or not? The anti-sycophancy rules say be critical. The honest engineering view: since it doesn't break (proven), normalizing input is optional hygiene. I'll present it as: today safe (proof), but recommend normalizing for uniformity + robustness against future parser changes. Low priority.\n\nAlso note: the mentor's AGENTS.md § 4.9 п.3 says etalons generated on the fly, but input files are real test data in repo. And the repo encoding policy (CP1251) — CRLF is separate from encoding.\n\nLet me write concise response now.",
