          "text": "The user asks: is switching stdout to binary mode a good idea? Then there won't be \\n -> \\r\\n substitution.\n\nI already addressed this partially in a previous turn (Q about CRT binary mode). Let me give a definitive answer.\n\nI'm in plan mode, read-only. I should answer the question, maybe verify some facts.\n\nLet me think about the tradeoffs of binary stdout:\n\nOption: put `_setmode(_fileno(stdout), _O_BINARY)` in each main() under `#ifdef _WIN32`.\n\nPros:\n- Deterministic output: program writes \\n, file gets \\n on both platforms. Etalons in LF work everywhere.\n- This is a legitimate, documented MSVC idiom for outputting binary/UTF-8 data.\n\nCons:\n1. Spreads `_WIN32` platform code into all 8 mains (t1-t4 C and C++). This violates the isolation principle the project has been following (WCMD in make, _WIN32 in headers). It's test/OS plumbing in the pure logic.\n2. Doesn't fix the trailing-newline asymmetry: t1_test1 etalon ends without newline, program output ends with newline (78 vs 76 bytes). fc tolerated it on Windows; diff would flag on Linux. Binary mode doesn't change that — the program still writes its trailing newline. So even with binary mode, you'd still need etalon normalization or comparison normalization for t1.\n3. Console UX regression: in binary mode on Windows, interactive console output loses \\r\\n → \"staircase\" effect (LF without CR). For a program meant to be run from console, this is a real (if minor) behavioral change.\n4. stdin: binary mode on stdin would break reading CRLF inputs — but you'd only set stdout. However the question: does the input come through same FILE*? No, stdin separate. So only stdout setmode needed. But note: if they only set stdout to binary, then stdin stays text mode (CRLF->LF translation) — fine.\n5. It changes behavior of ALL consumers of the program's stdout, not just tests — if someone pipes to a file in production it's arguably more correct (LF), but it's a behavior change.\n6. Where does _O_BINARY come from? `<fcntl.h>` and `<io.h>` are MSVC-specific. In C there's also `<io.h>`. So need conditional includes.\n\nAlso — the alternative of the compare-utility approach: single point of normalization, no program changes, fixes both CRLF and trailing-newline asymmetry, removes fc/diff entirely.\n\nIs there a hybrid? The user seems attracted to binary mode because it's \"cleaner\" conceptually (program output is canonical). Let me weigh honestly.\n\nActually there's an important nuance: with binary mode, the program's output files become LF on Windows too. Then etalons stored in LF work. But t1_test1 etalon ends WITHOUT trailing newline while program emits trailing newline → diff flags \"No newline at end of file\" difference. So even binary mode alone doesn't fully solve t1_norm; you'd need to also either fix the etalon or normalize. Actually wait — if program output has trailing newline (LF), and etalon has no trailing newline, diff will show difference. So binary mode doesn't remove the need to handle trailing newline mismatch.\n\nSo binary mode solves only the CRLF half, not the trailing-newline half. It adds platform code to 8 programs and changes console UX. The compare utility solves both halves in one place with zero program changes.\n\nThere's another consideration: the \"right\" layered answer. The project's philosophy: programs are pure, platform concerns isolated in build/tooling. Binary mode inverts that.\n\nHowever, I should be balanced: binary mode IS a legitimate technique and some projects do it. But for THIS project, it's worse than the utility approach.\n\nAlso worth noting: there's a subtle point about whether binary mode even fully prevents CRLF in *redirected file* output. `_setmode(_fileno(stdout), _O_BINARY)` — after this, writing \\n via std::print/printf writes just 0x0A. Yes. But the CRT still... Actually one subtlety: when stdout is a console (interactive), in binary mode the console still handles \\n as... it may still 