normalized equality.\n\n- t4_test1/2/3 (17 bytes, no newline) — these use FC (fc/diff) already per the Makefile after her edit. Wait, she said \"в тестах 1-3 таск4 был FIND_SUBSTR хотя там через FC - убираю FIND_SUBST\". Let me verify current state: t4_cpp_test1 now has prereqs `$(T4_CPP_EXE) $(RUN_CASE) tests/expect/t4_test1 tests/input_data/t4_test1` and recipe uses FC. Good.\n\nSo the diagnostic tests using check_contains: t2_norm, t3_empty/not_digit/too_few, t3 test1-3 (data! inconsistency), t4 radius_* and coords error cases.\n\nGiven the user's insight, we could simplify: use one tool with normalized equality for ALL tests. But then the diagnostic tests become strict equality. Is that good? It would catch e.g. if the program prints the message twice or with extra text. That's arguably better. But it's a change in test strictness — tests become more brittle to legitimate output changes. E.g., if a diagnostic message's formatting changes slightly (adds \"ошибка: \" prefix), the test breaks — but it should break, because the message is part of the contract.\n\nHmm, but there's a real concern: the messages contain VALUES that might differ. E.g., t3_not_digit \"Строка 1. Нечисловое данное: 3 2 bam\" — the \"bam\" and \"3 2\" come from the input file. Since input is fixed, expected is fixed. Fine.\n\nBut t4 radius cases: expected files like t4_radius_empty (45 bytes) — the usage message. Fine.\n\nOK so my recommendation: \n1. The user is right that substring was a historical workaround. \n2. But I should be careful: moving everything to equality changes semantics from \"message present\" to \"output exactly equals\". For diagnostic tests, the expected files ARE the full single-line messages, so equality after normalization works and is STRICTER (catches extra stderr content). \n3. However, I'd push back slightly: if she wants the diagnostic tests to verify \"the right message appears\" regardless of extra context, substring has a real (if small) value. The choice is