          "text": "The user asks: in a byte string, is `std::replace(s.begin(), s.end(), \"0x0D\", \"\");` normal/correct?\n\nLet me analyze this. This is a C++ question. The user wants to remove 0x0D (CR) bytes from a string.\n\n`std::replace` signature: `template<class ForwardIt, class T> void replace(ForwardIt first, ForwardIt last, const T& old_value, const T& new_value);`\n\nProblems with `std::replace(s.begin(), s.end(), \"0x0D\", \"\");`:\n1. `\"0x0D\"` is a string literal (const char[5]) — it's the literal text \"0x0D\" (four characters: '0','x','0','D'), NOT the byte value 0x0D. So this compares each char against the string literal, which is wrong. Also T would be deduced as `const char*`, and comparing `char` vs `const char*` won't compile (or will, with issues).\n2. `\"\"` is `const char[1]` — cannot convert to `char` for new_value.\n3. `std::replace` cannot change the length of the string — it replaces element values, it can't erase. To remove CR you need the erase-remove idiom: `s.erase(std::remove(s.begin(), s.end(), '\\r'), s.end());` or in C++20 `std::erase(s, '\\r')` (non-member erase for std::string since C++20).\n\nAlso note: 0x0D is '\\r'. So correct approach:\n\nOption A (C++20): `std::erase(s, '\\r');` — the std::erase overload for std::string. Since they use C++23, this is available.\n\nOption B (erase-remove): `s.erase(std::remove(s.begin(), s.end(), '\\r'), s.end());`\n\nSo the answer: no, that's wrong on multiple counts:\n- `\"0x0D\"` is the literal text, not byte value 0x0D — need `'\\r'` (char literal) or `static_cast<char>(0x0D)`.\n- `\"\"` empty string literal can't be `new_value` of type char.\n- `std::replace` doesn't erase, it only replaces 1:1. To remove, need erase-remove idiom.\n\nLet me be careful and precise. Also since they're on C++23, recommend `std::erase(s, '\\r')`.\n\nI'm in plan mode, read-only. I should answer the question, maybe check if there's existing context in check_contains.cpp. I already read check_contains.cpp. It reads bytes into std::string. So the normalization would be applied to a std::string.\n\nAlso I could mention: they may want to remove trailing '\\n' too. For trailing newlines: could use a loop or std::string::find_last_not_of.\n\nLet me answer concisely but thoroughly. This is a plan-mode Q&A — I answer, no edits.\n\nLet me be precise about `std::replace`:\n- `std::replace(first, last, old_value, new_value)` — replaces each element equal to old_value with new_value. Both old_value and new_value are of the value type (char).\n- `\"0x0D\"` — string literal, array of 5 chars `{'0','x','0','D','\\0'}`. Decays to `const char*`. `*it == \"0x0D\"` compares `char` with `const char*` — compile error (or in some weird deduction). Actually with T deduced... `std::replace` deduces T from old_value and new_value; if old_value is `const char*` and new_value is `\"\"` (`const char[1]`), T = `const char*`. Then the comparison `*first == old_value` is `char == const char*` → compile error. So it won't even compile.\n- Even if it compiled (e.g., using `'\\r'` and `' '`), it wouldn't erase — `std::string` would still have the same length.\n\nSo the correct way:\n```cpp\n// C++20+: std::erase for std::string\nstd::erase(s, '\\r');\n\n// or classic erase-remove\ns.erase(std::remove(s.begin(), s.end(), '\\r'), s.end());\n```\n\nFor trailing newlines:\n```cpp\nwhile (!s.empty() && s.back() == '\\n') s.pop_back();\n// or\ns.erase(s.find_last_not_of('\\n') + 1);\n```\nActually `find_last_not_of('\\n')` — if all chars are '\\n', returns npos, npos+1 == 0, so `s.erase(0)` clears. That works. Or a loop with pop_back.\n\nAlso note the user's `\"0x0D\"` confusion — 0x0D is CR, decimal 13, `'\\r'`. The literal in C++ would be `'\\r'`.\n\nI should also mention: since the plan is to strip CR everywhere and trailing LF, maybe suggest using views or a small normalize function. But keep it focused on their question.\n\nLet me give a clear answer. Also, given AGENTS.md style, mark [ФАКТ/МНЕНИЕ] an